const e={11:["After escorting Ziyu out of the garden, Xu Ziyun lingered a moment to converse with Xiao Cixian before wending his way homeward. His own residence, the vast and imposing ancestral mansion built by his late great-grandfather the Grand Chancellor, lay only a short distance across the road. With his parents, elder brother, and sister-in-law departed from the capital, the sprawling estate housed only Ziyun, his wife, and a retinue of servants. When he recounted the tender devotion between Qin Yan and Ziyu to his wife, Mrs. Yuan, they both marveled at such delicate affection. Smiling, she inquired:",`"These young gentlemen of the stage, what enchanting spell do they cast to so thoroughly captivate you all?" Ziyun answered with a laugh: "You have laid eyes on them and heard their songs. Can you truly claim they lack a certain exquisite charm?" "To my eye," Mrs. Yuan replied, "they merely mimic the delicate graces of sheltered maidens when they perform. Even unadorned, they are certainly fair and fragile. Yet to elevate them as matchless, priceless treasures escapes my understanding. They seem scarcely more refined than my own maidservants." Ziyun reasoned: "From your station, naturally the feminine grace appears supreme. But we cannot bring young ladies of good standing to our banquets and revelries, lest we tarnish the purity of the occasion. The true brilliance of these young actors is this: they possess a woman's visage without a woman's form. They delight the eye while restraining the heart, offering joy untouched by base desire. Is this not a twofold perfection?" Mrs. Yuan laughed: "A well-tailored defense, indeed." "I speak only truth," Ziyun chuckled. "Never in my life have I uttered words I did not sincerely mean." "Perhaps you are so pure of heart," Mrs. Yuan teased, "but can you say the same of your companions?" Ziyun replied earnestly: "If they were not of a similar disposition, we would be as incompatible as fire and ice. Did I not just tell you how Mei Yuxiang erupted in fury, even turning his wrath upon me, merely because Yu Ling uttered a few flirtatious jests? Does that not prove their honor? Moreover, the boys themselves disdain such frivolous teasing." They conversed a while longer until Mrs. Yuan observed that the morrow marked Lady Hua's twentieth birthday, a milestone of great significance, demanding their attendance. "Indeed, we must go," Ziyun agreed. "She honored your birthday last year with her presence and numerous gifts. We are obliged to prepare a selection of fine treasures in return." Thus the peaceful evening drew to a close.`,`At the break of dawn, Mrs. Yuan meticulously selected ten exquisite curios of immense value and drafted an inventory. It listed a jade interlocking ring crafted of jasper and nephrite, a hairpin inlaid with seven treasures, a fan formed of vibrant kingfisher feathers, a coral hair ornament, a filigree gold censer shaped like a mountain, a qin carved from pale jade, a rare block of aquilaria incense, a zither bridge of precious sinking-wood, a rosary of lignum aloes, and an agate seal. These treasures were sent ahead by a servant. From her retinue of twelve maidservants, each named with the character 'Hong' (Red), she chose Hongxue, Hongzhu, Hongxiang, Hongyu, Hongwei, and Hongwen. These six, all fifteen years of age and in the very springtime of their youth, were slender and graceful. They were literate, skilled in calligraphy, and deeply accomplished in both poetry and embroidery. After assisting her mistress with her morning toilet, Hongxue observed: "The air bites bitterly today, and snow seems to linger in the clouds. We must prepare additional garments." From the heavy winter wardrobe she selected a sky-blue satin cloak, richly embroidered with golden motifs upon a lining of purple sable fur. To this she added a red satin skirt adorned with golden celestial steeds, jade pendants that chimed with every step, and a cascade of pearl tassels. A large, crimson padded bundle was also packed with several extra layers, while all floral hairpins and precious ornaments were carefully nestled within a brocaded case. Once the six 'Reds' completed their own preparations, the entire party boarded the fragrant carriage, while the household men mounted their steeds and led the procession forward.`,`The young master Hua was then twenty-one years of age. Though his striking figure had fleetingly caught the eye of Wei Pincai and his temperament had been hinted at by Fu San, his true nature remained untold. Master Hua possessed an extravagant, commanding bearing, yet his heart was uncommonly pure and genuine. He immersed himself freely in the worldly delights of fine delicacies, luxurious dwellings, and the boundless comforts of vast wealth. Pampered and unburdened, he held little affection for tedious social obligations. He proved an exceptional horseman and archer, yet his mastery of poetry and rhapsody was even more profound. Having devoured a thousand volumes, he could effortlessly compose verse within seven paces. His wife, Lady Hua, was born of the maternal Su lineage. Her father, Su Chentai, descended from the glorious founders of the dynasty, held the hereditary title of marquis, and currently reigned as President of the Board of War. Lacking sons, he cherished his two daughters: the elder, Huanxiang, and the younger, Huanlan. Both were endowed with surpassing, celestial beauty. When they strolled languidly amidst the gardens, a hundred butterflies would flutter and dance in their wake. They were masters of poetry, musical theory, calligraphy, and painting, equally adept at the qin and chess. Every talent they touched reached the pinnacle of exquisite refinement. Beyond the high walls of their estate, a popular couplet echoed their fame:

"Better than ten pecks of dragon-palace pearls,
Is but a single glimpse of the Marquis's twin jades."

At eighteen, Huanxiang was wed to Hua Guangsu, forging a union as harmonious as a jade qin paired with a jade zither, as natural as fish swimming in water. Their days were spent in quiet refinement, chanting beneath the moon and gazing at the wind in unutterable leisure. Serving them were ten beautiful maids, each bearing the character 'Zhu' (Pearl) in her name: Baozhu, Mingzhu, Aizhu, Huazhu, Hezhu, Ruizhu, Zhangzhu, Zhenzhu, a second Huazhu, and Zengzhu. All fifteen or sixteen years of age, they bloomed like ten distinct varieties of fresh spring flowers, fluttering like a cluster of powdered butterflies. Each possessed a nimble mind and a fragrant tongue, walking with dainty lotus-steps upon willow waists. Their needlework rivaled the artisans of heaven, and their poetic verses attained marvelous elegance. Compared to the poetry-composing maids of Zheng Kangcheng, they lacked any pedantic stiffness; compared to the household singers of Guo Lingong, they radiated the enchanting grace of a romantic muse. Such exquisite talent and boundless fortune in a young couple was a rarity in the mortal realm. Furthermore, their parents remained vigorous in their twilight years, commanding reverence across the lands, sustaining seven generations of untarnished glory, and standing foremost among the Three Excellencies.`,"The outside world remained entirely ignorant of Lady Hua's birthday festivities. The day prior, the young master and his wife had shared a private family banquet, entertained from dawn till dusk by the Eight-Ages Troupe. Every boy in the troupe bore the character 'ling' (Age) in his name, known as Golden-Age, Jade-Age, Orchid-Age, Cassia-Age, and the like. Some were cultivated by the household's private tutors, while others had been handpicked from renowned traveling companies. Though they could not rival the legendary female-role performers celebrated in the Flower Selection, their artistry easily eclipsed the ordinary stage performers. In quieter hours, they were customarily appointed to serve in the master's study.",`Knowing her younger sister, Miss Huanlan, would attend, Lady Hua was further delighted by the arrival of the Xu household's ten precious curios, signaling Mrs. Yuan's impending visit. Rising early, she and the young master offered reverent respects at the ancestral shrine and the Buddhist altar. The young master had intended to honor his wife with another private banquet, but the arrival of her sister and sworn-sister necessitated his polite withdrawal. Before long, Miss Su's fragrant carriage rolled into the entrance corridor, where a soft sedan chair ferried her directly into the sanctuary of the inner courtyard. Four maids assisted the young lady as Lady Hua emerged to offer a joyous welcome. The sisters exchanged warm, ritual greetings before young Master Hua entered to pay his respects. He had scarcely inquired after the health of his parents-in-law and moved to take his seat when a servant announced: "The mistress of the Xu household has arrived." Master Hua discreetly withdrew as Lady Hua and her sister advanced to the grand hall to receive their honored guest. The palanquin curtain lifted, revealing a heavenly figure surrounded by six beautiful maids. With delicate, tiny lotus-steps, she entered the main hall, clasping Lady Hua's hand as they exchanged deep, smiling bows of profound warmth. Miss Su offered New Year's greetings to Mrs. Yuan, adding, "I had intended to call upon your home tomorrow, as my mother and aunts are planning a visit." Mrs. Yuan replied gracefully, "For the past two days I have longed to invite your honored mother and sisters to sit with us. It would be an absolute delight if you would deign to honor us." Lady Hua interjected, "On the occasion of my humble birthday, you honor me deeply with your gracious presence and shower me with such precious gifts. I receive them with the most profound gratitude." Mrs. Yuan laughed softly, "These trifling tokens merely convey my affection. They are far too slight to warrant mention. I had hoped to visit yesterday, but was unexpectedly detained by sudden matters."`,'Miss Su recounted, "On the eleventh, my mother dispatched a servant to inquire after your health. The messenger returned to report that your sister had hosted a gathering of ladies in her garden to admire the lanterns. He spent half the day spinning tales of the spectacle, speaking of the thousands of men required to arrange them, painting so vivid a scene that I dreamed of wandering your lantern-lit garden that very night, though I beheld not a single one awake."',`She laughed at her own fanciful tale. Mrs. Yuan smiled in return: "You certainly could have viewed the lanterns. We hardly needed thousands of men, though two or three hundred were indeed employed. We ladies hurried ahead on the eleventh to privately test their glow. Thereafter, various gentlemen arrived in constant succession, carrying the merriment unbroken through the eighteenth. Since you reside in the city proper and can only call by day, missing the true nocturnal beauty of the lanterns, we refrained from sending invitations." Lady Hua expressed profound admiration at the description. Turning back to Miss Su, Mrs. Yuan added, "We are deeply grateful that your honored mother kept us in her thoughts and so kindly sent gifts." "When you departed that day," Miss Su replied, "mother noted you seemed somewhat indisposed, and you have lingered in her thoughts ever since." Mrs. Yuan rose once more to express her gratitude. The Ten Pearls and Miss Su's maids then stepped forward to pay their respects to Mrs. Yuan, while the Six Reds mirrored the courtesy to Lady Hua and Miss Su. Following a gentle exchange of domestic pleasantries, Lady Hua proposed summoning the opera troupe. "We sisters are enjoying such a delightful, intimate conversation," Mrs. Yuan demurred gently. "There is no need to let the clamor of performers shatter the peace." They took a leisurely stroll through the elegant chambers before withdrawing into Lady Hua's private sanctuary. Her bedchamber spanned five grand bays: three formed an inner suite, while the outer two served as a refined study, adorned with rows of books and ancient bronzes. Mrs. Yuan admired the exquisite arrangement briefly before the Pearl-named maids entered to announce the banquet was prepared. "Lay it here," Lady Hua directed. The grand table was set, Lady Hua poured the first cup of honor, and they took their seats. Jade-clear wine flowed in abundance alongside a feast of rare mountain delicacies and ocean treasures beyond description.`,'The three ladies shared wine, laughter, and elegant conversation until Mrs. Yuan spoke: "I recently learned a highly entertaining wine-game. It strings together five set phrases, though I find fault with its lack of rhyme, and the final two phrases rely on unnatural filler words. That version draws arbitrarily from classical prose, Tang poetry, domino tiles, aria titles, and the Almanac, making it far too simplistic. I propose a more refined version: using six dice, the player begins based on the pattern thrown. The first phrase must be a domino name, the second a five-character line of Tang poetry, the third a lyric from the Western Chamber, the fourth an aria title, and the fifth a line from the Mao Shi. These five phrases must weave into a single rhyme, ringing out with a pleasing, poetic cadence when spoken." Delighted by the challenge, Miss Su eagerly urged her sister to summon the dice. "It is a clever game, yet quite demanding," Lady Hua observed. "One must remain perfectly natural while holding to the rhyme. Is it not overly taxing?" She signaled a maid to fetch the dice tray, placed the ivory cubes within, and offered it to Mrs. Yuan, saying:',`"Elder sister, pray set the example for us." Gathering the dice, Mrs. Yuan cast them into the tray, landing upon the pattern 'A Flock of Crows Cawing in the Wind.' She gazed at the ivory faces, lost in quiet reflection for a moment, before murmuring, "I shall venture a modest attempt. Pray do not laugh if it falls short." She then recited:

"A flock of crows cawing in the wind,
Clamor of phoenixes descending through the void,
Each gone their own way, the shrike and the swallow, east and west,
At the fifth watch it turns,
I shall share with you a dream's content."`,'Lady Hua and Miss Su offered lavish praise. "The opening phrases are truly excellent, and the final three are utterly exquisite," Lady Hua marveled. "The sentiment breathes such a warm, languorous beauty that it melts the heart to hear it. I fear I could never weave anything quite so lovely."',`Mrs. Yuan laughed gently: "You are forever modest in your claims. Yet when your turn arrives, you summon immense effort, refusing to let anyone surpass you by even a hair's breadth." Lady Hua smiled, took up the dice, and threw the pattern 'Iron Chains Mooring a Lone Boat.' Pausing briefly in thought, she softly recited:`,`"Iron chains moor a lone boat,
The broad river rushes swiftly through the night,
He set our reunion for the ninth day of the ninth month.
Night Journey on the Boat,
Adrift, now sinking, now floating."`,'Mrs. Yuan exclaimed, "What did I tell you? I knew you possessed verses to astonish the ear. These five lines flow as a single breath, far surpassing my own meager attempt. Using a phrase from the Sequel to the Western Chamber shows particular wit. Now we must see what Younger Sister Lan will compose. Surely it will be finer still, proving that the last to speak surpasses the first." Lady Hua merely offered a few graceful, self-effacing disclaimers.',"Impatient and eager, Miss Su abandoned all pretense of modesty, pulling the dice tray eagerly toward herself. With a sharp clatter, she threw the pattern 'A General Hangs His Seal.' Delighted, she narrowed her autumn-water eyes in deep concentration, arranged her thoughts, and recited with a brilliant smile:",`"A general hangs his seal,
Three frontier regions lie in quiet solitude,
All because within you there are a million troops.
Song of Victory,
A pillar of the realm, a duke and a marquis."`,`"I proclaimed the last would surpass the first, and so it has," Mrs. Yuan praised warmly. "Sister Lan's verse leaves me in absolute admiration; I can offer nothing less than a brimming cup in congratulation." Immensely pleased, Miss Su smiled while offering a gentle disclaimer. "It is truly fine," Lady Hua agreed, "though fortune aided you in throwing so auspicious a pattern for your opening." Offering her own cup of congratulation, Lady Hua commanded the attending maids to drink as well, serving as a grand reward to the three armies in honor of the victorious general. As the Ten Pearls and Six Reds partook of the wine, Aizhu tugged Hongxue's sleeve, whispering: "Your mistress's verse of sharing a dream's content overflows with tender emotion. My mistress's vision of the lone boat adrift is imbued with profound philosophical depth. But the Second Young Lady's verse radiates immense martial grandeur. Each possesses its own unique, flawless excellence." Hongxue nodded softly. "You perceive it perfectly." Overhearing this whispered critique, Mrs. Yuan and the ladies could not suppress their knowing smiles.`,`Taking the dice once more, Mrs. Yuan rolled the pattern 'Fallen Red Petals Blanketing the Ground.' Determined to maintain her supremacy and refusing to speak in haste, she pondered deeply before composing a verse that brought her quiet satisfaction. She recited:

"Fallen red petals blanketing the ground,
She wipes away tears and knits her dark brows,
It is only that since last night this waist has grown more slender still.`,`Scolding the Jade Lad,
You shall not return until you are drunk."`,`Miss Su sighed in admiration: "Elder Sister, this is truly magnificent. How do you summon words steeped in such delicate, graceful allusion? My own efforts feel impossibly blunt and coarse by comparison. We must surely congratulate you for this." They each raised a celebratory cup. Mrs. Yuan laughed, "Your verses echo the grand, sweeping style of Li Bai and Du Fu, whilst I linger in the delicate, ornate shadows of Wen Tingyun and Li Shangyin. How can they be compared?" Lady Hua smiled knowingly. "It is absolutely exquisite and perfectly suited to the theme. I suspect Elder Sister is subtly scolding her husband for bringing his young actor companions to the garden each day, returning home so deep in his cups that she pines away, her waist growing ever more slender." A faint blush touched Mrs. Yuan's cheeks as she replied, "Have you come merely to tease me? Tread carefully, for you are waiting on the dice, and I shall surely return the favor." As Lady Hua and Miss Su shared a laugh, the Ten Pearls and Six Reds smiled faintly, thoroughly enchanted by the elegant banter of their mistresses.`,"Lady Hua cast the dice, revealing the pattern 'Two Gentlemen Entering the Peach Blossom Spring.' She spent a quiet moment gathering her thoughts, quickly weaving together several exquisite phrases. Yet, having just teased Mrs. Yuan, she feared a swift retaliation if she voiced them aloud. A sudden hesitation seized her, a rosy blush spreading across her peach-blossom cheeks. Finding it a pity to discard the lovely lines, she reluctantly recited:",`"Two gentlemen enter the Peach Blossom Spring,
The path to Peach Blossom Spring may still be found,
A heaven-ordained new-wedded pair of swallows.
Beside the dressing mirror,
Walking hand in hand."`,'Hearing this, Miss Su turned a knowing, luminous smile toward Lady Hua. Mrs. Yuan laughed softly: "What possessed you to suddenly dream of the days when you were newly wed? These phrases represent the very pinnacle of romantic, entwined devotion. One can almost envision a fragrant-hearted beauty leaning against her dressing mirror, two lovers resting side by side, murmuring softly to one another,','their lips still sweet with the fragrance of rouge. We sit here today as mere guests, making it rather awkward for you to render us so entirely out of place." Lady Hua laughed in surrender:','"I knew you would exact your revenge. That is precisely why I hesitated, hoping to alter a phrase or two, yet I could summon nothing finer. I have only myself to blame for provoking this from you, Elder Sister." The room erupted in harmonious laughter, the maids sharing in the mirth as celebratory cups were poured all around.',`The turn fell once more to Miss Su, who rolled 'Moonrise Above the Plum Branch.' After a brief contemplation, she recited:

"Moonrise above the plum branch,
Fragrant and translucent the flowering tree,
The jade face of the beloved, deep-locked within the embroidered bed-curtains.
Cold Lock on the Casement,
The dew falls thick and heavy."`,`Lady Hua was the first to offer her praise. Mrs. Yuan observed softly, "Your verse achieves the very ultimate in gentle warmth and fragrant allure, capturing precisely the pure voice of a sheltered maiden from a noble house. Being a few years your senior, I find I can no longer summon such delicate, exquisite innocence. It is a grace that truly cannot be forced." Miss Su responded with a serene smile as another round of wine was shared. Beyond the curtain, Hongxiang leaned close to Baozhu, whispering, "Have you noticed? Each lady's verse perfectly mirrors her own voice, her deepest feelings, even her very appearance. If you were to exchange the verses among them, the words would utterly fail to suit the speaker." Baozhu and the other maids smiled faintly in silent agreement. Mrs. Yuan took up the dice again, casting the pattern 'Viewing Lanterns on the Fifteenth Night.'`,`"This is Elder Sister's true domain," Miss Su declared. "Surely you can paint for us the hundreds of birds and beasts, the divine dragons and mottled elephants, the fire-blossomed trees and cascading silver flowers, conjuring the grand, festive spectacle of your lantern festival." Mrs. Yuan laughed: "That was indeed my intention, yet I fear my pen lacks the power to capture it." After struggling fruitlessly to weave the grand imagery together, she set the thought aside and recited instead:

"Viewing lanterns on the fifteenth night,
What need of someone to support me when I am not yet drunk,
Voice after voice saying, better to go home.
Step by Step Graceful,
Beware the heavy dew when you walk."`,`Lady Hua and Miss Su showered the verse with praise. "Elder Sister is so carefree and elegant, her true spirit glowing through her words," Lady Hua marveled. "These lines conjure the very image of you leaning gracefully upon a maid's arm, stepping slowly through the night, viewing the brilliant lanterns of your garden. It fills the heart with an admiring envy." As they shared celebratory cups, Lady Hua instructed Baozhu and the other attendants to retire to the back rooms with the visiting maids to partake of their own feast.`,'With the attendants withdrawn, the room grew intimately quiet. Yet from the back chambers, Mrs. Yuan could discern the intermittent, sharp clatter of dice, mingled with bursts of laughter and the playful barbs of mutual teasing. "They are playing the wine game as well," Miss Su noted. "I wonder what manner of verses they are managing to compose?" Lady Hua laughed softly:',`"Even if they string a phrase or two together, they are unlikely to produce anything of true elegance. They shall surely fall far short of our own efforts." Drawing the dice tray near, she threw the pattern 'Peach-Pink and Willow-Green,' and after a moment's reflection, recited:

"Peach-pink and willow-green,
Flowers and heart alike feel renewed,
Beyond the flowers the beloved is far away, yet the horizon feels near.
Drunk Beneath the Flowers,
Playing the zither, blowing the pipes."`,`Mrs. Yuan smiled warmly. "This, too, captures the entirety of your sentiment and grace. It is as if I behold you resting in the cool shade of the blossoms, surrounded by a lush abundance of red and green, gently dispelling the quiet longings of your heart. You are truly a companion fit for immortals beneath the jade-peach trees." Lady Hua demurred gently: "I feel my lines inevitably fall flat, utterly lacking the electric, dazzling wit of Elder Sister's verse. Today, Elder Sister undeniably stands foremost. A seamless richness of fragrant warmth illuminates your every word, and I can only bow in respectful defeat." "Not at all!" Mrs. Yuan protested. "The true, bright elegance has been entirely claimed by the two of you sisters.`,`"The ancient scholar Xie Lingyun once famously declared: 'Of all the talent in the world, there is but one measure of ten; Cao Zijian alone claimed eight of it.' Today, I would say you two together claim six tenths. Then there is another small talent who would snatch away three tenths, leaving merely one fleeting drop for all the other educated ladies of the world to divide amongst themselves. By the time the cup reaches me, nothing remains." As Lady Hua and Miss Su laughed at her charming exaggeration, Miss Su inquired, "And who is this 'small talent' you speak of?" "Do you truly not know her?" Mrs. Yuan asked in mild surprise. "She is the second daughter of our Senior Kinsman Wang Zhifu, named Wang Qionghua. We are all sworn sisters." "Would that be the Senior Kinsman Wang currently serving as Supervisor of the Court of Transmission?" Lady Hua mused. "We have rarely interacted with that household." "What manner of girl is this Wang Qionghua?" Miss Su pressed. Mrs. Yuan's eyes brightened. "She is seventeen this year, and her beauty is absolute and beyond compare. She and the two of you could truly stand as three peerless equals, forming a perfect tripod of grace. A few days ago, I invited her and her sisters to view the lanterns. Right there at the banquet table, she composed a 'Lantern and Moon Lyrics.' Within moments, she produced a sweeping masterpiece of seven or eight hundred characters, so brilliant and dazzling it astonished both ear and eye. It was as if the immortal Li Bai himself had returned to the mortal realm. It is a work simply impossible for the secluded women's quarters to ordinarily yield."`,`"Does Elder Sister happen to remember the verses of this poem?" Miss Su asked eagerly. "I cannot recall it fully," Mrs. Yuan admitted, "but on another day, I shall transcribe it carefully and send it to you." They drank another cup of wine and sampled the delicate dishes before them. From the back room, the lively commotion of the maids grew louder: a chorus of laughter, teasing jokes, and the clinking of penalty cups. Throwing the dice, Miss Su landed the pattern 'Lattice-Work Eyes' and laughed, "This pattern holds no obvious poetic appeal." After pondering for a moment,`,`she softly recited:

"Lattice-work eyes,
A gentle breeze makes a pleasing sound,
Flutter, flutter, the paper strips are singing.
Hating the long night,
The eastern sky has not yet grown light."`,`Mrs. Yuan smiled in admiration: "You claimed the 'Lattice-Work Eyes' pattern lacked inspiration, yet it has yielded you this delicate, beautiful entry. It is wonderfully natural and unaffected." Having progressed through several rounds, a pleasant stretch of the afternoon had passed. "Let us rest and allow the game to pause for a moment," Lady Hua suggested. "We should partake of the food rather than exhaust our minds for nothing." We shall leave the ladies in the outer room for now and speak of the beautiful maids gathered in the back chambers, deeply engrossed in their own wine game. While some had composed clever entries, others had stumbled awkwardly, and a few, unable to form a rhyme at all, sought desperately for others to compose on their behalf. Though they dared not become overly raucous, they leaned close, whispering urgently into one another's ears, their shoulders brushing and dark tresses tangling as they crowded tightly together. The Twelve Reds of the Xu household and the Ten Pearls of the Hua household were perfectly matched in age, striking beauty, and sharp intellect. In truth, they should have been the dearest of friends, bound by mutual respect and fondness.`,`Yet this was far from reality. Every girl harbored a fierce, competitive spirit, entirely unwilling to yield even an inch of superiority to another. As long as they avoided matters of literary skill or artistic accomplishment, a fragile peace reigned. But the moment poetry, lyrics, or cleverness entered the fray, it inevitably spiraled into a contest of mutual belittlement, each desperately striving to seize the upper hand. Such skirmishes inevitably ended in a bitter war of words, leaving faces flushed crimson on both sides. In this particular round of the wine game, several had already faltered, suffering a relentless barrage of cutting remarks. When Hongwei threw her pattern, her mind went entirely blank. For a long, agonizing moment, her cheeks burned a furious crimson with frustration. Suddenly inspired, Aizhu laughed and said, "I shall compose a verse for you, but you must thank me properly as your matchmaker!" Still smiling her sweet, cutting smile, she pointed a delicate finger at Hongwei and recited:

"Intoxicated Xi Shi,
The flush of wine and beauty come on slow,
Last night in the clear moon and gentle breeze, deep in the night.
Good Elder Sister,
The gentleman of quality tempts her with his wooing."`,`The room erupted in praise for the clever verse. Hongwei retorted sharply, "You are indeed a 'good elder sister', small wonder there are those longing to tempt you!" Aizhu replied smoothly, "I was merely describing you and your striking looks. Do you not agree you perfectly resemble an intoxicated Xi Shi?" The maids dissolved into laughter once more.`,"Ruizhu threw the dice, landing the pattern 'A Loach Slipping into a Water-Chestnut Nest.' Disgusted by the unrefined name, she declared it invalid and demanded a redraw, but the others vehemently refused to allow it. Forced to comply, Ruizhu thought desperately, yet try as she might, she could not string the awkward phrases into any connected, poetic sense. Seeing her struggle, Hongxue laughed aloud:",`"I shall compose for you as well, but you too must thank me as the matchmaker." Ruizhu eyed her suspiciously. "If the verse is decent, speak it. But if it is merely an insult, spare me the favor." Hongxue smiled mischievously. "I would not insult you. In truth, you shall be deeply grateful to me." Urged on by the eager crowd, Hongxue laughed and recited:

"A loach slipping into a water-chestnut nest,
A green-feathered bird sporting on the orchid sedge,
A marquis's gate, the old monk is not permitted to knock.
That bald little fellow,
Shall grow old together with you."`,`With a shriek, Ruizhu lunged forward and pinched Hongxue's mouth tightly shut. As Hongxue struggled wildly to pry the fingers from her lips, the entire room doubled over in helpless laughter. Mingzhu laughed so violently that her flailing sleeve knocked a wine cup entirely off the table. The raucous merriment drifted clearly to the ladies in the outer room, prompting Mrs. Yuan to laugh, "They are proving vastly more entertaining than we are." Meanwhile, Hongyu threw the pattern 'Eight Refusals' and immediately groaned, "This pattern is far too difficult. It cannot be assembled; allow me to throw again." Baozhu interjected, "Of course it can be assembled. I shall weave it for you, and I guarantee it will not leave you suffering 'eight refusals.'" Hongyu warned her, "Are you speaking in jest or earnestness? If you intend to be cruel, I swear I shall tear your mouth open." "I take no pleasure in cruelty," Baozhu replied innocently. Pointing directly at Hongyu, she recited:

"Eight refusals,
Startled from her dreams, the mandarin-ducks rise,
What sustains her through this long, long night?
Taking Off the Cloth Shirt,
The heart's longing, center-deep."

She paused, adding with a wicked glint, "The character for 'longing' here must, of course, be read as 'itching.'" Hongyu cursed her roundly. "It seems there is a terrible itch in your mouth as well. Allow me to scratch it for you!" Snatching up a slippery piece of sea cucumber with her chopsticks, she thrust it forcefully toward Baozhu's lips. Baozhu struck the chopsticks away with a swift backhand; they clattered loudly to the floor beneath the table, where a white cat instantly darted from the shadows to devour the fallen delicacy. The maids once again dissolved into tears of irrepressible laughter.`,`Zhangzhu rolled the pattern 'Climbing the Ladder to Gaze at the Moon,' offering a verse that was merely passable, devoid of any true brilliance. Hongwen immediately criticized her: "The name of this pattern is exquisitely poetic, yet presenting it so poorly is a grave injustice to the subject. Let me speak it properly for you." She then recited:

"Climbing the ladder to gaze at the moon,
Song Yu is in the western neighborhood,
Over the wall they exchanged poems and compositions until the sky grew light.
Stirring of the Flower's Heart,
A young woman is moved by longing."

Zhangzhu laughed bitterly, scolding Hongwen: "What a disgraceful way for a proper girl to behave! Climbing a ladder to peep at a man, and then boldly declaring your own heart is stirred! Have you no shame?" Hongwen laughed brightly in return: "I am speaking of your heart, for you suppress it all so deeply inside. It is far better to speak your desires plainly than to sicken yourself holding them back." Furious, Zhangzhu shoved Hongwen hard. Caught entirely off guard, Hongwen stumbled backward, crashing heavily into Baozhu and landing squarely upon her tightly bound feet. Baozhu winced in pain, placing one hand on Hongwen's shoulder for balance while frantically rubbing the tip of her embroidered shoe with the other. After soothing the ache for a moment, she delivered two sharp, punitive smacks to Hongwen's back, sending the room into another fit of laughter.`,`Hongxiang threw the pattern 'A Pair of Matched Butterflies, Precisely,' but found herself utterly incapable of assembling the verse. The few phrases she conjured stubbornly refused to rhyme. Hezhu offered sweetly, "I shall compose a beautiful verse for you, one that will ensure you never ignore me again." Assuming the offer was born of genuine kindness, Hongxiang replied, "Good elder sister, please compose it for me then." Hezhu laughed, "Though I compose the words, the entry remains entirely yours." She then recited:

"A pair of matched butterflies, precisely,
When will the wish be granted,
Every moment endured stretches like a summer's day.
Parallel Lotus Blossoms,
May it be so, morning and night."

Realizing the mockery, Hongxiang's face flooded with crimson fury. She lunged to tear Hezhu's mouth open, but the others swiftly intervened, hauling her back. It was then Hezhu's turn; she threw the pattern 'A Single Flower.' As she began to quietly arrange her poetic phrases, Hongzhu turned toward her with a brilliant, predatory smile. "Let me compose for you." Suspecting nothing but malice, Hezhu shook her head fiercely. "No, thank you." "Even if you do not want it, I am going to say it regardless," Hongzhu insisted. Hungry for more amusement, the entire room clamored for her to speak. Hongzhu recited loudly:

"A single flower,
We still cherish the time of the embrace,
This is what is called 'the talented youth and the beautiful maiden, indeed.'
A Single Point of Red,
Lightly soiling my private heart."

The room exploded into utterly irrepressible laughter. Beside herself with rage, Hezhu marched over, seized Hongzhu fiercely around the waist, and with surprising strength, slammed her down onto the heated bed-platform. Pinning the struggling girl, Hezhu demanded furiously, "Now, let me ask you in absolute earnest about this 'single point of red!'" Being the weaker of the two, Hongzhu could not twist free, her silken skirt parting wildly to both sides. The room watched in hysterics as her two tiny, bound feet flailed and kicked desperately in all directions, the maids laughing until they clutched their sides, barely able to remain upright.`,'Hongxue and Hongxiang finally rushed over, prying the furious Hezhu away. Sitting up, Hongzhu discovered the delicate flowers had been torn from her hair, her elegant tresses left a disheveled ruin. She spent a long moment smoothing her hair back into place before fiercely scolding Hezhu: "What brutish, reckless play! Speaking a jest is one thing, but you had no right to take it to such violent extremes." The chaotic commotion had now reached a pitch that Lady Hua and Mrs. Yuan could no longer ignore. They dispatched a senior married maidservant to investigate and strictly forbid any further rough play. The cluster of maids fell instantly into a terrified silence, hurriedly composing themselves and finishing their bowls of rice before marching back out to resume their attendance. The ladies observed this troupe of unruly girls: some with floral hairpins knocked askew, some with hopelessly disheveled hair, others turning their backs in a desperate bid to suppress lingering giggles, and a few with brows knit in silent, aggrieved reproach. Despite themselves, the ladies found the entire spectacle deeply amusing. Turning to Aizhu and the others, they scolded gently, "At your ages, how can you still behave with such unbridled mischief? Were it not for our honored guest being present today, I would certainly see you punished." Mrs. Yuan added her own words of mild censure to the Six Reds. The maids bowed their heads deeply, standing in contrite silence. Unable to contain her curiosity, Miss Su asked, "What manner of verses were you playing at in there? What on earth was so terribly funny?" At this, several of the maids failed to hold back their smiles, which in turn brought both ladies to the very brink of laughter themselves.','Lady Hua laughed softly: "These foolish girls, they are at once maddening and entirely amusing." Miss Su pressed further: "If any of your entries were truly clever, you may as well share them with us, for we would love to hear and appreciate them. If they are genuinely fine, I shall even bestow a reward upon you. And I promise your mistresses will not scold you for them."',`Aizhu's bright eyes suggested she was eager to speak, but Hongxiang sharply tugged her sleeve, warning her to remain silent. Aizhu demurred, "There were a great many verses, I cannot recall them all clearly." Eager to hear the tale, Miss Su turned to Lady Hua and Mrs. Yuan: "They are terrified of their mistresses and dare not speak. But if you command them to recite, they surely will." Knowing full well her maids possessed razor-sharp wits and had likely composed highly entertaining verses, Lady Hua exchanged an amused glance with Mrs. Yuan. Being of a lively, unconventional spirit, Mrs. Yuan was secretly eager to display her own maids' cleverness. "If what you composed is coherent, you may go ahead and speak it," she commanded. "But if you recite something incoherent, each of you shall kneel and drink a massive penalty cup." Knowing their own memories were flawless and that their personal verses were free of offense, Hongwei and Mingzhu stepped forward. "They are coherent enough," they admitted cautiously, "but we fear that by speaking them, instead of earning a reward, we shall only invite punishment." Lady Hua laughed, "Go on, recite them one by one." Thus, Mingzhu recounted the three offensive entries composed by Aizhu, Baozhu, and Hezhu, while Hongwei recited the three scandalous verses authored by Hongxue, Hongwen, and Hongzhu. The effect was absolute: both ladies laughed so helplessly that the heavy pearl ornaments in their hair quivered and swayed wildly. Though they desperately attempted to compose their features into masks of stern reprimand, they completely failed. While Miss Su felt the verses were undeniably too free and scandalously familiar, she deeply admired the nimble sharpness of mind required to compose them. Refraining from laughing outright, she offered a subtle, appreciative nod.`,'After laughing together for a good while, the two ladies summoned the six culprits forward: the three Reds and the three Pearls who had authored the scandalous verses. Suppressing their lingering amusement, they scolded: "You are impossibly impertinent! How could you utter such things? If word of this escaped these walls, what face would you have left to show the world? Now kneel, every one of you!" Blushing furiously with deep shame, the six maids had no choice but to kneel before the gathered company. Miss Su quickly interceded on their behalf: "Two dear sisters, for my sake, I beg you to forgive them this first offense. Though their words were highly improper and excessively free, we must grant them credit for their lightning-quick wits and nimble tongues. Let their poetic merit cancel their fault." Mrs. Yuan sighed in concession, "Since Second Sister has spoken for them, I dare not refuse her request. Yet we must still admonish them; if we do not, they will grow accustomed to such license and become completely unbridled." She and Lady Hua then passed judgment upon the six entries: for the most egregious offenses, the penalty was a massive cup of wine to be drained in a single draft, accompanied by three sharp slaps of the palm as token chastisement. For the lesser offenses, a simple forfeit cup of wine was deemed sufficient. Thus, Hongxue, Hongzhu, Hezhu, and Baozhu suffered both the wine and the stinging slaps, while Aizhu and Hongwen escaped with only the wine forfeit. Receiving their punishments in utter humiliation, and having thrown back the harsh wine in such haste, the maids found themselves swaying unsteadily on their feet. They fought to hold themselves upright, privately, bitterly regretting their fleeting moment of high spirits.',`Noticing the shadows lengthening as the day grew late, Mrs. Yuan realized the banquet must draw to a close. She smiled and turned to Lady Hua: "Throw the dice one final time and weave a few well-turned phrases to conclude our game. Let it be something pure and beautiful to cleanse the air after all this foolishness." She softly instructed the maids to serve the rice. Seeing the hour was indeed ripe and wishing not to overstay, Lady Hua ordered the final dishes brought forth. Picking up the ivory dice, she cast them, producing the highly auspicious pattern 'Golden Chrysanthemums Facing Lotus Blossoms.' Privately thrilled by the beautiful omen, she retreated into quiet thought before reciting her final verse:

"Golden chrysanthemums face the lotus blossoms,
Patterned candles curl as the plates gleam red,
And yet you ask me, blue-sleeved, to hold the jade cup with tender care.
Perfectly at Peace and Happy,
May ten thousand blessings converge upon you."`,"Mrs. Yuan and Miss Su praised the verse endlessly. Lady Hua warmly urged them both to drink two final cups, after which the rice was served and the magnificent meal gracefully concluded. Following the washing and rinsing of hands, Mrs. Yuan noted the evening sun slipping low on the horizon and knew she could delay no longer. She offered her deepest thanks and bid a fond farewell. Accompanied by the Ten Pearls and the entire cluster of maids, Lady Hua and Miss Su escorted her to the palanquin. The Six Reds carefully supported the sides of the chair, taking small, measured steps all the way to the outer corridor before Mrs. Yuan mounted the carriage, which then flowed smoothly away into the dusk.","As for Miss Su, she did not return to her own estate until the second watch of the deep night. To learn what transpired next, you must hear the tale told in the following chapter."]},l=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations11:e},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),a={12:["After Mrs. Yuan returned from the Hua household that evening, she arrived home late, changed her clothes and removed her ornaments, and then told Ziyun all about the wine-game they had played, including the maids' entries. Ziyun praised it all enthusiastically.",`Later, without telling his wife, he had all sixteen entries engraved and divided into two grades: the ten entries by the ladies and the young miss formed the Upper Game, and the six entries by the maids formed the Lower Game. He composed titles and wrote a good number of poems for them, though without recording any names. Because the first entry had been "a flock of crows cawing in the wind," and the maids' ruckus had followed afterward, he named the whole collection the Flock-of-Crows-Cawing-in-the-Wind Game. When people outside the household saw it, they passed it around as a delightful story.`,"By the time Mrs. Yuan found out, it had already spread everywhere, and there was nothing to be done about it.","Time passed swiftly, and before one knew it mid-spring had arrived. There is now one particular person's affairs that must be told — a person of great importance in this book. Who is he? In a previous chapter, Xiao Cixian mentioned two lantern riddles in the form of love letters, which had been solved by someone whose name he had unfortunately failed to ask. This person's surname was Tian, his given name Chunhang, his style Xiangfan, and he was twenty-three years old. Also a native of Jinling, he was residing in Yangzhou. He had lost his father while still young. His mother, née Zhang, came from a distinguished family of long standing and was well versed in the classics and histories. She bore Chunhang at the age of twenty-five; when Chunhang was three years old, his father Tian Hao passed the jinshi examination and promptly died in the capital. Lady Tian kept her chaste widowhood and raised her son alone, with the support of her elder brother, Prefect Zhang Tongsun, who helped them from time to time. Chunhang's learning owed much of its foundation to his mother's teaching. As a child he had a reputation for extraordinary gifts; as he grew, people marveled at his exceptional qualities. At thirteen he entered the district school; at eighteen he passed the supplementary examination.","He had a distinguished and imposing appearance — luminous as a jade mountain, clear as autumn water. His disposition was graceful and cultivated; his bearing was outstanding and free. In the school, people said he stood among the crowd like a crane among chickens. But his luck had not yet come — he failed three times in the provincial examinations. He had taken a wife from the Yan family — virtuous and comely, daughter of a district school instructor — and they were harmonious together, like qin and zither in tune.","In the first month of the previous year, Lady Tian, seeing that her son was trapped in the countryside with no prospects, concluded this could not be a long-term plan. Moreover, a good number of his father's fellow jinshi from the same examination year had by now risen to prominent positions. So she directed Chunhang to go to the capital to pursue his studies, with the old family servant Tian An accompanying him. Departing from home, he first went to Hangzhou, then to Suzhou, where old friends and current acquaintances, as well as several influential officials, all lent their support. Chunhang lingered in those two places for half a year; his poems and writings were copied and circulated almost everywhere. People of the day said he could rival the Hanlin compiler Hou and the district magistrate Qu in literary fame, and as a result his purse filled up nicely and his luggage box was full of gifts. Needless to say, there was also visiting the pleasure quarters and carousing with wine, calling on singing-girls and sleeping in their fragrant rooms, until he was nearly too happy to think of leaving. When he received his mother's letter urging him to proceed promptly to the capital, Chunhang was obliged to choose a departure date. He first sent a thousand taels home, then engaged two dashing servants, outfitted himself in furs and fine horses, was seen off by singing-girls and escorted by pleasure-seeking friends. The whole journey was wind and flowers, poetry and wine, roaming with eyes and heart wide open — thoroughly enjoyable.","He took a roundabout route, gazed east upon Mount Tai, paid his respects west at Mount Hua, and did not arrive in the capital until the end of the eleventh month. He took lodgings at Hongji Temple in the southern part of the city, living in the courtyard just adjacent to Gao Pin's. All the fellow provincials and old acquaintances he had yet to visit could wait; alone, he spent his days at wine houses and opera theaters, finding pleasure and solace. Fortunately, the singing-girls here were by no means attractive — they wore their trouser legs tied tight, stuffed their hair full of paper flowers, puffed out their chests with bloated, overfed airs, powdered faces and greasy hair; they ate garlic and onions, drank raw spirits, lay on heated beds hot as the Goddess of Wushan's mountain terrace, and practiced their secret arts like exhausted hunters after a day's chase — so that Chunhang's appetite for women was thoroughly extinguished. Seeing the young gentleman-actors, who in their way resembled those of the south, he devoted himself wholeheartedly to watching opera. And he did not care for kunqu, but loved the clapper-style bangzi operas, and so was captivated by several rather undistinguished performers. Chunhang's capacity for feeling was truly like scattered coins all over the ground — utterly without a central thread. Moreover he was soft-hearted and warm-faced: if someone treated him well, he would treasure that person like a priceless jewel and could absolutely not bring himself to part. In the several months since coming to the capital, he had not accomplished a single serious thing — every day he took several performers along, and beyond eating and drinking, he was also buying them clothes, gifts, and contributing to various expenses.","What little money Chunhang had could not possibly withstand such wholesale dissipation; by the time the new year passed, it was entirely spent. Afterward he began to pawn clothes one after another; as the clothes dwindled, some of the performers began to sense his circumstances and gradually grew distant. Chunhang, being a man of broad and easy temperament, was entirely untroubled by this.",'Though Tian An repeatedly remonstrated with him, Chunhang paid no attention and continued finding pleasure in hardship just the same. He had a prior philosophical conviction of his own: "When funds are exhausted, friendships dissolve" — if he could enjoy himself one more day, he would count that one day gained; only when he had truly reached the end of all resources would he stop. By this point, Gao Pin and Chunhang had already become acquainted.',"They gathered together morning and evening and were on the most intimate terms. Gao Pin would often weave words of gentle remonstrance into his banter. Chunhang would mouth his agreement while inwardly dissenting, and would even try to drag Gao Pin out with him; Gao Pin went along a few times. Gao Pin was currently a supplementary seventh-rank minor official at the Board of Punishments; keeping up carriages, fine clothes, and social obligations was already a strain, so he could not manage things the way Chunhang did. Moreover, he was not fond of the performers Chunhang admired, saying that the crowd Chunhang loved were not remarkable, and Chunhang disagreed. But then, when Li Yulin came to visit Gao Pin, and Chunhang saw that particular graceful, tender, elegant beauty — which seemed somewhat finer than Rong Guan and the others — he spent a few days privately revising his own opinion.","One day Gao Pin came over and happened to find Chunhang at his meal — half a dish of plain vegetables and a bowl of white rice.",'An old manservant and a young boy stood in attendance on either side. The dashing senior manservant had long since departed. Chunhang talked and laughed easily, entirely at his ease. Gao Pin said: "You treat yourself so sparingly, yet you treat others with such lavish generosity — I must say you fall far short of the little players in this." Chunhang, hearing this suddenly, thought Gao Pin was mocking him; yet knowing that mockery was habitual with him, he let it pass and asked: "How do you arrive at that conclusion?" Gao Pin said: "Look at your current lodgings and meals — in which respect do you match the little players? How is it that you have such warmth for others and yet none for yourself? Besides, I know your circumstances well enough — you are no better off than I, Gao Zhuoran, in this regard. Even granting that generosity is your natural character and lavishing money is your habit, you need not pour good gold into a bottomless cesspit. Let me ask: did you come to the capital to seek a career, or to make merry with young players? Buying flowers and plying wine may be occasional pleasures; but to become as reckless as you, spending yourself to the bone — I fear you may meet a bitter end before you ever drink the pledging-cup in triumph, and suffer a fate you cannot undo while the plum blossoms still bloom. This is what I cannot fathom."',`Chunhang gave a wordless laugh and said: "I used to take you for a man of true understanding. Having heard you now, I see you have not yet attained it. You have read twenty years of books and still do not understand even the two characters 'nature' and 'principle' — yet you come to lecture me on right and wrong. Let me explain it for you." Gao Pin said: "Please do enlighten me." Chunhang said: "True and without falsehood — that is sincerity. From sincerity one reaches clarity — that is human nature. If there is one part falseness, one part emptiness, then sincerity is forfeit." Gao Pin said: "Granted. But surely you are not saying that true sincerity means making merry with young players?"`,`Chunhang said: "Ranging across ten thousand li in every direction, up and down five thousand years of history — where is there anything finer than these young players? Anyone who does not love them is not worth counting. If there were one part falseness in one's love for a young player, that person would be false through and through — and if not genuine here, where would one use one's genuine self? If there were one part emptiness in one's love for a young player, that person would be empty through and through — and if not true here, where would one use one's true self? Furthermore: nature is principle, and principle is Heaven. If one cannot be at peace with one's nature, where does one seek principle? Without principle, where does one speak of Heaven? The Creator expended great effort in bringing these young players into being — surely the Creator has been most generous toward them. Since the Creator spares no labor, arranging for each one a face like this, eyes and brows like this, skin and body like this, a coquettish smile and artful pout, delicate and alluring — if the Creator did not intend for people to love them, why not place them in some primordial wilderness, some vast and empty waste, cut off from the world, left for wind and smoke to erode, so that the world would never know that such beauties exist — would that not have saved a great deal of trouble? Since they are not permitted to live out their lives in quiet obscurity, but must instead be gathered in the capital, the center of power and distinction, clearly the Creator's intent is that eminent gentlemen and men of fame throughout the realm should appraise and appreciate them — lest this devoted effort of the Creator's go to waste. Consider seasonal flowers and beautiful women, the bright moon and gossamer clouds, rare books and renowned gardens — all manner of supremely beautiful things, which everyone loves, yet which can seldom be gathered in one place; to obtain even one of them is already a source of deep satisfaction. But a young player is like a seasonal flower, yet not a mere plant; like a fine jade, yet requiring no artifice; like the bright moon and gossamer clouds, yet close enough to hold and enjoy; like a rare book or a famous painting, yet capable of speech and conversation; like the most exquisitely beautiful of all wonderful things, yet with a thousand charms and a hundred graces shifting and varying endlessly. To lose a young player and gain all the beautiful things of past and present would not be worth calling remarkable; to gain a young player and lose all the beautiful things of past and present need cause no regret. Mencius says: 'When young, one pines for father and mother; when one discovers beauty, one pines for lovely maidens; when one holds office, one pines for the ruler.' We of the humble scholar class have no occasion to offer hymns to sage rulers and worthy ministers; and living far from home, the rites of morning and evening attendance on parents are forgone. Of the three pining loves, only the two characters 'lovely maidens' — which the sages decreed thousands of years ago as our object of longing — remain. And surely even the sages must have been there themselves at some point; otherwise, how could they have said it so precisely? What I most fail to understand is why people today consider love for women ordinary and love for men extraordinary — in the end it is just love of beauty, so why draw a distinction between male and female? To love women but not men means ultimately one loves lechery, not beauty. If a man is devoted to lechery, then beauty is not his real concern; whereas if he is truly devoted to beauty, he will not dare indulge in lechery. And what I most fail to understand of all is placing equal weight on wealth and beauty: loving another's beauty while begrudging one's own money — exchanging rotten, stinking dung for a rare and fragrant treasure flower, which is heavier and which lighter? Zhuoran should be able to judge."`,`Gao Pin, having heard this speech, had no ready answer. He then said: "The deepest feeling belongs to people like us — am I someone who does not understand human sentiment? My reason for advising you is that your bedside gold is entirely gone, and I have no purse I can offer. Even if you could one day be ennobled as a duke of Xingyang, I fear that among this crowd there would be no one willing to play the Guo Guifuren to your salvation. The young man in the dark kerchief may enjoy a fine name in the wine houses for now — but the tattered beggar in rags may suddenly find himself starving in the flower streets. I privately fear you may end up like Zheng Yuanhe, mocked by history." Chunhang laughed: "Would a true man be spoken of in the same breath as a miser who hoards money? What I gained by myself, I lose by myself — what regret is there in that?" The two were deep in their lively exchange when a servant of Gao Pin's came to say: "Young Master Yan has come to call on you, sir." Gao Pin went out to his own room and found Zhongqing seated there. Asking where he had been for so many days, Zhongqing replied: "After viewing the lanterns on the festival night, I caught a chill and fell ill for several days — I have not left home for more than half a month and was growing restless indoors." He then spoke of Nan Xiang's drunken state on the lantern festival evening. Gao Pin laughed: "I was there that day too and was quite thoroughly drunk myself. I managed to slip away at an opportune moment — otherwise I too would have been caught in the crossfire. Why on earth would anyone try to pick a quarrel with a man sodden with drink?" They then fell to talking about the lanterns at the Yiyuan garden and the lantern riddles. Gao Pin said: "There were two fine riddles — two love letters: one made up of flower names, one of medicinal herb names. Both were solved by someone staying in this same temple — a fellow named Tian Xiangfan. Truly inspired." Zhongqing, hearing the name Xiangfan, thought of the young man who had written a lyric in the wine house while snow was falling the previous year — the signature had been Xiangfan — and so he asked Gao Pin:`,`"What sort of person is this Xiangfan?" Gao Pin said: "Also one of our kind. I mentioned him to you last year — exquisite in every way, a truly refined specimen. Nowadays he is exquisite in a different sense — having polished himself clean of everything." Zhongqing smiled and asked: "How so?" Gao Pin then recounted his arguments just now and everything Chunhang had done since arriving in the capital, one by one. He added: "This man is truly a pity — talented and handsome together, broad in temperament and spirit, but loves making merry and has no restraint at all. He is also a native of your Jinling, though now residing in Yangzhou. He says his wife's family is named Yan — perhaps a kinsman of yours? Why not meet him?"`,'Zhongqing said: "A fine idea. Go in and introduce me." Gao Pin immediately brought Zhongqing inside. Zhongqing caught sight first of a young man — luminous as jade, radiant as a pearl — was this not the very one he had seen in the wine house the previous year? Gao Pin introduced them. Chunhang, seeing Zhongqing, also felt he looked familiar.',`Zhongqing said that ever since seeing the lyric in the wine house last year he had been full of admiration, and truly regretted that they had not met sooner. Chunhang also recalled that encounter, and they exchanged words of mutual esteem. Zhongqing inquired in detail about his family background, and learned that Chunhang's father-in-law was indeed his own relative by a collateral line — Zhongqing had simply been so long in the capital that he had lost track of this branch of the family. The two found their temperaments perfectly matched; being also kinsmen, they were thoroughly congenial, and before long the conversation turned to the most intimate confidences. Zhongqing noticed that Chunhang's dress, which had been so elegantly fine the previous year, was now quite unlike what it had been; recalling Gao Pin's words that he had polished himself clean and had nothing left, he was also curious about the caliber of people he had been so lavish with. He asked: "I hear you have a considerable fondness for young actors — but which ones have you been admiring? Do they live up to the distinction you bestow?" Gao Pin cut in: "His way of admiring is quite unlike other people's — let me tell it for you:" and then he recited:

"Clack-clack-clatter, the bangzi beat;
Eeee-aaah, the two-string fiddle sings.
Trouser-legs white as autumn cloud, thin;
Climb Shamans' Mount and the farts smell sweet."`,`Zhongqing burst out laughing. Chunhang's face went red and he said: "Rubbish! Your farts, for their part, have a certain fragrance about them. It's just a pity you've buried Bai Juyi's fine line inside your three stinking verses." Zhongqing laughed: "In all seriousness — who exactly have you been admiring, elder brother?" Chunhang said: "I have by no means surveyed all the celebrated beauties in all the troupes — they vary in quality, I imagine. I have simply been going along with whatever presented itself — as they say, unable to escape convention, just making do. Among the main troupes, I would put Dengchun's Yumei and Quanfu's Cuibao at the top; and Lianzhu's Rong Guan is also passable. I suppose these are the ones that meet with general appreciation." Zhongqing gave a small smile and said: "Lord Ye was fond of dragons — yet when a real dragon appeared, he took fright. The man of Zheng dreamed of a deer — yet in the end it was only a dream-deer. Xiangfan has the reputation of being fond of young actors, yet the reality falls short of the reputation. The finest young actors in the world are in the capital, and the finest in the capital are assembled in the Lianjin troupe. Shi Zhujun's Qutai Huaxuan ranks them most fairly: Yuan Baozhu, Su Huifang, and such as these alone may be called celebrated flowers — and moreover they are accomplished in poetry, lyrics, calligraphy, and painting without exception, truly capable of being our good companions. As for Cuibao, Yumei, and the like, they are nothing more than foxy charmers courting patronage, narrow-eyed creatures given to jealousy, who regard money as their very lives and use clothes to cultivate friendships, welcoming the new today and casting off the old tomorrow. How can Xiangfan be so reckless in his affections?" Chunhang sat in silence for a long while.`,`Chunhang said: "What is there to be wrong or right about? It was a passing fancy. Besides, different people have different taste. When it comes to appraising these matters, one must develop one's own discernment and not simply echo others. This crowd need not be expected to affect the manners of the cultivated class — if they try too hard to cultivate refinement, it will seem contrived, and they are better off with the natural ways of young actors, which is where true, unaffected vitality lies. If I were to achieve my ambitions, I would not begrudge ten thousand taels of gold to build a golden chamber of several storeys, with light-robed, long-sleeved attendants in front and powdered and rouged beauties behind, to wait upon me for a few years — and then I would match all these good men and faithful women off in pairs, like jade zithers and jade lutes, fulfilling the grand wish of every heart under heaven, creating a world of joy; and then I, like Maitreya, would smile a smile that never closed — how happy would that be?" Gao Pin said: "For your golden chamber, I shall certainly compose you a couplet." He then recited:

"In the bright moon of the jade isle, three thousand li wide;
The person stands at the very summit of the Penglai peak."

Chunhang said: "That couplet doesn't quite fit the subject." Gao Pin said: "It fits perfectly — the upper line fits your powdered and rouged beauties in the rear; the lower line fits your long-sleeved, light-robed attendants in front." Zhongqing and Chunhang both puzzled over this. Gao Pin said: "With this couplet up on the wall, everyone will know that this golden chamber of yours has a tent erected out front and a kiln opened in the back." Zhongqing burst out laughing. Chunhang said: "Put away that sharp tongue of yours." The three talked and laughed for half a day; then Zhongqing returned home and told Wang Xun that Chunhang was a kinsman of his, and also the very young man who had written the lyric in the wine house last year — truly possessed of both talent and looks, but with ambitions too lavish and pleasures that had carried him off. A few days later he went to call on Chunhang again, but made several successive visits without finding him in. Chunhang had by now fallen into complete disarray. Zhongqing, pitying his talent and wishing to help him, heard that he was in a hopeless predicament and scraped together two hundred taels of silver, which he sent with a letter saying he had heard that Chunhang's journey was going poorly and was offering a small contribution toward the cost of his pleasures — it was in truth a test of his character. Chunhang was overjoyed and wrote back to thank him, then proceeded to enjoy himself for another ten or more days, and was once again empty-handed. The things he had recently redeemed from pawn were in pawn again. Zhongqing heard of this and sighed deeply.`,"One day Chunhang was again at the theater watching the opera — this time the Lianzhu troupe. He sat alone in cold isolation at a spot behind the exit door, in shadow. Watching Rong Guan's performance, he felt his heart stirring with pleasure again. Just as he was absorbed in enjoyment, he noticed a table in front of him where a plump man of about thirty sat down, wearing a fur-lined coat inside out, accompanied by a clever-looking young man of about twenty; behind them stood a manservant. The plump man spoke in pure Beijing speech; the other was from the south. These were in fact Fu San and Wei Pincai. Before long, Rong Guan had removed his stage makeup and was sitting in the gallery upstairs talking with a young man. He came down again and sat for a moment in the lower area, then walked over toward this side, greeting and acknowledging people along the way.",`Suddenly catching sight of those two at the table in front, he quickened his steps and greeted them, then sat down in the middle. Chunhang's clothing was by now utterly unlike what it had been — he had only a few everyday old garments left — and moreover he was sitting in the shadow; his appearance was all the dimmer and darker for it, and Rong Guan did not come over to acknowledge him at all. He only heard Rong Guan say: "Second Master, someone was very grateful for your kindness yesterday." The plump man said: "Who was that?" Rong Guan said: "Er Xi of the Lianzhu troupe — he says you are very good to him, gave him quite a number of things, and he stayed at your house for a night. Is that right?" The plump man said:`,`"I don't know him, actually. That day Master Wei brought him into town and they drank a few cups of wine; by evening the city gates were closed, so he stayed overnight. In the morning they visited a temple, and then he said he wanted to buy a few small things and went out. This Er Xi is all right — he knows how to make himself agreeable." Rong Guan said: "Nowadays everyone is about making themselves agreeable. Our master was not good enough — when he was teaching us to perform, he forgot to teach us how to make ourselves agreeable."`,`The young man put his hand on Rong Guan's shoulder and said: "You too just need to know how to make yourself agreeable — would Master Fu San not like you then?" Rong Guan said: "I've said I don't know how. If you would teach me, I would take you as my master. However you teach me, that's how I'll learn from you." The young man smiled and gave Rong Guan's cheek a pinch. Rong Guan turned his head and caught sight of Chunhang; he lowered his eyes briefly, then cast them back for a lingering look — then turned away again. After a long moment he turned back again and looked Chunhang up and down, in a manner that seemed to want to acknowledge him but then stopped itself. Chunhang was quite puzzled and thought: "Can it be that he doesn't see clearly? This is already mid-spring, yet people are still wearing medium-weight furs. Chunhang was already in a full cotton-padded garment.`,`His boots were also stained with mud — utterly unlike his former appearance. Rong Guan was therefore startled, and inwardly thought: "It looks plainly like Master Tian — but how has he come down to this? Sitting there all alone in the cold and quiet." He was inclined to go over and acknowledge him, but feared it might not be him. After looking more carefully and satisfying himself, he went over, made a greeting bow, sat down, and they talked quite a good while. Fu San paid no attention; but Pincai noticed and tugged at Fu San's sleeve, saying: "Look there — Rong Guan is actually paying court to that person. What sort of person could that be, to have anything worth paying court to?" Fu San said: "Hard to say." Rong Guan took his leave of Chunhang and returned to Fu San's side. Pincai said with a smirk to Rong Guan: "Some grand old patron, eh?" Rong Guan's face reddened and he said: "He really was grand, once. He never left debts for others to settle on his behalf." After a short while, Fu San and the others took Rong Guan with them and called for another performer, and they all left.`,"The sky had turned to a fine drizzle. Chunhang had no more heart for watching; he paid what he owed and left. Outside, the ground was already slippery as oil. After a while, the carriage of Cuibao of the Quanfu troupe came straight toward him; Cuibao caught sight of him, turned his head away, and passed right by. Chunhang felt distinctly displeased and could only lower his head and slowly pick his way toward the drier patches of ground.",`Who would have expected that the streets here were narrow and crowded with horses and carts — where was there any dry ground to be found? Up ahead a large mule-cart came bearing down at flying speed, its curtain drawn, a person perched on the carriage rail beside the driver. The road was too narrow; the cart was already upon Chunhang. The mule tossed its head and knocked against Chunhang's shoulder; Chunhang swerved, stepped on a slippery patch, lost his footing, and went sprawling. The fall was rather well-timed in one respect — he was splattered head to foot with mud, yet his face was spared. The person inside the carriage was startled and hurried to lift the curtain, leaning out with a voice like a warbler calling: "Quickly hold the beast still and help that person up!"`,'The driver had already leaped down and reined in the mule; the manservant also jumped down to help Chunhang up. Chunhang, burning with shame and rage, was on the verge of scolding the driver, when he saw the one riding in the carriage — smiling profusely and leaning out from inside — saying: "You must have had a dreadful fright! The driver was careless and failed to look out properly — how dreadful to have soiled your clothes so!" And she gave the driver a few sharp words of reproof.',"Chunhang looked—and saw before him a young actor of surpassing, unearthly beauty. A sudden flash of transcendent light seemed to stream from within the carriage, bathing his vision in radiance. In that instant, every trace of his burning anger vanished into the void, leaving no shadow behind. The young actor seemed fashioned from purest ice and snow, sculpted from the most flawless jade. Within his grace lay deeper grace, and beyond his fragrance lingered an even more ethereal perfume.",'Radiant as the bright moon, pristine as pear blossoms, he was arrayed entirely in white silk—a pure, unsullied reflection of his orchid heart and sweet-grass spirit, exuding a breathtaking, unblemished purity. Chunhang gazed at him, utterly spellbound. Could the fabled Turmeric Hall of the Lu family or the opulent brocade screens of Shi Chong ever have sheltered a beauty such as this? In that fleeting moment, all the karmic debts of five hundred lifetimes and the romantic tribulations of three thousand kalpas came crashing down upon him. Forgetting entirely his own disgrace in the mire, he simply beamed with a rapturous smile. Pressing his two mud-stained hands against the carriage rim, he said: "It is nothing, nothing at all. A mere misstep of my own carelessness. My garments are old and weathered; their spoiling is of no consequence. I beg you, let it not trouble your heart." Bowing repeatedly by the roadside, he urged: "Please, go in peace." The young actor leaned out once more, offering graceful, lingering apologies before the carriage rolled away. Chunhang stood rooted to the earth, his eyes tracing the carriage until it vanished into the distance, before finally turning to resume his way. The passersby, seeing his disheveled state, covered their mouths to hide their laughter.',`Chunhang, dripping mud and water, made his way step by step back to the temple, regretting all the while that he had not thought to ask which troupe this small-role actor belonged to. Entering the temple gate, he took off his clothes and handed them to Tian An to clean, changed out of his muddy boots, and in only a lined jacket went to sit in Gao Pin's room. Gao Pin, seeing that he wore no outer robe, and that the rain had made the weather cold, asked why he was not wearing more. Chunhang explained that the rain had soaked his clothes and he had had Tian An dry them out. Gao Pin took a robe from his own luggage and gave it to him to wear. Chunhang sat down and said: "Although I took a spill today and got rather muddy, that particular spill was in fact a most amusing one. Two months of making merry with young players was not as satisfying as this one fall. By some extraordinary dispensation of Heaven I encountered a surpassing beauty — even if I had fallen to my death from the encounter, I would not become a wronged ghost." Gao Pin laughed: "What sort of nonsense are you talking?" Chunhang then described the young actor he had seen, and Gao Pin said: "Let me compose a little verse in the style of the Classic of Poetry and recite it for you." He then recited:`,`"Rain falls, rain falls, east of the Pear Garden.
There is one beautiful person, whose carriage moves swiftly.
Not the carriage moves swiftly —
Why is he in the midst of the mud?"

It is in the mode of direct presentation.
Chunhang laughed and then described the young actor's appearance and clothing, and even the color of the mule-cart's curtain, and asked: "Do you know which troupe this actor belongs to?" Gao Pin thought for a moment and said: "From your description, it must be either Lu Sulan, or Jin Shufang — or else Yuan Baozhu." Chunhang said: "Jin Shufang is in the Lianzhu troupe and I have seen his performance — he is rather thin and spare-looking; not him. As for Lu Sulan and Yuan Baozhu, I do not recognize either — I cannot say who this was." Gao Pin said: "Yuan Baozhu is not much given to wearing pale-colored garments. From your description, it doesn't quite sound like Lu Sulan either. Then it must be Su Huifang — yes, it must be Su Meixiang. A truly ice-clear autumn sky of purity, absolutely spotless and refined. He is neither heavy nor slight in build, with a face like a hen's egg, and habitually wears pale-colored garments. He is in the Lianjin troupe. Shi Zhujun ranked him second." Chunhang said: "Only second — then who is first? Is there really someone finer than him?" Gao Pin said: "The first is Yuan Baozhu. In a few days when the spring drains are opened, you will see for yourself." Chunhang said: "Why then?" Gao Pin said: "Seeing the second-ranked actor already landed you in the carriage rut — if you were to see the first-ranked, you might well end up in the drainage ditch." Chunhang just kept laughing; he continued to trace the young actor's image in his mind, and after a while that face and voice, those graceful spirit and charm, appeared vividly before him — he sat there without speaking, smiling foolishly to himself. He dined that evening at Gao Pin's and they talked on until the third watch, when they each went to bed.`,"The next day the sky had cleared. Chunhang rose very early, hung out his clothes to air and dry, brushed away the mud, changed into a new pair of boots, and felt the urge to go and hear some opera — but was tormented by the lack of funds, having nothing left to pawn.","He thought of Tian An, who still had a few garments. He went to Tian An's room, but found him absent; unable to wait, he opened Tian An's luggage pack, and there inside, wrapped up, was a lined robe of pongee silk. He took it out, sent the young servant Zhanghe to pawn it — yielding six strings of cash — and was greatly pleased. He skipped eating and went to see the Lianjin troupe five days running before catching a glimpse of that young actor. He watched him perform one scene of a solo opera, then inquired after his name — it was indeed Su Huifang. Huifang happened to pass near Chunhang, recognized him as the one who had tumbled in the mud the other day, and, noticing his clothes still mottled with stains, could not help smiling faintly — but felt too awkward to come over and acknowledge him. Chunhang, seeing Huifang smile at him, felt as if he had encountered a jade maiden tossing rings from Heaven, as if Heaven itself had opened its mouth in welcome — and was so happy he could find no words. He thought and thought, but alas he could not call him over. And looking at the way this one conducted himself, it seemed he would not easily consent to sit beside someone at a banquet — he was clearly not the kind to come flying over at the drop of a note. With no plan in mind, he sighed and groaned by day; his dreams were tossed and turned at night; he was on the verge of coming down with lovesickness. Then Tian An staged *Exclusive Possession* would come in with one fretful complaint after another — saying he had pawned his robe, what did he need to keep it for — and also saying that coal and rice were completely gone, that the shop refused to extend further credit because the previous debt was still unpaid, and that the monks were pressing for the rent, which was due positively the next day.","Chunhang simply let it wash over him; he lay on the heated bed fully clothed, thinking only of Huifang. Tian An went out, muttering and grumbling without pause. Chunhang was also somewhat at a loss.","But in his whole life he had never asked anything of anyone — to whom could he go now to borrow? Moreover, he had been in the capital for two or three months without paying a single social call on any fellow provincial or old acquaintance; how could he now go to ask someone for a loan? Then his thoughts turned to Yan Zhongqing — just one meeting between them the other day, yet he had spontaneously given him two hundred taels of silver, without Chunhang even asking. This person was truly an ancient man in a modern age. It seemed he had not done it out of the slender bond of distant kinship — he must have understood Chunhang's true nature. It seemed there was still room to discuss matters with him.",`After spending the night in thought, the next morning he wrote a letter — not stating things plainly, but hinting obliquely in terms that suggested a plea for help — and sent Zhanghe to deliver it. Chunhang waited at home for good news; before long Zhanghe returned empty-handed, without even a reply letter, and said: "The gatekeepers said Young Master Yan had received the note and would send a reply shortly." Chunhang assumed he must be working out the money, ate his meal, and waited a while. Then suddenly someone came from Yan Zhongqing, carrying a rolled painting, and said: "Our young master sends his respects to your honor. He asks that your honor write an inscription on this painting — I am to wait and take it back." Chunhang was mystified, and no reply letter had come; he could only open the painting and look. It was a portrait of Zheng Yuanhe painted by Tang Liuru — Zheng in his tattered rags, begging for food in the wind and snow. Chunhang understood at once that he was being mocked; an involuntary surge of fury reddened his cheeks. Yet he could not very well make a scene in front of the messenger, so he simply said: "Wait outside for a moment — I shall write something at once." The messenger went out. Chunhang, seething with indignation, spread the painting on the table, and saw that two seven-character quatrains had already been inscribed on it, under the signature "Composed by Jiantan." The poems read: "On the day the prince went begging, starving near the Huai —
On the day the Minister Wu languished in strange poverty —"`,`"These are the trials that test the mettle of true heroes through the ages;
How unlike the frivolous idler, adrift in the pleasure lanes?

Though his coat bears a hundred patches and his sheepskin hangs in tatters,
He still sings the lotus song, his spirit unbroken, knowing no shame.
Had that bewitching beauty not harbored a heroine's fierce and steadfast devotion,
His life would have withered away, a wanderer never to return homeward."

Chunhang thought to himself: "Though his mockery cuts deep, his reasoning strikes true—how am I to counter such words?" Gathering his thoughts for but a moment, he took up the brush and, with a single, unbroken sweep of his wrist, wrote:`,`"To temper the moth-browed beauty into a paragon of righteous devotion,
One must first allow the noble steed to endure the bitter trials of hardship.
Such was the grand design of Heaven, woven from the very beginning—
A truth easily understood only by one whose heart has known the depths of true feeling.

A talent to eclipse all ages—truly no hollow boast;
Even in the tattered remnants of a beggar’s coat, the soul of a passionate man burns bright.
Let not the gilded youths of the Five Tombs regard him with fleeting contempt—
For who among them could ever rival the shining glory of his eventual name?"

He signed his name with a flourish, pressed his crimson seal onto the paper, rolled the painting back into its silk wrapper, and handed it to the messenger. Weighed down by a heavy heart and an air of quiet desolation, Chunhang walked out into the world alone.`,"The messenger returned and presented the painting. Zhongqing and Wang Xun read it together. The two quatrains, though forced in their argument, revealed something of their author's spirit — and they could not help feeling a renewed pity for the man. Zhongqing had originally intended his two poems to reform Chunhang; who would have thought they would only provoke him further? He also heard from the messenger that Chunhang's circumstances were still more wretched than before.",`According to his manservant's account, that very day the kitchen fire had gone out, and there was nothing to cook. Zhongqing and Wang Xun both sighed. Zhongqing said: "From the look of things, this man is truly like the saying: 'My heart is not a stone — it cannot be turned.' What can be done? What can be done?" Wang Xun said: "You sent him two hundred taels the other day; in less than half a month it has completely evaporated. Given the way this man behaves, sending him another two hundred taels would be equally useless. Unless you were to divide half of Xu Duxiang's entire fortune and give it to him — then he would have something to squander. But a man who cannot even keep his kitchen fire going is in a sorry state. In my view, we might help him with another hundred taels right now, to be held at Zhuoran's, who can dispense it as circumstances warrant, and gradually enlighten him. Perhaps relying on Zhuoran's way with words, he might yet be brought around — it is not entirely impossible." Zhongqing agreed. Wang Xun immediately prepared a hundred taels and handed it to Zhongqing to deliver to Gao Pin's quarters. As for what happened next — hear it told in the following chapter.`]},u=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations12:a},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),t={13:["After Zhongqing had provoked Chunhang into anger, he sent the hundred taels that Wang Xun had prepared to Gao Pin, to cover Chunhang's daily expenses of firewood and food. Chunhang sat brooding for two days; pressed on all sides for rice, coal, and the repayment of debts, with nowhere to turn for a loan, he accepted Gao Pin's kind offer to stay and was thus obliged to take his meals there for the time being.","One day, after eating, Gao Pin went out to pay calls, and Chunhang went to the theater, his heart entirely fixed upon Su Huifang. Having no money to watch the opera, he could only stand at the entrance, waiting to catch a glimpse of Huifang going in or coming out. Just as the performance was about to begin, he did indeed see Huifang arrive in a carriage and step out at the entrance. But just then a crowd of people pressed in to see the opera, and in the jostling Chunhang was shoved to the rear, making it impossible for them to come face to face. Chunhang was deeply vexed; he pushed his way out again, but Huifang had already gone inside. He could only stand there motionless, waiting for him to come out. While waiting he also saw many of the celebrated performers, every one the equal of Huifang.","Chunhang thought to himself that he never would have expected the Lianjin troupe to have such fine performers, proving their fame was no exaggeration. He waited a full three hours before seeing Huifang emerge with his head lowered. Two splendidly dressed beautiful young men walked ahead of him, and two manservants carrying cushions and clothing bundles followed, and they all boarded his carriage and were gone. Chunhang, knowing Huifang had not seen him, returned home in a mood of dejection.","He passed the night, and the next day went again to wait at the theater entrance. Yet again he failed to see him, letting the day slip by in vain while sighing endlessly. Chunhang had been caught in this obsession for so long that he could not easily awaken from it, going every day to seek out the Lianjin troupe and wait for Huifang. For more than ten days in a row, Huifang did notice the man who had previously fallen in the mud. Every time he got in or out of his carriage, that man was always standing by the theater entrance, staring at him with unblinking, entranced devotion. This struck Huifang as deeply strange. He looked closely at his face and saw that he possessed a pure and refined grace. Although his features bore a trace of haggard weariness, his elegant bones and poetic charm were vividly apparent. Huifang already knew in his heart that this person had come for his sake, and he could not help but feel touched, casting many a lingering, affectionate glance his way. Chunhang, overjoyed to the point of dancing, would follow Huifang's carriage every day, walking alongside it until they reached the gate of Huifang's lodgings in Jixiang Alley, where he would pace back and forth for a long while before finally departing.","One day, Chunhang's luck finally turned, perhaps by the hand of destiny. He was following Huifang's carriage as usual when Huifang, paying special attention, felt a stir of tender pity in his heart. As soon as he entered his gate, he told his manservant to invite the gentleman inside. The manservant went out.","He looked Chunhang up and down and asked if the gentleman was looking for their master, adding that their master had invited him in for tea. Chunhang was overjoyed beyond expectation, yet he stood rooted to the spot, unable to step forward. The manservant invited him a second time, but Chunhang remained overwhelmingly shy and hesitant. Suddenly someone else came out from inside and said to invite the gentleman who had been following the carriage to come in. Chunhang had no choice but to straighten his clothes and follow the manservant through the main gate. He found himself in a courtyard lined on both sides with delicate double bamboo fences. It was late in the second month, and a hundred flowers were in full bloom, filling the entire courtyard with vibrant crimsons and purples and a dazzling, fragrant beauty. Above sat a small, three-room reception hall, furnished with ancient bronzes and zithers and books, exquisite and elegant in every detail. Before long, Su Huifang emerged wearing a plain-colored pearl-trimmed fur garment, and stepped forward to offer a courteous greeting. Chunhang immediately took his hand, finding it as soft as fresh spring shoots, delicate and slender. The two stood side by side, almost exactly the same height, for Huifang was also seventeen years old. Huifang looked at Chunhang with a smile, remarking that they had seen each other every day, yet he still did not know his esteemed name and native place. He expressed his deep gratitude for Chunhang's generous forgiveness regarding the incident in the mud the other day, and added that to be favored with his continuous attention was a blessing accumulated over three lifetimes. Chunhang was deeply astonished by his elegant speech and graceful choice of words. He replied that ever since gazing upon his lovely face, he had been haunted by him day and night. He said his heart was filled with admiration, which he could only swear to in secret. To be invited in rather than rebuked was a sign of true understanding and deep feeling that he, Tian Xiangzhi, would never forget until the end of his days. He then detailed his native place and family name, and spoke further words of yearning. They looked at each other in silence for a long moment, communicating without words.","Huifang then invited Chunhang inside. They left the reception hall, passing through the western bamboo fence into a small courtyard containing a row of five rooms. The easternmost room was set aside as a guest room for unexpected visitors. The middle two were kept open as a small study, and the western suite served as Huifang's bedchamber. Chunhang first sat on the heated bed in the middle room. He saw eight meticulously detailed paintings of 'Purification Rite on the Long River,' 'Picture of the Immortals' Grand Assembly' by Qiu Shizhou hanging on the walls. The sides were lined with nanmu-wood frames set with glass windows, and the floor was covered with a pure blue velvet carpet, completely spotless. After sitting for a moment, Huifang led him into the western suite. The space was divided by a floor-to-ceiling wooden partition carved in an 'ice-plum' pattern. In the outer room stood two small bookshelves and a curio cabinet. Above was a small wooden heated bed, spread with a beige, finely embroidered cushion. On the bed-table rested a rectangular Ding-ware porcelain basin, from which five or six pure-hearted orchids bloomed. On the main wall hung six pieces of small regular script on gold-flecked paper, each written by a different hand in balanced, elegant calligraphy. The signatures included 'Master of Duxiang', 'The Recluse Jingyi', 'The Lyricist Zhujun', 'The Mountain Man Jiantan', 'The Unofficial Historian Qianzhou', and 'The Lay Scholar Yongan'. They appeared to be matching seven-character regulated verse poems. Looking closer at the dedication, it read: 'By Meixiang's request, matching the original rhymes of the six seven-character verses on the Long River Purification Ceremony.' Chunhang's respect deepened even further. He thought to himself:",`"So he knows how to compose poetry." He then asked to see the original poem, noting that his knowledge of poetry must be exceedingly profound. Huifang laughed and replied that it was mere scribbling, just cobbling together a few colloquial phrases, not true poetry. Chunhang asked why the original verses were not displayed. Huifang explained that Yuan Baozhu had written them out for him the previous year, but someone had borrowed them to look at and lost them. Chunhang looked at Huifang more closely, observing that his refined bearing and elegant speech were entirely unlike those of an ordinary performer. He asked when he had come to the capital to learn the opera. A look of shame passed over Huifang's face, and with a sigh he said that since he asked of his origins, he would tell him that he too came from a respectable family, and his father had once been an official. Chunhang stood up in apology, saying he had indeed felt he did not look like someone from a humble background, and asked why he had been forced to take up this profession. Huifang's eyes grew red, and he said:`,`"Please sit, and I will tell you." Chunhang sat down, and Huifang continued. He explained that as a child he had followed his father to his official post in Yunnan. His mother had died when he was eight. When he was twelve, his father was impeached by a superior, fell ill from the shock and anger, and died within a month. Having always been an upright official with empty sleeves, they had no private savings. What little remained was quickly divided up and plundered by relatives and attendants, leaving him with only one old servant. They stayed in Yunnan for over a year. It was pitiful to be entirely without family, and in that world of snobbery, no one was willing to help; they survived solely by the old servant taking on menial labor. When they could no longer sustain themselves, they had no choice but to head home. On the road they endured every kind of bitter hardship, walking for a year and two months before finally reaching Suzhou. There they found only overgrown weeds and ruin. The world had changed completely, and relatives and neighbors looked upon them with cold eyes, refusing to lift a finger. They could not even borrow a single branch for shelter or beg a single meal. After staying in a temple for a few days, they found out that a relative was working as a secretary in Zhili. Expending the effort of nine bulls and two tigers, they managed to board a grain transport boat and travel north. They first went to Baoding to seek out this relative, only to discover he had been involved in some trouble and had already been exiled beyond the Great Wall, with his family's whereabouts unknown. He asked Chunhang if he did not think this was a wretched fate. Chunhang agreed that it was indeed a case of the mountains and rivers ending with no path in sight, and asked what had happened next. Huifang said they could not stay in Baoding, so they decided to come to the capital to find a way to survive. Just as they arrived outside the Front Gate, they fortuitously met a good man, a fellow townsman who had also been his childhood tutor, Mr. Gu. He was a scholar. Seeing their miserable state, he took them to his lodgings and asked them about everything that had happened. He asked Chunhang if he could guess what this tutor was doing in the capital.`,"Chunhang replied that he naturally assumed he was teaching. Huifang said he was not teaching, and that his behavior was somewhat like Chunhang's own. He was only twenty-seven this year. Upon arriving in the capital, he had spent every day listening to the opera until his money ran out, but by then he had learned the operas by heart and befriended many of the performers, eventually becoming an opera instructor. When he failed the provincial examinations that year, he burned all his essays and joined the Lianjin troupe as a young male lead. Chunhang remarked that this was the action of a truly liberated man, completely free from conventional constraints. Huifang continued, explaining that Mr. Gu had taken them in. In his free time, he taught Huifang to read and write, and discussed poetry and lyrics with him, allowing them to live in peace and stability. Tragically, the old servant, having suffered the wind and frost of the journey and carrying deep sorrow in his heart, fell ill upon entering the capital and died two months later. At that point, Huifang felt even more isolated and alone, caught in a dilemma with nowhere to turn, and could only rely entirely on his teacher for survival. It was not until the spring of the previous year that his teacher earnestly urged him to learn the opera. He had been unwilling at first, but realizing he had no other path, he followed his teacher's advice. He learned a few roles, and gradually, day by day, he naturally grew into the profession. His teacher loved to compose poetry, and whenever he wrote a poem, he would explain it to him and teach him how to write. Though his attempts were clumsy, he began to enjoy it himself. But his fate was unfortunate and his deep debt of gratitude remained unpaid. In the summer of the previous year, his teacher contracted a seasonal illness and passed away. Left entirely alone, he could only look at his shadow and pity himself. Speaking to this point, he choked back tears.","Hearing this, Chunhang also felt a deep sorrow, remarking that in five years the stars had shifted and things had changed, and he had tasted so much of the ways of the world. He offered a few words of comfort. Then, drinking two cups of tea, Huifang asked Chunhang if, since he enjoyed the opera so much, he had come to admire any particular performers in the various troupes. Chunhang laughed and confessed that he prized beauty over artistry, paying no attention to the technical aspects of the opera and having no understanding of how to rank performers. He simply took those who had fine looks and treasured them as the supreme. He admitted that at first he was reckless, his eye untrained, and upon meeting any seductive face he would be quite swept away. But having now gazed upon Huifang's jade-like countenance, he felt as though the wondrous dwelling Bodhisattva had appeared upon the lotus throne, beyond what a creature of the lower world could ever hope to approach. He said his past experiences were like Wang Xizhi studying calligraphy under Lady Wei, a futile expenditure of years, and his shame and remorse had no end. Huifang, having heard these words, was already half won over. He gazed at Chunhang for a good while without speaking, then smiled again and asked: 'You say you treasure those with fine looks as supreme, but I would like to know, what specifically is it that you treasure?' Chunhang stood up, and in high spirits, arms and feet moving expressively, face glowing with animation, he said: 'Meixiang, you are a person of understanding, try to guess!' Huifang blushed slightly and said he would not know how to guess.","Chunhang replied that it was for no other reason at all. Huifang then asked with a serious expression what the reason was. Chunhang declared that if the looks were fine and the temperament fine, he would willingly die for that person. Huifang asked why someone else being fine had anything to do with him, and why he should die for them. He urged him to explain what it was that deserved such treasuring. Chunhang asked him to listen. He explained that for those who travel thousands of miles from home, aside from two or three intimate companions, there are four other kinds of fine friends that are most difficult to obtain, and even when obtained they commonly have some unsatisfying imperfection. So while one can call them treasures, one cannot call them the supreme treasure. Huifang expressed surprise and asked to be enlightened about these four kinds of fine friends. Chunhang said the first was fine weather: the evening sun, the bright moon, gentle rain, clear breezes, light mist, or crystalline snow. Even sitting alone, one's heart and spirit become open and glad. One feels the beauty of spring and autumn days, lingering over the scenery and surroundings. Whether in the open countryside or a pavilioned courtyard, elegant bamboo and scattered blossoms present themselves. Under the shade of parasol trees or willows, one recites verse and strolls slowly, savoring the fragrant season and forgetting the mundane world.","Huifang nodded, agreeing that this was indeed fine, and guessed that the second must be fine scenery. Chunhang confirmed this, describing a hill, a valley, the mountains and waters serene and secluded, with every step revealing a changed view and drawing one deeper in. The third, he said, was fine books, those that lack a single dead sentence or a single idle brushstroke, leaving the reader endlessly exploring. Huifang nodded again. Chunhang said the fourth was fine poetry arising from the spirit's depths, noting there was no need to confine oneself to worshipping Du Fu and venerating Han Yu. If one or two lines could express what others could not express, one might contend with the ancients for supremacy.","Huifang praised him as a person of refined and universal understanding. Chunhang countered that while these four friends are indeed fine, each has its imperfection. He asked how many days of fine weather there are in a month. Often the morning is beautiful and by afternoon it changes, spoiling even the morning's beauty. When one's mood flags, one begins to dread wind and rain and dares not linger. He asked how many places of fine scenery exist in an entire province. Some can only be reached on foot by treacherous or obscure paths, over sand and rocks and thorns, miles away, leaving one exhausted, often knowing its beauty yet unwilling to make the journey. As for books, aside from the few volumes recited in every household, the ocean of writing is vast, yet most of it wastes wood and paper. Even where something new emerges from the old, it reflects individual hands and styles, and cannot fully satisfy every taste. Poetry is small in scope yet difficult to perfect. Sometimes a newly composed poem seems like refined gold, yet upon rereading it tastes like chewing wax, leaving one regretting having let the brush fall too easily. These four friends are difficult to obtain, and finding them perfectly satisfying is even more difficult, so while one can call them treasures, they are not the supreme treasure. He concluded that only those of Huifang's distinguished profession form a perfectly beautiful friend of unmatched quality, a treasure friend beyond these four.","Huifang laughed, finding the term 'treasure friend' quite remarkable, and confessed they had no idea they possessed anything worth treasuring. Chunhang elaborated: 'Soft as jade, warm as perfume, flowering with richness, gleaming like snow, that is the treasure of beauty. Rounded like Lady Huan, slender like Lady Yan, flesh smooth and bone fragrant, that is the treasure of the body. Bright eyes that glance bewitchingly, a coquettish smile and an artful pout, that is the treasure of the face. A thousand charms gathered to one side, a hundred allurements born across the whole, that is the treasure of manner. Tender crying that sips the dew, lovely speech that chides the flowers, that is the treasure of feeling. Pearl hairpins carved in kingfisher hues, gold hair ornaments with flying rosy clouds, that is the treasure of adornment. Add to all this the pure song and the exquisite dance, the sandalwood clappers and the golden goblets, the trills of the voice-box and the light steps on the verge of falling, and one calls this the treasure of artistry, the treasure of a person.' Huifang replied that his argument was truly excellent, though in some places it set the standard too high. Even as Huifang said this, he was in fact deeply moved and grateful toward Chunhang within his heart. He could not help casting languid sidelong glances and allowing his powdered cheeks to soften with sweet tenderness. He studied Chunhang carefully, and the more he looked the more fine qualities he found. In his eyes, all the wealthy princes and elegant young gentlemen were quite eclipsed.","Chunhang continued: 'Tea-smoke and the sound of the qin, wind and rain and the cock's crow at night, thinking of one's old friends with a heart that reaches a thousand miles. Without a kindred spirit to share morning and evening, where is there joy to be found? And then in the desolate temple, loneliness and sorrow, a fading lamp and solitude. Where indeed can one find two or three intimate companions to endure the bleakness together? Only you people of refinement can offer a word or half a phrase that sinks into the bones of the heart, turning a hundred-tempered blade of steel into soft silk wrapped around the finger. And then a heavenly beauty like you. Even if you could only be seen and not approached, I would be like the pilgrim who, upon catching sight of the Bodhisattva, prostrates himself at every step, and I would do that willingly. How could I dare to harbor any other hope?' Hearing this, Huifang could not restrain the tears that flowed down his face. He said he now understood Chunhang's heart and told him to say no more. He then asked Chunhang to give a full account of everything that had happened since he arrived in the capital. Chunhang recounted everything from the previous winter to the present day. Huifang smiled again, praising him as a person of utterly devoted feeling and wild fancy, yet admiring his two legs for running every day and standing motionless at the theater entrance. Chunhang declared that if it were not for Huifang, he would not come even if invited. Huifang smiled, went out, and directed someone to bring in several kinds of fruit, several dishes, and two pots of wine, inviting Chunhang to drink a little.","Chunhang made no show of refusal. The two sat across from each other at the small huali-wood table and drank, each pouring out what was deepest in their hearts. By this time Huifang's own heart was completely drawn in, without a trace of calculating self-interest.","When the meal was finished, they moved to the inner room and sat for a little while. Huifang then told the manservant to hitch up the carriage outside and take Master Tian back to his lodgings. Huifang clasped Chunhang's hand and declared that they had become friends today, and must not betray this friendship for the rest of their lives. He swore that if he, Su Huifang, spoke a single false word, the bright sun would be his witness. He told Chunhang he need not come out to look for him anymore, promising to visit him early or late, every single day. He urged Chunhang to take good care of himself and make an effort toward his prospects, pleading with him not to lose his good name and give others occasion to talk for their sake. Hearing this, Chunhang was so moved that his admiration turned to reverence, the feeling penetrating to his very marrow, and tears streamed down his face. The two looked at each other and choked with emotion for a while. Only the manservants and other attendants did not understand what this was about and found it quite puzzling. Still talking as they went, they came outside. Huifang saw Chunhang into the carriage and gave him a few more parting words, and Chunhang went directly back to his lodgings.","Huifang then went to bed, carefully recalling everything Chunhang had said and pondering his situation for a long time. When he fell asleep, he dreamt of Chunhang appearing before him, dressed in magnificent robes and an imposing crown, his expression so stern it inspired awe. Then Chunhang transformed into a middle-aged man wearing the robes of a first-rank official. He had a restless night of confused dreams and woke up late the next morning.","It was already breakfast time when he finally washed his face and ate a light meal. His manservant came in and announced that there were guests outside. Huifang asked who it was, and the manservant replied that it was Master Zhang from Fuhu Bridge, accompanied by Third Master Pan of the Qisheng Silver House. Huifang had no choice but to dress and go out to meet them.","This Master Zhang was none other than Zhang Zhongyu. The Master Pan was Pan Qiguan, a wealthy man of the capital with a vast fortune. He owned three silver houses, two pawnshops, and a spice shop, and had purchased a sixth-rank official title. His family originally hailed from Shanxi but had lived in the capital for two generations. He was a petty, vulgar, and stubbornly foolish man, incredibly stingy, and an insatiable lecher who pursued his desires relentlessly day and night. He had one redeeming quality: he was entirely terrified of his wife. His current wife, whom he had married after his first wife's death, was quite beautiful, but she was exceptionally fierce and jealous. Though she had little affection for Pan, she strictly forbade him from making trouble outside. If he failed to return home for a single night, she would take a carriage and lead her servants to search through all the brothels; if she caught him, she would create a tremendous, earth-shattering scene. Left with no other options, Pan had recently conceived the idea of purchasing a young boy, and had been keeping an eye out among the various theater troupes.","He had set his sights on Su Huifang. Today he had dragged Zhang Zhongyu along to act as a go-between. Zhongyu thought to himself that a person of Huifang's refined character was unlikely to agree to follow Pan Qiguan, so he was evasive and reluctant to help. However, after Pan pleaded with him repeatedly and promised a heavy reward of gold and silk, he had no choice but to come along, intending to just see how things played out. They arrived at Huifang's house, sat down, and made idle conversation.","Consider the appearance of this Pan Qiguan:","He was short and stout, with a round, dark-complexioned face and a mouthful of yellowish, bristly hair resembling pig bristles. He was over forty years old, with a protruding belly and flat buttocks, exuding a vulgar and foul air. He wore a padded jacket of black silk, a soft cap edged with velvet trailing a small marten tail, black satin socks, and grey trimmed shoes. A short tobacco pipe hung from the lapel of his jacket, revealing half of a green leather tobacco pouch. His pale yellow eyes were laced with red veins. He smiled ingratiatingly, speaking in a low, obsequious voice, putting on a grand show of humble warmth. Huifang had no choice but to sit and keep them company. Zhang Zhongyu looked at Huifang, appearing as though he wanted to speak but holding back. Huifang lowered his head, stood up at one point to look at the orchids by the window, his mind entirely on Tian Chunhang. Unable to simply ask them to leave, he sat in restless, listless discomfort. Pan Qiguan sat motionless, not speaking a word, his eyes fixed entirely on Huifang. Zhang Zhongyu suggested they not bother finding another place, but simply arrange a small banquet right there and have a few dishes prepared. Pan Qiguan enthusiastically agreed, noting it was peaceful in the house. Huifang replied that while it was a fine idea, he could not keep them company for long because he had to perform at the Gusu Guildhall, and his act was the second on the program. Pan Qiguan dismissed this, saying it did not matter if he skipped it. Huifang insisted he had to go. Pan Qiguan argued that since he had no master binding him, he had nothing to fear, and as such a popular star, he could not possibly offend anyone. Huifang remained silent and finally ordered his manservant to quickly prepare wine. Before long, the wine and dishes were set out. Huifang offered them seats, and Pan Qiguan pushed Zhongyu to take the seat of honor. After drinking a few cups, Pan Qiguan began a relentless stream of nauseating, overly familiar chatter. Huifang was thoroughly disgusted and hatched a plan. Feigning attentive hospitality, he stood up to offer several toasts and played a few rounds of drinking games, hoping to get Pan completely drunk so he could make his escape.","Little did he know that Pan Qiguan was a seasoned drinker who only grew more obnoxious with every cup. As the wine went down, his words grew fouler. He reached out with his short, fat, stiff hand and grabbed Huifang's hand, calling him a good child and asking why he never came to see him, claiming he missed him terribly. He said that every time he saw him perform, he dreamt of him at night, playing together intimately, only to wake up feeling exhausted. He accused Huifang of being the death of him, and since he had no son, he questioned what use his vast fortune was. He proposed that Huifang become his adopted son so they could enjoy themselves together every day. Hearing this, Huifang was so furious he nearly cried. His eyes reddened as he thought to himself that this wretched slave had no sense of his own standing, and decided to trick him thoroughly. Suppressing his anger, he put on a smiling face and told Pan he was speaking nonsense. He claimed a clumsy child like himself could never hope for such a connection. He pointed out that Pan listened to operas every day but never even cast a glance his way, nor had he ever cheered for him, and accused him of lying in front of Master Zhang just to deceive him. These few words left Pan Qiguan feeling light as a feather, entirely swept away.","Zhang Zhongyu was inwardly astonished. He thought it strange that Su Huifang actually seemed to like Pan Qiguan. He wondered if money truly could move the gods, and rejoiced that his own luck had arrived, anticipating a massive windfall.","He quickly chimed in, assuring Huifang that Third Master Pan truly praised him to everyone he met, admiring his looks, his temperament, and his skills, speaking of him at least twice a day. He claimed Pan often said that if Huifang would only favor him, he would give him an entire silver house. He then turned to Pan Qiguan and asked if those were not his very own words. Pan nodded vigorously. Huifang laughed and asked how many silver houses he had. He pointed out that if he gave one to every performer, with hundreds of performers in the capital, he would need hundreds of silver houses.","Pan Qiguan declared that while others could not get a single copper coin from him, if Huifang was willing, he would give him anything. Huifang, his plan firmly in mind, cast a sidelong glance at Pan Qiguan that completely captivated his soul. Zhongyu was also immensely pleased, tapping his fingers on the table and continuously shouting his approval. Huifang challenged Pan, saying that since he held him in his heart, he must drink freely today and not hide his true capacity. Pan Qiguan immediately called for large cups.","Huifang personally fetched two large cups, filled them with wine, and offered one to each of them. He then poured two more cups and told Pan that he had originally intended to drink a paired toast with him, but his tolerance was too low, so Pan must drink the pair himself. Pan Qiguan bobbed his head and drank them down. Huifang poured two more cups and turned to Zhongyu, urging him to also drink a paired toast. Zhongyu laughed and asked who he was supposed to pair with. Huifang suggested they pair together, but insisted he first pair with Pan. Zhongyu flicked Huifang's forehead, joking if he was even worthy. Huifang pressed him to drink, and he did. By this time, both Pan and Zhang were mostly drunk, though they had only eaten two dishes. Huifang then went into his room and changed into a fresh outfit, emerging looking even more striking. Pan began to knead his wrists in a sickeningly intimate manner. Huifang was incredibly anxious but could not run away, so he played more drinking games with them and sang a few short melodies. Zhang Zhongyu, seeing a pipa hanging on the wall, took it down and plucked the strings in accompaniment as they slowly conversed.","It was approaching late afternoon. Seeing they were not yet dead drunk, Huifang decided to test him further. He told Pan Qiguan that there was something he perhaps shouldn't say, given they had no real relationship. But he explained he was in a desperate situation, owing someone a sum of silver that had to be repaid the next day. He said he had planned to go out and raise the funds, but since the God of Wealth himself had arrived, he asked if Pan might be willing to accommodate him. Pan Qiguan asked how much he needed. Huifang said it was not much, just two hundred taels. Pan looked at Zhongyu, and Zhongyu remarked that it was absurd for Pan to hesitate over a mere two hundred taels for someone like Huifang, noting it made no difference in the long run. Pan paused for a long moment, then reached into his leggings and pulled out a leather account folder. He gathered together a stack of cash notes, in denominations of ten and eight strings, until they totaled two hundred strings of capital cash. He handed them to Huifang, telling him to take the two hundred strings for now. Huifang thanked him and tucked the notes into his boots, remarking that it was difficult to accept such a heavy reward. Pan Qiguan told him to just act on his conscience. Smiling sweetly, Huifang shot Pan a look that made him delirious with joy, drool literally leaking from his mouth. Huifang poured a large cup of wine and held it up, declaring that in honor of the two hundred strings of cash, he would break his own rule and offer Third Master Pan a 'skin-cup'. Hearing this, Pan felt his entire body go soft, and his chest began to heave with anticipation. Huifang took a mouthful of wine, walked over to Pan, and with a sweet smile, spat it back into the cup, laughing. Pan had already opened his mouth wide in expectation. Seeing this, Huifang picked up a piece of fish with his chopsticks and brought it to Pan's mouth. Pan took it. Huifang then picked up another piece and ate it himself, suddenly crying out in pain. Zhongyu warned him not to choke. Huifang gasped that he feared he had. Pan Qiguan called for rice, saying a mouthful would clear it.","The servant brought half a bowl of rice. Huifang ate a few bites, then leaned his head back against the chair, complaining it was useless and hurt terribly. Zhongyu suggested eating green olives to dissolve the bone. Huifang ate a few olives but still claimed it was no better. Pan came over, bringing his face close to Huifang's, acting coaxing and asking him to open his mouth so he could look. Huifang covered his face with his sleeve and asked how he could possibly see anything. He blamed the pain entirely on offering Pan the skin-cup, and promised that if Pan drank a few more cups, he would stop hurting. Pan asked if that was true, drank a large bowl, and asked if he felt better. Huifang nodded. Pan drank two more cups before stopping. Huifang then cried out in pain again. Pan forced Zhongyu to drink a cup as well. Huifang then claimed to feel better, saying that watching them drink so heartily made him forget the pain. By this time Pan was completely mesmerized and heavily intoxicated, oblivious to the trick being played on him. He grabbed hold of Zhongyu, and they began exchanging endless cups. Zhongyu was also drunk, losing all judgment, and they drank wildly together. Their intoxication soon reached an overwhelming extreme. A sudden wave hit Pan; his head spun, he leaned back, flipped backward with his feet in the air, and crashed to the floor. Seeing Pan drunk, Zhongyu stood up with a loud laugh, then immediately crouched down and collapsed to the side. The two of them lay on the floor looking half-dead, completely motionless. It was now dusk. Huifang ordered the table cleared and laughed, remarking that they had brought this upon themselves for trying to eat swan meat, and now they finally knew the fierce capability of Su Meixiang. He instructed his manservants to help them onto the heated bed in the reception room, remove their outer garments, lay a large quilt over them, and let the two of them enter the clouds of Shamans' Mountain together.","Having made all these arrangements, Huifang ordered his carriage to be hitched. He went to his own room, opened his chest, and selected five sets of clothing made of lightly padded cotton, lightweight silk, and single-layer gauze. He packed these together with the two-hundred-string cash notes Pan had given him, added a set of bedding, and had his manservant carry it all out and load it onto the carriage. Huifang mounted the carriage, his manservant rode on the footboard, and they headed together toward Chunhang's lodgings. Just as they reached the entrance of the alley, they spotted a figure standing in the moonlight. The driver looked closely and recognized Tian Chunhang. He stopped the carriage and called out that they were just on their way to his place. Hearing this, Huifang and the manservant both jumped down. Huifang seized Chunhang's hand and asked what he was doing there again. Chunhang replied that he had waited all day without seeing him and simply couldn't go on. He confessed he had been standing outside Huifang's door for a long while, too embarrassed to go in, and had eventually ended up waiting in the alley. Huifang sighed, calling him an impossible creature and saying he truly gave him no way to handle him. He asked Chunhang to sit inside the carriage while he rode on the footboard. They talked the whole way there and arrived at the temple gate, where they dismounted. The manservant took the clothing bundle and shouldered the bedding, and they all went inside together, sending the carriage back with instructions to return the next day.","Gao Pin had already gone to bed. Chunhang, not wanting to disturb him, went directly to his own room. Tian An was dozing over the table. Chunhang trimmed the lamp brighter and woke Tian An, telling him to quickly make tea. Tian An rubbed his eyes; seeing a beautiful young person, he assumed it was a young gentleman and hurried off to prepare the tea. Huifang sat down and, looking at the sparse and meager state of the lodgings, felt a genuine pang in his heart. He then had his manservant bring in the clothing and the cash notes. He told Chunhang that the five sets of clothing were things he had worn himself, and asked him to keep them if he didn't mind they were used. He offered the money for travel expenses, explaining he had originally intended to invite him to stay at his own place, but it would have been unsuitable for the sake of appearances. He urged Chunhang to simply ask him if he was ever short of anything. Chunhang protested, asking how he could possibly accept such gifts, declaring he absolutely could not receive them. Huifang replied that refusing them meant looking down on him, and asked if he thought he was playing a trick. He insisted Chunhang must not harbor such thoughts, because once calculation enters the heart, even genuine feeling becomes false. He asked only one thing of him: from that moment on, he was not allowed to go out to hear the opera. Chunhang eagerly gave his assent over and over. They spoke further of their most intimate thoughts and feelings, each moved by a sense of having found a kindred spirit, until they could not help but be stirred to deep emotion. Sitting facing each other, the two formed what was purely a friendship of moral principle, entirely devoid of any improper thought. They talked until the cock crowed, at which point each lay down, still clothed, and slept.","Meanwhile, Pan and Zhang slept through the night in complete oblivion. At the fourth watch, Pan Qiguan turned over, rolled off the heated bed with a rumble, and sat on the floor, needing to relieve himself. He groped around in all directions for a chamber pot. His hand found one of his own shoes; pulling down his trousers, he urinated into the shoe, soaking mostly into his own trouser seat. Fumbling in the dark and groping about the floor, he found the edge of the bed and climbed back up. His mind, while still muddled, tried to recall where he had been drinking, and somehow, even in his drunken stupor, his thoughts fixed on Su Huifang. He groped around on the bed, found Zhang Zhongyu, and mistook him for Huifang. He wrapped his arms tightly around him, endlessly crying out terms of endearment, tugging and pulling at him so violently that the quilt was soon knocked to the floor. He seized Zhongyu's clothing and pulled with all his might, tearing one lined jacket. When his arms grew tired, he pulled at his own trouser cord with such force that it broke. Yet rather than pulling his trousers down, he kept trying to pull them up. The urine-soaked trousers were already thoroughly wet through, soaking the mattress beneath, but they remained stuck. He simply pressed himself tightly against Zhang Zhongyu's back and writhed. Zhongyu awoke feeling as if someone had grabbed him and was violently shaking him. The wine in his stomach surged straight up to his throat, and as he turned his head, he vomited. Pan Qiguan happened to have his mouth wide open at that exact moment, and received a generous, full-face installation, the foul offering delivered directly into all seven orifices. A thick coating accumulated all over Pan's face. He cried out loudly, shaking his head and splattering the filth in all directions. Zhongyu's second wave arrived, this one pure wine, pouring down and washing Pan's face clean, though the stench remained utterly unbearable. Frantic, Pan sat up and wiped his face wildly with his sleeve, cursing the little demon. Hearing this, Zhongyu demanded to know who he was and who he was cursing. Pan Qiguan shouted back, accusing the unbearable little wretch of splattering his own father's face with filth. Zhang Zhongyu flew into a rage, demanding to know who his father was, and shoved with both hands, sending Pan Qiguan rolling off the bed. Zhongyu sat up and furiously asked what wretched bastard dared to curse at him in his own bed. Pan Qiguan yelled back, questioning how the accursed creature dared to curse his own father, calling it an outrage. He scrambled back onto the bed to strike, but just as he did, Zhang Zhongyu was climbing down. They collided, and in the confusion Zhongyu landed a hard slap, sending Pan Qiguan sprawling once again. Zhongyu demanded to know who on earth he was. Pan Qiguan bellowed at the top of his lungs, screaming mutiny and accusing the wretch of striking his own father. He accused him of wanting to sleep with his father while pretending not to recognize him, and demanded to know if he thought Third Master Pan had come there to assault him by force. Zhang Zhongyu paused to think it over, then realized it was indeed Old Third Pan, and asked when he had arrived. Pan Qiguan cursed again, protesting that he was first accused of turning up uninvited after being asked to stay, calling him a death-defying evil wretch, and demanding to know where he had hidden Zhang Zhongyu. Zhongyu scoffed at the incomprehensible scene, declaring that he himself was Zhang Zhongyu.","Pan Qiguan asked what he meant, accusing him of impersonating Zhang Zhongyu to frighten him. The ruckus woke the entire household. The odd-job workers and gatekeepers all came in with lamps, immediately overcome by the heavy reek of wine. Holding up the lights, they saw Zhang Zhongyu standing with the quilt underfoot, and Pan Qiguan seated on the floor, his face mottled with a chaotic splatter of colors, one foot bare, pointing a finger at Zhang Zhongyu. Everyone who saw the sight could not help but burst into laughter as they helped Pan Qiguan to his feet. Zhang Zhongyu stepped over and got a good look at Pan Qiguan, and Pan Qiguan in turn got a good look at Zhang Zhongyu. Each turned his back in disgust and walked away, setting everyone laughing again. When the quilt was picked up, a thoroughly soaked single shoe reeking of urine was discovered underneath. A large dark puddle stained the floor, and the quilt itself was half-ruined. The heated bed looked as devastated as an outhouse. It was a true pity for that fine set of bedding. Pan Qiguan asked where his stockings had gone. A search found one trouser-leg, one stocking, and the bills from the leather account folder scattered all over the middle of the floor. Pan Qiguan paid no attention and simply let the servants pick it all up. Two servants brought two large basins of hot water, and Pan and Zhang washed their faces clean. By now they had both finally sobered up. Pan Qiguan, feeling the crotch of his trousers cold and clammy, put a hand to it and found them thoroughly wet through and unwearable. He took them off, borrowed a pair of single-layer trousers and a pair of shoes from a servant, and put them on. Zhang Zhongyu looked at Pan Qiguan and called the situation remarkable. Pan Qiguan dryly agreed. The two of them thought back over the whole sequence of events from start to finish, and then clapped their hands and laughed for a good while. By now the sky was fully light and the sun had come out. Pan Qiguan then asked where Huifang was hiding. Huifang had, as it turned out, given all the necessary instructions and then slipped out. The odd-job worker explained that the previous night, after the two masters had fallen asleep, a message had come unexpectedly from Young Master Hua, who lived outside the city, summoning Huifang. He added that they dared not refuse Young Master Hua, and feared that he had been taken into the city and might be staying several days before returning.","Zhang Zhongyu remarked that one could hardly blame Huifang for that, as Young Master Hua was not someone to be trifled with. Pan Qiguan had no recourse. He could only bitterly regret the two hundred strings of cash, which had effectively bought him nothing but a face full of vomit from Zhang Zhongyu and a hard slap in the bargain. He had no choice but to bide his time and figure things out later, and the two departed together in a state of gloomy frustration.","On the other side, Huifang and Chunhang rose in the morning, washed their faces, and ate their breakfast. Huifang noticed a qin hanging on the wall. He asked Chunhang if he knew how to play the qin. Chunhang replied that he knew a little. Huifang suggested he play a piece for him to hear. Whether Chunhang played or not, hear it told in the following chapter."]},g=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations13:t},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),n={14:[`Huifang had been asking Chunhang to play the qin, and Chunhang said, "Just a moment." He kept his eyes fixed on Huifang without blinking. Huifang laughed and said, "Can you really not make me out yet? Why do you keep staring so blankly?" Chunhang laughed and replied, "I look at you from every angle and see a hundred different charms. You are like the glow of flowers in morning dew, or the evening sun greeting the breeze. My eyes cannot hold you fast; the more I look, the less I can fully take you in." Huifang clicked his tongue, stood up, and began undoing Chunhang's buttons, helping him out of his robe. Chunhang said, "Let me do it myself, you're not accustomed to this, there's no need to trouble yourself." Huifang opened the clothing bundle and took out a small fur garment, which fit Chunhang perfectly when he put it on. He then had Chunhang change into new boots and a new cap.`,"Huifang smiled as he took up a mirror and held it beside Chunhang, and it reflected back two figures of jade. Chunhang looked at Huifang's image in the mirror. There he was, like a lotus that could speak, like autumn water without a speck of dust. Seeing this, Chunhang gave a slight nod. When he turned his face back, it happened to brush against Huifang's cheek. Huifang tilted his face aside, and half of it flushed crimson.","Chunhang felt his heart stir; he could not resist a wave of strange fragrance that penetrated directly into his nostrils and the innermost chambers of his heart.","His will could no longer hold itself in check, but he urgently pulled his thoughts back: this person is my confidant in times of hardship; how could I allow the slightest impure thought? He steadied his spirit and composed himself. Huifang gave a smile and moved away. Chunhang, now dressed in his new clothes, was once again resplendent in bearing and radiant in spirit, just as he had always been.",'Huifang sat down and idly leafed through the books on the writing desk. He came upon a manuscript of poems, written in a hand halfway between regular and running script, running to several dozen pages, with the title "Travel Drafts from Yantai" (燕台旅稿) inscribed on the cover. Huifang turned at random to a page and found a heptasyllabic ancient-style poem entitled "Annoying the Lord" (恼公), which he began to read aloud in a low voice:',`The curtain hooks click like jade, their sound crystalline;
Cherry blossoms are mirrored in the silver latticed window.
Green cloudy hair tilts to one side, the swallow-tail hairpin falls.
Year after year the brocade characters bloom red on the spring loom.

Huifang said, "A fine poem! This style derives from the 'Thirty-Six Forms' of Wen Tingyun and Li Shangyin, exquisitely lush and delicate." Chunhang replied, "It was written on a whim; I can only approximate their manner, no more." Huifang read on:`,`"Far hills, an inch of blue-green, two eyebrows of kingfisher hue;
Gauze half-dyed with the red of rouge-laden tears.
In the painted beams the swallows fly;
On the mandarin-duck tiles the cat lies asleep."

Huifang said, "What fine craftsmanship, and the rhyme turns crisply. The line about the cat surpasses the one about the swallows, and placed against the mandarin-duck tiles, it becomes all the more fresh and original." He read on:`,`"A waist of drifting smoke, of clasped moonlight, a single foot around.
Star-bright eyes jealous of the spring clouds' proud allure."

Huifang called out "Wonderful!" and added: "'She drew near, and a hundred charms were born; she turned to go, and that autumn glance', even those lines are insufficient to convey this poem's subtlety." Chunhang said, "The image seems to be you." Huifang said, "Am I worthy of it?" He read on:`,`"Jade dragons, slender, coiling in the ring's tracery;
Golden sparrows, paired, flying in the trembling hairpin.
The tender lover, like the paulownia blossom wind,
Draws near her cloudy tresses, yet her body does not stir.
Soft love for fragrant gauze, the mist of fine silk light;
Coy disdain for brocade curtains, the silver hooks too heavy."

Huifang said, "How richly gorgeous, how even and assured. Even I feel tenderness looking at it. But for whom did you write it? If she 'draws near her cloudy tresses yet her body does not stir,' why must you haunt the theater every day?" Chunhang walked over and leaned lightly against the back of Huifang's chair. "Could this person perhaps not be reckoned among the theater folk? Before, I longed to draw near such fragrant grace and could not; now, it seems, my wish is fulfilled." Huifang said, "Who is it? Someone from our troupe?" Chunhang nodded. Huifang said, "Let me think, who does it resemble? The upper couplet, 'a waist of embracing moonlight, star-eyes jealous of clouds', could only describe Yuan Yaoqing. The lower couplet, 'soft love for gauze, coy dislike of heavy curtains', fits none but Jin Shouxiang. Is it those two?" Chunhang shook his head. Huifang said, "Then who is it?" Chunhang said, "There is one person who combines the charms of both, and you simply cannot guess him." Huifang said, "I truly cannot guess; tell me plainly." Chunhang laughed and said, "I'll tell you plainly: it is an empty allegory. If there happens to be someone who resembles him, we may as well say it is that person." Huifang did not press the matter and read on:`,`"Behind painted railings and pearl curtains a dragonfly hangs;
A single peach tree blossoms, graceful as a lovely girl.
Morning after morning she permits the young man to gaze at her flowers,
Only a single pane of glass stands between them."

Huifang closed the volume and pondered a moment. "What a beauty, fair as flowers, bright as the moon. What a fine talent with embroidered lips and a brocade heart. What does 'a dragonfly hangs' refer to? I suspect there is an allusion." Chunhang said, "Li Yishan's (Li Shangyin's) poem: 'At dawn the curtain's strings break a dragonfly's wing; on the gauze screen there is only an empty blue expanse.'" Huifang said, "I have seen that poem. I had momentarily forgotten it. Let me see how you make the transition below." He read on:`,`"Young man, the peach blossom's color rivals my face,
Yet the peach blossom is easily seen, while I am hard to meet.
My beauty will always be like the moon at the second day,
But your heart, I pray, will not follow Wen Sanbian's example."

Huifang said, "It must open this way, only then does what follows have room to breathe. 'Wen Sanbian', is that to say you have changed your heart?" Chunhang replied, "I am drawing on a line from the Preface to the 'Arts and Literature Preface' (艺文序): 'Tang literature changed no fewer than three times.'" Huifang looked at Chunhang and said, "So by that reckoning, you too cannot be counted a man of good conscience." Chunhang said, "How so?" Huifang replied, "Her beauty cannot always remain like 'the moon at the second day,' and so your heart will inevitably arrive at 'Wen Sanbian's three changes.'" Chunhang laughed and said, "When reading poetry, one cannot take it so literally, otherwise the poem becomes a dead thing through and through." Huifang gave a smile and read on:`,`"The gauze curtains lie desolate in the pearlescent chamber;
Musk and dragon-brain incense linger, how one mourns their fragrance.
Thirty-six brocade carp-scales are too hard to convey;
The jade flute blows broken, cloud and sky stretch without end."

Huifang nodded and sighed: "In this life, partings and reunions, joy and sorrow, they are all fixed and certain." He continued reading:`,`"Green embroidered sheng-bag hangs on the eastern wall;
No flowers, no words, spring is desolate and still.
The wronged woman yearns to pluck the Sang-fu zither string;
The palace lady leans in grief upon Yang Fei's flute."

Huifang said, "What a clever parallel. The pairing of 'Sang-fu's zither' and 'Yang Fei's flute' is truly an ingenious oblique correspondence. The upper line naturally alludes to Luo Fu of the 'Luofu on the Mulberry Path.' But this 'Yang Fei's flute' brings to mind Zhang Hu's poem:

'In the small window of the quiet courtyard, unseen by any,
At leisure I take up Prince Ning's jade flute to play.'

And I once read the 'Unofficial Biography of the Noble Consort' (贵妃外传):`,`"The Emperor Xuanzong was dwelling with his brothers when the Consort secretly took Prince Ning's jade flute and played it, and for this she incurred the imperial displeasure. Is that the allusion you are using?" Chunhang said, "That interpretation can stand, but it does not quite fit the phrase 'the palace lady leans in grief.' My source is a passage in the 'Record of Strange Gatherings' (集异记): when the Emperor fled to Shu, on a moonlit night he ascended a tower, and a former attendant of the Precious Consort, Hong Tao, sang the 'Liangzhou Melody' composed by the Consort, while the Emperor took the Consort's jade flute and played along, and when it was done they looked at each other and covered their faces and wept." Huifang nodded, then read on:`,`"The begonia flowers, drunk, fall; butterflies drift away;
Cotton-soft willow catkins, too weak to fly, cling and yearn.
The water at the bottom of the well is like my heart's intent;
The dust on the roadside clings to the garment of my lord."

Huifang felt a pang of sadness, and his expression changed. "Such depth of emotion, such lingering tenderness. What a devoted soul. This must be the closing passage." He read:

"Kingfisher-feather wind-trains drag the phoenix's red tail."

Huifang said, "This line arrives from out of nowhere. The brushwork is bold and startling, and then it opens up again. Does a phoenix have a red tail?" Chunhang said, "Wen Feixing's (Wen Tingyun's) poem has the line: 'The Qin King's daughter rode upon a red-tailed phoenix.'" Huifang continued:

"Astride the phoenix, following my lord for thirty thousand li.
One day, this fragrant heart turns over a hundred times;
At leisure it rises again with the smoke from the censer."

He had just finished reading when Gao Pin walked in. "What a fine poem! Only such a sweet and winsome voice is fit to recite these fragrant and elegant verses. But there is one line in the poem that would be even more apt and fitting with three words changed." Huifang stepped forward. When he saw who it was he said: "Last night I meant to come and pay my respects, but you had already retired." Gao Pin laughed: "By that account, the two of you have already spent a night together." Huifang clicked his tongue: "We talked through the night until just now." Gao Pin said, "Your complexion doesn't look like it." Chunhang said, "Which line of the poem do you think should be changed?" Gao Pin said, "The line that corresponds to 'The water at the bottom of the well is like my heart's intent.'" Huifang looked again at the next line and read: "'The dust on the roadside clings to the garment of my lord'. What is wrong with that?" Gao Pin said, "It is fine, but too vague. It is better to change it to 'The mud before the carriage wheels smears the garment of my lord', for that is vivid and full of flavor." Huifang smiled sweetly. Chunhang said, "Whenever you open your mouth, there is never a single good word." Gao Pin then examined Chunhang from head to toe for a good while and said, "Yesterday I cast a divination: Heaven-Wind Gou, transforming to Mountain-Wind Gu, with Water-Heaven Xu as the mutual hexagram. The line text is rather difficult to interpret." He then recited:

"The field yields one hare;
Going, one meets rain; tarrying in the mud;
Meeting the golden man; encountering the master at the temple;
There is a torn hemp garment; perseverance brings good fortune."

I cannot quite work out the meaning."`,'Huifang listened intently, as if lost in thought. Chunhang laughed: "You cast the divination yourself, yet you cannot interpret it." Gao Pin laughed too.',`Huifang was about to ask Gao Pin's opinion when footsteps were heard outside the window as a shadow passed back and forth. Chunhang asked, "Who is it?" A cough sounded, followed by a reply: "It is I. I have a word to say to Lord Gao." Gao Pin recognized the voice at once and said, "Ah, how delightful!" He stepped out and looked: sure enough, it was Tang the Monk from the temple. Gao Pin asked, "What do you have to say?" Tang the Monk grinned and squeezed himself inside. He greeted Chunhang, then caught sight of Huifang and pressed his palms together: "Amituofo! A bodhisattva has descended to our realm! This little monk failed to come out and welcome you, what a sin, a sin! No wonder there were auspicious clouds and blessed rain all through last night. And this very morning, beside Guanyin in the main shrine, one of the Dragon Lady's bodhisattva attendant figures had gone missing, and here she is!" Huifang, who recognized Tang the Monk, covered his mouth and laughed at this. When Chunhang had first arrived in the capital the previous year, he had gone in pursuit of pleasure and fragrant company, and Tang the Monk had served as his go-between on those occasions, which is why the monk had often come to call on them. Later, finding him vulgar and tiresome, Chunhang had largely stopped associating with him. Gao Pin was on terms of regular banter with him, so he snatched the monk's hat off his head, rubbed his tonsured crown, then said to Huifang: "Meixiang, I'll propose a couplet, you match it." He said:`,`"Like an awl inside a bag, the tip pierces through and emerges." Huifang gave a small smile. Tang the Monk snatched his hat back and put it on, then started, "Lord Gao, you, you, you..." and said nothing more, just grinning. Huifang said, "I have already matched it," and recited: "Like a gourd floating on water, the top is round and bald." Chunhang and Gao Pin both laughed and said, "Wonderfully matched, quick-witted and apt!" Tang the Monk laughed and said, "Many thanks, many thanks! This little monk has the great good fortune of being praised by the bodhisattva, and it didn't even come out to mean what I feared it might." He then drew Gao Pin aside and they exchanged a few words in the courtyard, after which the monk departed. Gao Pin came back in, and the three of them ate together. Huifang wished to listen to Chunhang play the qin, so he retrieved the instrument, loosened the brocade case, placed it on the table, and said, "Play on! Shall we burn incense?" Chunhang replied, "Burning incense is a rather hackneyed convention." Gao Pin said, "With Meixiang's own fragrance already filling the room so richly, incense is surely unnecessary." Huifang pushed Gao Pin aside with a laugh, sat down beside the qin table, and looked on attentively as Chunhang tuned the strings. Gao Pin said, "I understand none of this; it seems to me no different from a cotton-fluffer fluffing cotton. What is there to enjoy?" Huifang replied, "You don't understand; today you are truly a case of playing the qin to an ox." As it happened, Gao Pin had been born in the year of the ox, and he laughed and said, "In that case, please match that line for me." Huifang paid no attention and said off the top of his head, "There is no match." Gao Pin said, "Seeing a hare, one releases the arrow." Huifang paused a moment, then said, "Your Li Yulin is born in the year of the rabbit and he is sixteen this year. Why not go and call that jade rabbit to us?" Chunhang also wanted Gao Pin to summon Yulin, and Gao Pin was delighted at the idea; he immediately sent someone to fetch Yulin, and also ordered several dishes to be prepared.`,'Chunhang sat tuning the qin for a while. The first and third strings were a little flat and could not be tightened properly, so he settled on the Man Shang tuning (慢商). He slackened both the first and third strings by a peg, then adjusted the second, fourth, fifth, sixth, and seventh strings in the standard five-tone method. He played a few passages from "Immortal Chen Tuan Attains the Way" (陈抟得道仙翁). Then, sounding some harmonics, he launched into the suite "Young Gallants Gathering Clients" (结客少年场). Starting with harmonics on the fourth string at the ninth position, he plucked the second string and struck the sixth, plucked the fourth and struck the fifth, producing a qiong-qiong sound for twenty-two notes, and back again to harmonics on the ninth position. The words of the piece ran:',`"On stony-cornered fields a fine bull grazes;
A biting-hoof horse neighs through the flowers, apart."`,"After these four lines, he swept the seventh string open, then the sixth, hooked the fourth, struck the sixth, and hooked the second.","From here on came the solid tones. One saw his left hand's thumb on the second string at the ninth position, vibrating the string twice; then he played five notes, executed a pinch-and-lift ornament of three notes, the middle and index fingers plucking the fourth and sixth strings together while the left thumb hovered over the sixth string at the ninth position. He played five more notes, plucked the seventh and fifth strings together; five more, the fifth and third; five more, the seventh and fifth; five more, the fifth and third. In all some thirty-four notes sounded. The words of the section were:",`"Across the flowers the proud horse knows men well;
The stalwart young man's spirit is true and bold.
Fine-cloud soft shoes of light leather;
A gleaming single-line cap upon his brow.
A lined robe like Su Qin's, a sword at his side;
And wind-and-rain firewood, like Feng Yi."`,'This was the first section. It was measured, with deliberate rhythmic rises and falls, its lingering resonance unforced and natural. He then moved into the second section, plucking and thrusting on the seventh string. The opening was full of vibrato techniques and ascending glides, with many back-and-forth pulls and slides. The fingering was intricate, giving rise to an ardent and impassioned quality. The tenth note brought a double-string pluck, and the fifteenth another. The twenty-third note produced a clear ding-dang of two notes of great beauty, forming a back-lock cadence. It was most lovely to hear. After that came six more notes. The words of this section were: "Elder Brother holds life lightly, his heroic spirit piercing rainbow and sun. Second Brother holds money lightly, fearing the laughter of misers. Little Brother holds power lightly, bending his knee before no king or lord."',"After a brief pause, he launched into the third section, hooking the first string while the left hand's middle finger slid down from the thirteenth position. From there, he hooked the second, then third, then fourth strings at the thirteenth position. The sound suddenly opened up, vast and rich, as the shang mode shaded into gong. After a few more notes, there came three resonant cries on the seventh, sixth, fifth, and third strings, articulating a suoling ornament, the loveliest sound of all. Then, after the thirteenth note, the seventh string rang out langlinging on five or six notes, making a brief suo cadence. the fifth and seventh, then the fourth and sixth strings, were plucked in turn for four notes, and then nine slow notes brought the section to a close. The words were:",`"Achievements that will ring through a thousand autumns,
The spirit for them lies in youth.
Below the age of twenty,
Stand at the head with Elder Brother.
At twenty-four, three-and-twenty more than one,
Second Brother, I stand shoulder to shoulder with you.
In the white light of day I point to the blue sky above,
Pouring out the wine libation, wordless, in silence."

Chunhang made to rise, but Huifang pressed his hand down: "This is just the finest part, please play on." Chunhang said, "The piece is finished." Huifang said, "How can it be over so soon?"`,`Chunhang said, "This suite has only three sections." Huifang said, "Too short, play a longer one." Gao Pin laughed: "Xiangfan, Meixiang finds your piece too fast and too short. You had better go and paste up a placard advertising some thousand-charm, hundred-beauty remedy." Chunhang said, "Nonsense!" Huifang made to pinch Gao Pin's mouth shut; Gao Pin bowed deeply and said, "Grant me pardon this once." This made Huifang burst out laughing. Huifang asked Chunhang to play "Eighteen Songs of a Nomad Flute" (胡笳十八拍) and then "Spring Dawn in the Grotto Heaven" (洞天春晓), saying: "These two suites I have heard Xiao Jingyi play most beautifully; he also has a combined score for qin and xiao flute. He once taught me to play the xiao." Chunhang said, "'Spring Dawn in the Grotto Heaven' is indeed a fine suite, but it is too lengthy. 'Eighteen Songs of a Nomad Flute' does not quite suit my present mood. Better to play 'Narcissus Lyric' (水仙操)." After another pause, he retuned the strings and began to play. The music was clear, cool, and spare. This suite has twelve sections, requiring the most refined fingering, with every vibrato, roll, glide, and press precisely placed, not a hair's-breadth of error permissible.`,'By the fourth and fifth sections, one seemed to see the Spirit of the Xiang River beating her zither, and Feng Yi the River Lord striking his drum. By the sixth and seventh sections, one seemed to see the Xiang Goddess weeping over bamboo, and Lie Zi riding the wind. The music moaned and lamented, full of grievance and longing, of weeping and sighing. Truly it could split sword-strokes and probe the heavens; a cool wind stirred and rustled in through the cracks of the window. Huifang and Gao Pin both sat upright and composed, listening with breath held and expression gathered. Suddenly the seventh string at the second division of the sixth position went flat, the fifth string at the sixth position went sharp, and the fourth string at the ninth position was off by several fractions. Chunhang said, "How strange. Why have the tones suddenly gone out of order?" Gao Pin and Huifang could not detect it. Chunhang tried to retune the strings but could not bring them right, and so he stopped and asked Gao Pin: "Is there anyone in the temple who plays the qin?" Gao Pin said, "The monks may play the huqin fiddle, but no one plays the qin." Chunhang said, "There must be someone who knows the qin listening outside; that is why the string pitches have shifted." He had barely finished speaking when wild laughter broke out in the courtyard, startling Gao Pin and the others.',`Gao Pin hurried out to look, and who should it be but Shi Nanxiang, his left hand clasped around Wang Lanbao's arm and his right hand holding Li Yulin, already a little flushed with wine. Yulin held a sprig of apricot blossom in his hand, and three or four more people followed behind. Gao Pin noticed that his own attendant was in the courtyard, and asked, "Where have you come from?" Nanxiang said, "You asked your young protégé to keep it secret from me, and now you ask me where I've come from? Today I went with Jingfang to Yiyuan; they were all at home and kept me for dinner. Peijian was there too, along with Yaoqing and Shouxiang. After dinner, someone came from Peijian's household to fetch him. Duxiang asked about it and that's how I learned you had summoned them, so I took leave of Duxiang and came along." He pointed at the blossom in Yulin's hand and said, "We were enjoying the apricot blossoms over there today." Then he asked Gao Pin: "Since when do you know how to play the qin? If you want to learn, I'll teach you. That 'Narcissus Lyric' just now was rather well played." Gao Pin said, "When did I ever play? The one playing is Tian Xiangfan." Nanxiang had already heard of Tian Xiangfan's talents from Zhongqing, and said, "Since it is Tian Xiangfan, why does he not come out and meet Shi Zhujun?" Gao Pin said, "Allow me to make the introduction." At that moment Huifang had already come out to greet him, and promptly drew Nanxiang inside. Nanxiang said, "Well! You are here too. I had no idea that Gao Zhuoran's monastic chamber would today become Shi Jilun's Jingu Garden!" Chunhang also came forward to meet him, and the two exchanged the expected words of mutual admiration. Yulin and Lanbao greeted Chunhang as well, then sat down with Huifang. Nanxiang turned to Gao Pin: "Since Zhuoran has summoned the young gentlemen, there must naturally be wine. Don't pretend ignorance, produce it at once." Gao Pin said, "The wine is ready, but it is only the Immortal Peach Life-Extending Pill that I lack." Nanxiang said, "Even if I get drunk, I am unlikely to roll off an upper-story balcony." Everyone laughed.`,`Gao Pin's attendant and the cook brought out wine and dishes. They all sat around a round table. Nanxiang and Chunhang talked further about qin scores and literary arts, each finding much to admire in the other. Gao Pin said, "Today's Shi Zhujun is the Di Renjie of the pear-garden world; Tian Xiangfan is the Li Yaoshi of the theater troupe." Nanxiang said, "You are talking nonsense again." Chunhang said, "How so? I don't quite follow." Gao Pin said, "Zhujun compiled the 'Yantai Flower Selection' (燕台花选), and all these young dan actors became the peach-and-plum of his gates, every rabbit-toadstool and horse-puff ball now a useful ingredient in the medicine chest. Is that not Di Renjie? As for Xiangfan: he spent everything he had, and then last night a certain 'Red Whisk' came eloping with him in the dead of night. Is that not Li Yaoshi?" Everyone laughed, except Huifang, who reddened and said, "For falling off that upper balcony the other day, it would have been better if you had turned into a snapping turtle, or at least broken a leg." Gao Pin laughed: "Tumbling off an upper balcony is still fairly ordinary. What is really frightening is tumbling in the gutter by the theater entrance and being trampled to death by a mule." When Nanxiang asked for the story, Gao Pin told it from beginning to end, and Chunhang was so ashamed he wished the ground would swallow him.`,`Nanxiang burst out laughing too: "Xiangfan is truly a man of rare romantic sentiment! A peerless beauty moved to feeling by a single fall, that is something that has never happened before. Of old we heard that Sun Shou's falling make-up brought Liang Ji down from his horse. Today we see Su Lang performing onstage, and Tian Zi chasing the carriage. A splendid parallel couplet, let me present it to Meixiang." Huifang gave Nanxiang a sidelong glance and said, "Why must you also imitate that sharp-tongued fellow and mock me?" Gao Pin said, "These words are spoken as if you have a deep grudge against me, when in truth I bear you no enmity at all. Why so harsh?" Huifang said, "Say another word and I'll expose your secrets." Gao Pin said, "Go ahead and expose them, I am not afraid." Huifang then recited: "Presiding over banquets and feasting officials; Honored with the bestowal of sable-tipped plumes; Vice-Director of the Guild Hall; Officer in Attendance at the Theater; Proofreader at the Painting and Calligraphy Market; Concurrent Supervisor of the Southern City Streets Office; of all Pear-Garden Musical Troupes; Inspector-General of Intelligence from Every Quarter; Libationer at each venue attended; Scholar Drenched in Perspiration; Grand Counselor of Foreign Affairs; Minister of the Bureau of Music and Song; Hereditary First-Rank Duke of the State of Shi; add an urgent promotion, and one further step in meritorious service: Gao." Everyone burst out laughing.`,`This string of titles had been composed by Liu Wenze; of those present, only Nanxiang knew their origin, while to Chunhang it was entirely new. Gao Pin said, "There is one title you have left out." Huifang said, "It seems there was nothing more." Gao Pin said, "You have omitted Superintendent of the Compilation of the Rabbit Garden Catalogue." Nanxiang laughed again. Huifang paid no attention to this, and poured several cups of wine for Lanbao, Yulin, and the others at the table. Chunhang had met Yulin once before; now he saw that Yulin's grace and charm, though falling a step short of Huifang's, was far superior to the general run of young protégés he had previously admired. He noticed the peach-blushed cheeks, the powdery smoothness, the lotus-face fragrant with life, an altogether different quality of bearing and elegance. He also observed that Yulin seemed to show Gao Pin especially warm regard, so that one could clearly make out the territories each had staked out.`,"Then he noticed Wang Lanbao, the one Shi Nanxiang held in highest favor, and Chunhang felt a touch of awe before him. Willow eyebrows pressed close and green, containing both coquetry and vexation. Phoenix eyes cast sidelong glances that seemed to carry both sentiment and suppressed anger. He went his own way entirely, free and unconstrained. He was seventeen years old and a martial dan (wǔ dàn) actor, trained to a fine standard in boxing and acrobatics. Nanxiang was a man who lived entirely beyond social conventions; when he first met Lanbao there had been several major scenes between them, but afterward they had grown intimate again. Lanbao also understood Nanxiang's temperament and disposition well, and they were in fact most congenially suited. Whenever Nanxiang fell into a drunken frenzy, one glimpse of Lanbao standing before him was enough to bring him to his senses.","At the table that day, only Chunhang could not hold his wine well, and Nanxiang would not hear of excuses. They began finger-guessing contests and drinking forfeits, creating lively chaos all around.","Chunhang kept losing, and after a while refused to drink any more. Nanxiang ordered Lanbao to pour a cup and make Chunhang drink it. Lanbao rose and walked over to Chunhang with the wine; but Huifang, knowing that Chunhang could not hold his drink, leaned forward and drank it from Lanbao's hand for him.",`Lanbao laughed: "What does this have to do with you? Must you step in and act in another's place?" Huifang laughed: "This is what is called borrowing another's cup of wine to wash away one's own sorrows." Lanbao said, "In that case, please drain a few more cups." He poured several full cups and pressed Huifang to drink them. Huifang said, "I have had enough, I know when to stop." Lanbao said, "That is not for you to decide. Have you not heard: 'Between feuding enemies, bare blades cross'?" Nanxiang and Chunhang watched them, while Gao Pin pulled faces at Wang Lanbao, urging him to impose more forfeit wine on Huifang. Li Yulin reclined on one shoulder, smiling languidly. Lanbao also laughed: "Are you truly not going to drink?" Huifang was a little afraid of him, and could only smile along, saying, "Lan-ge, please let me off." Yulin pleaded on Huifang's behalf repeatedly, but Lanbao would not relent, and in the end still imposed one cup on Huifang before the matter was settled. The company drank on for a while longer, when a servant came from Huifang's household to summon him. Huifang went out and asked, "What is the matter? What became of those two drunkards?" The servant replied, "Those two made a scene all night and left early this morning. Just now someone unfamiliar has called, saying he is a Cantonese gentleman named Xi, Xi Eleven. He greatly admires your name and is waiting at home." Huifang said, "What sort of man is he? Don't tell me he is another Pan Qiguan type." The servant said, "By appearances he is quite well-off, and he has four attendants with him, around thirty years old." Huifang said, "Go back and tell him to leave, and say I am not returning today." The servant left.`,`Huifang came back inside, and Chunhang asked what the matter was. Huifang replied, "Someone at home came looking for me; I sent him away." Gao Pin asked, "Who was it?" Huifang said, "I don't know him. The servant said his name was something like Xi Eleven, a Cantonese man." Gao Pin said, "What a cumbersome surname! One stroke sweeping down at the top, three punches gripping at the neck, with tangled silk threads in the middle that will never sort themselves out, and he wants to pass himself off as a great official. Among the nine varieties of Eastern barbarians, I wonder which sort he is." Huifang said, "You would do well to set up a fortune-telling stall at the temple gate." Everyone laughed. Gao Pin said, "Today's drinking is dull without a game. Why not use 'Xi Eleven' as the basis for a forfeit round?" Nanxiang said, "How can 'Xi Eleven' serve as the basis for a round?"`,`Gao Pin said, "The three of us will find the character 'xi' (奚) in the Four Books, starting from the first occurrence and going to the eleventh. Whoever makes an error drinks a number of cups equal to the number of characters in the passage. Those three will serve as our seconds." Chunhang said, "There may not be that many instances of 'xi' in the Four Books." Nanxiang said, "Even if there are, we couldn't get through them all." Gao Pin said, "We'll simply drink a few cups of forfeit; there is no harm in trying. I'll go first." He said: "Xi (奚)." Chunhang said, "You must identify which passage in the Four Books the character comes from." Gao Pin said, "'What does he draw on from the three families', the 'xi' from that passage." Nanxiang said: "'Why should the son... why should the woman...'" Gao Pin said, "That is two passages, meaning two cups of forfeit." Nanxiang said, "Is it not permitted to cite two?" Gao Pin said, "Not permitted." Nanxiang drank. Chunhang continued: "'This thing, for what reason...'" Gao Pin praised him: "Well said!" He then said: "'Since it is so, for what reason...' and also 'The Son of Heaven is august and dignified, for what reason...'" Nanxiang said, "You've penalized others and then fallen into the same trap yourself. Who told you to cite two passages? Moreover this 'xi' is the very same first 'xi' you yourself began with, which deserves double the forfeit: ten cups." Gao Pin said, "Mine were one line of four characters and one of five characters. They are not the same, so why should I be penalized?" Nanxiang said, "You yourself declared it was not permitted to cite two passages. How can you break your own rule?" Hemmed in on all sides, Gao Pin had no choice but to drink five cups, slowly.`,`When it came to Nanxiang's turn, Nanxiang fell silent, unable for the moment to recall anything. Gao Pin said, "I was penalized five cups, let me say it for you." Nanxiang thought a while longer and could not recall a passage, so he drank three cups while Lanbao drank two on his behalf. Gao Pin said: "'This too is governance, for what reason...'" Nanxiang said, "How could I not think of that one!" Chunhang thought for a while and said: "'Yu did not employ Baili Xi...'" Nanxiang slapped the table: "Unfairly penalized! 'The people of Youbi, for what reason...'" Chunhang and Gao Pin both applauded this. It was now Gao Pin's turn to cite the seventh instance, but Chunhang jumped in first: "'To those who serve me, for what reason...'" Nanxiang then pointed at Gao Pin and said: "'In that case with the birds and beasts, for what reason...'"`,`Everyone burst out laughing. Gao Pin said, "You have all earned forfeits. The seventh 'xi' was my turn to cite, so why did you both rush in?" Li Yulin duly poured the forfeit cups. Nanxiang and Chunhang had been so eager to recite that they had quite forgotten about the penalties. Nanxiang drank five cups and Lanbao drank two for him. Chunhang drank three cups and Huifang drank four for him.`,`Gao Pin pressed Nanxiang to cite the eighth 'xi'; Nanxiang said, "You have not yet given the seventh, so you must be penalized." He called upon Lanbao to pour wine. Gao Pin said, "This is outrageous! You both rushed in and said it before me. What am I supposed to say now? And yet you want to penalize me? Where is heaven and conscience?" Li Yulin also spoke up on Gao Pin's behalf, and Nanxiang finally relented. Nanxiang then said: "'To exchange millet for it.' Therefore, 'Master Xu, for what reason...'" Chunhang said, "The ninth is left to me." He thought for a while, then said: "'Compare it with the lesser ritual, for what reason? Compare it with the greater ritual, for what reason?'" Huifang stamped his foot: "Why must you say two lines?" Gao Pin said, "Excellent, nine cups of forfeit." Huifang said, "This can't be right." Gao Pin would not give way; first he imposed five cups on Huifang, then four more on Chunhang. Nanxiang suddenly thought of two more lines and could not resist blurting them out, heedless of the penalties: "'South he marches, the Northern Di resent him. He says: for what reason...' and 'Trust in small things to be trustworthy in great ones, for what reason...'" Lanbao jumped up and said, "Grandfather, even if you love to drink there's no need to drag everyone else in. It was not your turn to speak, why say two lines?" Nanxiang felt a bit regretful himself. Gao Pin said, "There is nothing to discuss, eighteen cups." Nanxiang said, "Eighteen cups is absolutely impossible. I really would need the Immortal Peach Life-Extending Pill for that." Chunhang, Huifang, and Yulin all pleaded on Nanxiang's behalf, and the penalty was reduced to nine cups, which Nanxiang defiantly drained himself. Gao Pin said, "Now for the seventh 'xi', I have thought of it." He said: "'Therefore in his sincerity and delight in him, for what reason...'" And immediately continued: "'Not to supply the four directions with food, the steward said: for what reason...'" Chunhang counted on his fingers and said, "Now you have earned your forfeit. You should have given the tenth, but you gave the eleventh." Gao Pin said, "I was mistaken." He tried again: "'Then all they can do is save themselves from dying, for what reason...'" Nanxiang counted, and again it was only nine. Huifang stood up and insisted on penalizing Gao Pin nineteen cups. Gao Pin refused; Lanbao sided with Huifang and also demanded the full count without reduction. After much pleading by Gao Pin, the penalty was reduced to eleven cups; Yulin drank three on his behalf, and Gao Pin drained eight in a row. Nanxiang thought for a while, traced ten strokes on the table, and said: "'The brave warrior does not forget that he may lose his head, Confucius, for what reason...'" It was now Chunhang's turn; he also thought for quite a while, then said: "'Zilu lodged at the Stone Gate; the gatekeeper said: for what reason...'" Gao Pin said, "Retribution comes swiftly, ten cups of forfeit. You were supposed to give the eleventh." Chunhang thought, and realized he had indeed made an error. Huifang stepped in: "You must consider each person's capacity, and not just blindly impose penalties." Gao Pin said, "The drinking-game rule is as strict as military law; it must be applied without partiality." Huifang said, "We'll note it down and drink tomorrow." Gao Pin said, "Your promissory notes may be settled tomorrow, but the wine cannot wait until tomorrow." Yulin said, "Let's split it in half and drink five cups." Huifang drank three more on Chunhang's behalf, and Chunhang forced down the remaining two. It was now Gao Pin's turn to close out the round; he thought for a moment and said: "'Of old, Zhao Jianzi sent Wang Liang as driver for his favorite, Bi Xi...'", and with that the round was complete. Everyone looked at one another and smiled. By now it was well past the second watch of the night; they ate, and the party prepared to disperse. Huifang's carriage had been waiting a long time; he at once took leave of everyone and headed home first.`,"Nanxiang first saw Lanbao home, then escorted Li Yulin to his gate.",`Yulin invited him to come inside; Nanxiang said, "It is already late, let us meet again another day." He turned and went straight home. Passing by Wang Xun's gate, Nanxiang suddenly felt thirsty, and sent his attendant inside to ask whether young Master Wang had yet retired. The attendant went to the gatekeeper and passed on the message; the gatekeeper looked in on the study and found that Wang Xun and Yan Zhongqing had not yet gone to sleep. The gatekeeper reported back, and Wang Xun told them to invite him in. Shi Nanxiang entered. As for what happened next, let the following chapter tell the tale.`]},c=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations14:n},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),o={15:[`When Shi Nanxiang entered and greeted Zhongqing and Wang Xun, they shared a few cups of tea. Wang Xun asked him where he had been, and Nanxiang recounted the day's events one by one, then spoke at some length about Chunhang and Huifang. Wang Xun and Zhongqing found themselves unable to conceal their profound admiration. Zhongqing said, "Who would have thought Su Meixiang capable of this? Henceforth, Tian Xiangfan may truly shed his past and turn over a new leaf." He then recounted the episode of the painted admonition a few days earlier, and mentioned that Chunhang had still shown some resentment. Nanxiang said, "On another day, I shall offer myself as mediator between them. What do you say?" He also asked after Ziyu. Zhongqing said, "Gengxiang was here during the day. His tutor Master Li was appointed to the post of county magistrate in Anhui at the beginning of the month and is about to set off." After a few more words, Nanxiang took his leave and went home.`,"Now let us speak of Ziyu, who had been at Wang Xun's for a long chat before returning home. Master Li had already packed up his tutoring post and needed to arrange travel funds; Wei Pincai had found a financial connection for him, asking Zhang Zhongyu to borrow a draft of silver from Xi Rong, which included a measure of empty credit amounting to over two hundred taels. With this, Master Li supplemented his clothing and also hired young protégés for several days. Li Yuanmao wished to register his household registration in the capital; Xingquan had no choice but to let him do as he wished.",`That evening, Ziyu and Pincai were chatting in the study. That day was a memorial anniversary, so Pincai had gone alone to Cherry Blossom Lane (Yingtao Xiang) during the day and found Ye Maolin. The two talked for a long while, and Pincai treated him to a meal at the Flat-Dumpling Restaurant; they then went together to call on various young dan actors, visiting several tea parlors in a row. At the last stop they went to Qinyan's place; Qinyan actually came out, exchanged a few words with Pincai, and asked after Ziyu. Pincai dressed up Ziyu's feelings for him a little more vividly, and spoke in such a way that Qinyan was genuinely moved. He also arranged with Qinyan to bring Ziyu to meet him the following day. Returning home, he told Ziyu all of this, and Ziyu found himself with yet another thing to weigh on his heart, and lay awake all night. That evening, Shixie was on night duty in the Board of Rites and had not come home. The next day, Ziyu was just making plans to go with Pincai to see Qinyan when suddenly Mei Jin, the gatekeeper, came in with a face full of smiles and announced: "Congratulations to the young master — the master has been appointed Jiangxi Education Commissioner, and the messenger reporting the good news is at the gate now." Ziyu felt pleased at this news; he went with Mei Jin inside to report it to Lady Yan, whose delight goes without saying. Li Xingquan, together with Yuanmao and Pincai, went upstairs to offer their congratulations. Before long, Shixie returned home; colleagues and relatives and friends came calling one after another, and they were kept busy for several days on end. Then came the date of departure for Master Li's new post; Shixie also hosted a farewell banquet for the teacher. On the day of departure, Ziyu went with Yuanmao and Pincai to see Master Li off thirty-five li outside the city to the inn where he would spend the night. Xingquan gave him a thorough send-off, then admonished Yuanmao, "Although Gengxiang is younger than you in years, his learning far surpasses yours. You must in future ask him with humility." Yuanmao assented again and again. Xingquan then said to Pincai: "My son originally came out of the capital together with your brother. I see that in future he will accomplish nothing; I rely entirely upon you to look after him." Pincai also agreed repeatedly. Ziyu, by nature devoted and affectionate, lingered reluctantly at parting; he could not bear to say goodbye, but was forced at last to see the teacher off with Yuanmao, shedding tears as he and Pincai bade farewell.`,`Shixie had also chosen the tenth of the third month for his own departure, and today was already the fifth. Lady Yan said to Shixie: "At the New Year, Madam Sun acted as matchmaker and a verbal agreement was reached with Cousin Wang's wife for the second daughter, with a jade hairpin as a token of betrothal. Now you are going far away again. We must perform some formal ceremony; one cannot simply leave it at that, or people will start to grumble." Shixie laughed: "Without you to remind me, I would have entirely forgotten. It is certainly necessary — I'll ask Sun Bojing to act as matchmaker tomorrow." While they were still talking, Sun Lianggong came to pay a visit. Shixie received him and, after asking about Sun's planned travel date, brought up his wife's thoughts on the matter, saying: "At New Year we arranged an engagement with the Wang family. That was all conducted by the women among themselves; still, we must perform a proper ceremony to make it a respectable arrangement. Moreover, you will be away for three years before you return, and it is not right to leave things so hurried and casual." Shixie said, "We were just discussing this very matter, and had been planning to ask you for your help. Tomorrow I shall first send over a formal letter; the full ceremony can wait until I return." Lianggong agreed.`,"The next day, Lady Yan prepared decorative gift boxes with formal betrothal documents and asked Lianggong to convey them. The return gifts from the Wen Hui household were lavish; Yan Zhongqing helped Lianggong bring them back, and Shixie hosted a wine reception. That day no outside guests were invited; only Pincai and Yuanmao were asked to keep the company. This Li Yuanmao had, at this auspicious moment, achieved a sudden clarity of mind — his speech became quite articulate. When Xingquan left the capital, he had left two hundred taels of silver for Yuanmao; Yuanmao purchased several items of clothing and was now presentable from head to toe. Lianggong's discernment was ordinarily rather limited, but now, seeing Yuanmao's broad face and full features radiating an air of scholarly refinement, he pronounced him a man of promise and was very inclined to consider him further. He inquired about Yuanmao's marriage prospects. Zhongqing had already seen through the situation and praised Yuanmao effusively. Li Yuanmao himself was unaware of what was afoot and was thoroughly gratified, feeling genuinely grateful toward Zhongqing. But let us leave this thread for now.","Now, Ziyu found little enjoyment at home; while the others were drinking, he took Yun'er along and went to find Liu Wenze and Shi Nanxiang.",`He went first to Wenze's house, but Wenze was not at home. He went on to find Nanxiang, and just as he arrived Wenze's carriage also pulled up at Nanxiang's gate. Ziyu said, "I was just looking for you." Wenze said, "I missed calling on you earlier. I went to find Feng Zipei, but he had just gone into the city." So saying, they went in together and sat down in Nanxiang's study. Long'er, the attendant who served Nanxiang, brought tea and said, "Our young master hasn't got up yet!" He then went back inside; about the time it takes to drink a cup of tea, Nanxiang appeared, bareheaded and barefoot, wrapped in a woman's padded jacket. "You've come early," he said. Ziyu laughed at the sight: "I've already eaten before coming." Wenze said, "What a fine figure — wearing your wife's clothes out here. Don't tell me your wife hasn't got up either?" Nanxiang said, "She's been up for a long while. I had just woken from sleep; hearing that you two had come, I didn't have time to dress properly and just grabbed whatever was at hand." Long'er brought in water for washing his face, and Hu'er brought out his clothes, boots, and cap. Nanxiang washed his face and slowly dressed and put on his cap, then with a cheerful smile made a bow to Ziyu and said: "Congratulations, congratulations! You've been hiding from us such a fine attachment." Ziyu assumed he was speaking of his betrothal and became flustered. Wenze said, "What attachment is this?" Nanxiang said, "The other day I was at Duxiang's place; he told me there is a young dan actor called Du Yunong — the finest name actor of all ages past and present — and that Gengxiang has taken exclusive possession of him. They even performed a scene from 'Fixing Affection' (定情) at Yiyuan together." Wenze said, "Who is this Du Yunong? How is it we've never seen him?" Nanxiang said, "Splendid! According to Duxiang and Jingyi's assessment, he surpasses Baozhu — yet I don't know him. Gengxiang, why not take us today to have a look?" Only now did Ziyu understand that this was not about his betrothal; but still, this was his first foray out of the proverbial thicket, nothing like these worldly habitués of song halls and feast stages, and he was too flustered to reply. After Wenze pressed him repeatedly, he had to answer: "This Yunong is actually Qinyan — whom you've all seen before." Wenze said, "It's a grave injustice — we not only claim never to have seen him, we've never even heard his name." Ziyu said, "How is it an injustice? Have you forgotten that young dan actor who performed 'Waking from a Dream' (惊梦) at the Suzhou Guild Hall on the sixth day of the first month?" Wenze thought for a moment: "Yes, yes, that's right! In that case you deserve all the more to be penalized. That day the two of you had eyes only for each other, heart illumining heart — anyone could see it. I asked you then, and you still insisted you had no acquaintance with him whatsoever — how can you deceive people like this! Today there is nothing else for it — you must take us there, and you can hardly say now that you don't know him." Nanxiang burst out laughing: "Acknowledging a young dan actor is not such a great offense against propriety. Gengxiang truly has the manner of a girl in a secluded inner chamber peering around a folding screen. Today we'll see how you manage to wriggle out of this — quickly, let's go! We'll eat there today." After being laughed at and teased in this fashion, Ziyu found it impossible to explain himself, and could only blush awkwardly and say: "Whatever you say — I have nothing of the sort, and besides, I don't know where he lives." Nanxiang said, "You're lying again." Wenze said, "As for that particular person, I have made some inquiries. I only know he is called Qinguan (Qinyan), that he is the newly purchased disciple of Cao Changqing, and that he lives at Qiushui Studio in Cherry Blossom Lane." Nanxiang said, "Let's go!" He called out to Long'er to have the carriage brought round. Ziyu said, "I am not going." Nanxiang said, "Very well, very well! Now that you have a beloved, you no longer need your friends — you intend to enjoy yourself all alone." He pulled Ziyu into the carriage, and they set off directly for Qinyan's quarters in Cherry Blossom Lane.`,`Wenze's attendant went inside to inquire; Qinyan was not at home — they heard from within that the Lord Liu had taken him to Chunxi Garden. Wenze was somewhat put out; Ziyu, on the other hand, felt quietly relieved. Nanxiang said, "Where to now? I haven't eaten yet — isn't that Miaooxiang Hall, where Sulan lives, right across the lane? Let's go and find Xangwan." Wenze said, "I'm not sure he'll be at home either. Send someone to ask." As it happened, Sulan was indeed at home; someone came out from inside and invited them in. They sat down in the reception room and were served tea. Wenze asked Ziyu: "Have you met Xiangwan before?" Ziyu said, "No, I haven't."`,`Nanxiang said, "This gentleman's elegance and charm stands alongside Yuan and Su as one of the three pillars of the pear garden." Before long, Sulan came out, greeted Nanxiang and Wenze, and was introduced to Ziyu. Sulan looked Ziyu over carefully and then asked Wenze: "Is this young gentleman surnamed Mei?" Wenze said to Ziyu, "Another lie exposed — you just claimed not to know him, yet he already knows you. How can that be?" Ziyu was genuinely puzzled and had no idea how to answer; it was Sulan who clarified: "I don't know him at all — I simply guessed." Nanxiang said, "I don't believe it — how could anyone guess so precisely? If you can guess his name, I'll take you for a divine being." Sulan said, "His name contains the character 'yu' (玉), and his courtesy name is Gengxiang — am I right?" Nanxiang and Wenze burst out laughing:`,`"This proves it — and you still claim not to know him! We thought Gengxiang was a man of perfect sincerity, and now it turns out he is quite accomplished at telling lies." With these words Ziyu's cheeks flushed slightly; the injustice of it was such that he had no one to whom he could complain. He looked closely at Sulan's features and noticed a certain resemblance to himself; fearing that Nanxiang and Wenze might notice and make sport of it, he moved away and went to look at the calligraphy and paintings on the nearby wall. Nanxiang said to Wenze:`,`"Can you see who Xiangwan resembles?" Wenze said, "He resembles Gengxiang. The very first time I saw Gengxiang, I was going to say so, but because he looked so young, I held back." Ziyu pretended not to hear and let them talk among themselves. Sulan said, "Don't make sport of him — how could you compare me to him?" He laughed and drew Ziyu over to sit beside him. Nanxiang said, "We haven't eaten yet — please bring food quickly." Sulan at once ordered the kitchen to prepare a meal. Though Ziyu had seen Sulan's performance of "Dancing on the Tray" before, on that occasion he had been entirely preoccupied with Qinyan and had not paid Sulan any attention. Now, seeing Sulan before him — refined as orchid and iris, [radiant] as peach and plum, out of the purest clarity blooming the most vivid brightness — Ziyu was struck with admiration. He was seventeen years of age, wearing a padded jacket of lotus-pink crepe silk; his starlike eyes cast their gaze downward, his fragrant cheeks just barely parted — truly one who could bewitch the soul and stir the spirit. Ziyu secretly praised him greatly: he was in no way inferior to Qinyan or Baozhu. He only wondered what his character and temperament might be. The food and wine were already being brought in from outside; the three sat down without ceremony and helped themselves. Sulan poured wine and said to Ziyu: "This is your first visit — I must offer the first cup to you." Ziyu accepted it.`,`He then poured cups for Nanxiang and Wenze. Wenze asked: "Are you not going to the theater today?" Sulan said, "There is no role for me today, so I may stay away." Seeing Sulan's quiet, serene, and composed manner, Ziyu felt a deep affinity in his heart. Sulan raised his eyes and, noticing that Ziyu kept stealing glances at him, broke into an involuntary smile, from which wafted a quiet romantic sensibility and languid charm. Ziyu dropped his gaze. Wenze laughed: "Coming out with Gengxiang, we are at a disadvantage in so many ways." Ziyu did not understand. Wenze laughed: "With you here, it is like the night we went to see the lanterns: the young ladies in their carriages all had eyes only for you, none for the rest of us. Is that not putting us at a disadvantage?" These words made Ziyu's cheeks flush crimson. He said, "I'm sure I don't know what there is to admire." Sulan turned to Nanxiang and said, "I am particularly fond of that 'Slow Magnolia Blossoms' lyric you wrote to accompany my painted orchid." Nanxiang said, "Your own lyrics have gotten considerably better of late." Sulan suddenly fixed his gaze on Ziyu with a faraway expression, as if in thought. Wenze caught this and said to Sulan, "Are you taken with him?" Sulan smiled again. Ziyu felt embarrassed and could not sit still or stand easy. Sulan said to Ziyu, "Have you seen your beloved today?" Ziyu could not work out who was meant. "Which one do you mean?" Sulan said, "I only know of one for you — I have no idea how many there may be." Ziyu was even more puzzled. Nanxiang and Wenze could not guess either, and both asked: "Who is this beloved of Gengxiang's that you speak of?" Sulan said, "His beloved comes to see me every single day. He lives right across the lane. How is it you have not called, passing his door? Go and invite him here." Only then did Ziyu understand that it was Qinyan. His heart misgave him: "How is it they all know about this?" Wenze said, "You see — even Gengxiang's beloved is known to him. Clearly you two are on terms of deep friendship." Nanxiang said, "We went first to the other side of the lane — Qinyan was not at home, so we came here." Sulan said, "So it is because he was not home that you came over here." Ziyu, hearing this, felt somewhat uncomfortable in his conscience and was just about to say something when Wenze cut in: "Coming from that direction, we naturally passed his gate first and would inquire there before coming here. It is the logical order, from near to far." Sulan said, "I don't blame any of you, nor need you make smooth excuses. Let me tell you the truth: I have not actually met Gengxiang even once — everything Yunong told me. This Yunong naturally gets along very well with me; from the seventh day of the first month onward, he has come every single day to have long conversations with me, and we have become the closest of friends. Lately he often slips up and calls me by the wrong name — calling me Gengxiang." Ziyu, hearing this, already sensed what Qinyan must be feeling; he felt a pang in his heart, and quietly composed himself, waiting for Sulan to continue. Wenze pointed at Ziyu and said, "He is Gengxiang. Why does Qinyan call you Gengxiang?" Nanxiang said, "Do you even need to ask? Can't you work out the reason?" Wenze fell silent and listened as Sulan continued: "How was I to know that he was called Gengxiang? At first I paid no attention to it; but after he kept calling me by the wrong name, I pressed him, and he refused to say.`,`"One day Yaoqing was here, and I brought it up with him; Yaoqing then laid out your situation in full. After that, Yunong himself admitted it: 'I am somewhat like you. To see me is to see you.' So it is for that reason that he comes here so often. It is not that he is genuinely drawn to me as a friend; he is merely using me as a painted portrait, a small likeness. Would you say this is a deep feeling or not? He has put in all this effort for you, how rare. That you should come today is fortunate; I have told you all this clearly on his behalf, so that you may know and his devotion should not be wasted." Ziyu, hearing all this, was like a mute who has swallowed bitter herbs, unable to express the bitterness. The moisture in the corners of his eyes he could only force back down into his chest. Wenze and Nanxiang nodded repeatedly: "This is truly exceptional." Wenze added: "Yunong's feeling for Gengxiang must amount to twenty-four parts deep. I wonder what Gengxiang's feeling for Yunong is? Do you know?" Sulan said, "How could I not know? That too was told to me by Yaoqing." He then recounted the whole episode of Xu Ziyun testing Ziyu with the false Qinyan, and Nanxiang and Wenze laughed and praised it by turns. Ziyu found this all very difficult to bear, and could only say: "Such small matters, once they pass through others' mouths, they sprout a hundred embellishments." Sulan then sent someone to ask again after Qinyan; he still had not returned. They finished the meal and talked idly for a while. Ziyu asked Sulan for a sample of his calligraphy. Sulan said, "I have nothing done already." He went inside, and after a short while returned with a bamboo-paper folding fan of mottled Xiang-consort bamboo and presented it with both hands: "This is what I've just written. Please accept it as a trifling gift; it is poorly done and not worth viewing." Ziyu looked: the brushwork was like iron drawings and silver hooks, round as pearls and smooth as jade, rich with an antiquarian elegance, the seals also classically refined. Ziyu made a deep bow in thanks. They talked and chatted on until it was nearly the end of the申 hour (around 4 p.m.), and Ziyu prepared to leave; Nanxiang and Wenze departed with him. Sulan saw them to the main gate; they each mounted their carriages and left.`,`Now let us speak of Sun Lianggong, who returned home and discussed with Madam Lu the idea of betrothing his eldest daughter to Yuanmao. Madam Lu laughed coldly several times without saying a word; Lianggong did not dare press her further. Nevertheless, his mind was made up, and the next day he went to ask Wang Wenhui to act as matchmaker. Wenhui hesitated at length, thinking to himself: "How can this pallid creature be given in marriage?" But then he thought again: "That Li Yuanmao is no great match either. He is a dull-headed fellow if ever there was one; the day he bowed to me, he knocked my hat askew with his head. They'll suit each other perfectly." So he agreed. He went specifically to Shixie's residence to convey Lianggong's intentions. Shixie was greatly pleased; he called Pincai and Yuanmao out, Pincai naturally gave his wholehearted approval, and Yuanmao was thoroughly delighted. Shixie then wrote on Yuanmao's behalf a letter formally requesting consent, which he handed to Wenhui; on the sixth day the betrothal letter was conveyed. This was, in truth, a match woven across a thousand li, bound by threads of destiny spun a hundred years in advance. Li Yuanmao, that blockhead, could not wait until the very next day to move into the family — only that would relieve the misery of his chronic empty purse.`,`On the tenth day, Zhongqing and Wang Xun came very early to see Shixie off. The Academician Mei's luggage had long been properly packed and put in order; his servants had been dispatched out of the city on the ninth to transport everything. On the day itself, relatives and friends crowded the house. As it was still early, Zhongqing and Wang Xun waited first in the study with Ziyu, Yuanmao, and the others. Zhongqing offered Yuanmao his congratulations, saying: "Congratulations, congratulations! Today you have truly obtained a 'snow beauty.' Did you not once write a line of poetry declaring, "The white one's eyes draw near"? Now it has come true as a poetic prophecy." Yuanmao did not understand but was rather pleased with himself.`,"Shortly afterward, Shixie finished seeing off other guests and called Ziyu out to deliver a thorough admonition. He then also gave a few words of instruction to Yuanmao and Pincai. After that he bade farewell to Lady Yan, mounted his carriage, and departed. Yan Zhongqing, Wang Xun, Wei Pincai, and Li Yuanmao followed close behind. Lady Yan, leading Ziyu and a group of female servants and maids, also accompanied him in carriages out of the city. Outside the city gate, Wang Wenhui, Sun Lianggong, and a dozen or so fellow examination graduates and close friends were all gathered at a roadside inn to host a farewell feast. Shixie lingered for a while; Wenhui and the others then reentered the city. As the sky was growing late, Lady Yan could only take the female servants and maids back first, in tears. Ziyu, Zhongqing, Pincai, Yuanmao, and some servants stayed the night at the inn; they paid their final respects the following morning.","Shixie offered a few more words of encouragement to Ziyu; Ziyu had no choice but to return weeping with Zhongqing and the others. But let us leave that aside.","That day Xu Ziyun had also been at the roadside inn for the farewell; rather than returning directly to his residence, he went straight to the garden and on to Cixian's room — where he learned that Cixian was in the Peach Blossom Grove (Taohua Wu) enjoying the peach blossoms, along with Baozhu and Shufang. Ziyun came to the grove at once. Though it was within his own garden, he could not wander through it every day; in just a few days, the peach blossoms had opened in full — blazing like morning radiance, reflected in a clear expanse of water, with the grassy banks embroidered like brocade. It truly felt as though the full splendor of spring had arrived. He entered through the third gate and found, within the winding covered pavilion, Cixian, Baozhu, and Shufang drinking and enjoying the blossoms. Seeing Ziyun come, Baozhu and Shufang rose to welcome him, and Cixian too greeted him with a smiling face.",`Ziyun laughed: "Jingyi, today you've entirely left me out of your pleasures." Cixian said, "Knowing you would return early today, I left a seat waiting for you." Shufang said, "Did you not notice there are four places set?" Ziyun sat down across from Cixian.`,`Cixian asked: "Were there many people at the farewell today?" Ziyun said, "Quite a few — Gengxiang and Jiantan went to see him off to the first inn overnight, so they will not be back until tomorrow." He then pointed with a smile at Baozhu: "I dare say none of those in their party were here to see him off; I suspect this elder gentleman has never once invited any of them throughout his life." Baozhu laughed: "This Lord Mei, whenever there was a theater feast, would summon us to attend on a number of occasions. His manner was modest and refined — he simply never once exchanged a single word with us. Little did we expect him to produce such a romantic-hearted son. This Mei Gengxiang was actually at Xiangwan's place for dinner the other day, and then went over to Yunong's place without finding him. According to Xiangwan, the depth of his feeling for Yunong is truly unparalleled." Ziyun said, "How do you know he went to find Yunong? Did he go alone?" Baozhu said, "It was Xiangwan who told me. He went with Zhujun and Qianzhou together; Xiangwan also gave him a fan. They were well suited to each other." Cixian said, "I think those two — Gengxiang and Yunong — are truly what one might call 'minds focused and undivided, their spirit crystallized in each other.'`,`Now that these two have found each other, all manner of trials and sorrows will surely come to them in future — just watch them slowly unfold." The four of them drank for a while and enjoyed the blossoms. Cixian said to Baozhu: "Those sixteen drinking-game lines that Duxiang had engraved — have you seen them?" Baozhu said, "Of course we have." Ziyun said, "Why don't you two today compose a few rounds of your own in the same vein? It would give us a chance to see what your minds can produce." Before Baozhu could answer, Shufang said: "I'm afraid we won't be able to manage it — in the first place our wits are not nimble enough, and in the second, we are not very familiar with the Tang poetry or the Classic of Poetry (The Classic of Poetry), so how could we produce anything as perfectly synchronized as those? Unless we went home and spread out several books and searched out suitable lines one by one — only that way might we piece something together." Baozhu said, "We are truly ashamed of ourselves. Those young ladies are roughly the same age as us — how can their minds be so brilliant and their mouths so fragrant, while we are so dull?" Ziyun said, "Try a round today — I guarantee you'll do well." He called for a set of dice; a servant fetched a set in a basin and placed it on the table. Ziyun asked Baozhu to throw first; Baozhu still demurred, but after Ziyun and Cixian pressed her, she had no choice but to say: "Why make us a laughingstock? Not only can I not remember other things clearly, even the tune-pattern names (qu pai ming) are only vaguely in my memory. Perhaps Gengxiang might manage, but I am sure to do it poorly." She threw the dice several times until she got a combination whose name was "The Shadows of Green, the Scarceness of Red" (绿暗红稀). She sat blankly thinking for a while; unable to come up with anything, she said:`,`"This is nothing but inviting vexation!" Shufang said, "Let me throw to see what pattern I get before I start thinking." He too threw several times before getting "Su Qin Turns His Back on His Sword" (苏秦背剑). He said, "This is even harder." Then Baozhu was seen asking Cixian: "There is a line in the Classic of Poetry about "sighing without end." I can't recall it exactly." Cixian said:

"'Whenever I have good companions,
All the more do I sigh without end.'"

Baozhu said, "Good, that's one. Only it's not the most apt." Ziyun said, "Go ahead and say it." Baozhu recited:

"The shadows of green, the scarceness of red,
Good dreams are rare to find again.
In the twilight upon your powder-tower the apricot blossoms are spent.
Too weary to paint the brows,
All the more do I sigh without end."`,`Cixian and Ziyun both praised her: "Very well said, and so coherent for the first one! Truly exceptional. The one line from the Classic of Poetry is slightly less apt, but it still works." Baozhu said, "The Classic of Poetry is genuinely difficult to fit rhythmically, and with the rhyme scheme adding another constraint, it gets even harder." Shufang said, "What I've thought up is even less polished. Is there not a line in the Classic of Poetry that goes 'None would look to me'?" Ziyun said, "There is. Say it quickly." Shufang was about to recite, then hesitated, feeling a touch of shyness. Cixian urged again, and at last Shufang recited:

"Su Qin turns his back on his sword.
Do not submit a memorial to the northern palace.
It is you who have misled these Three Academicians of the Hall of Jade and the Terrace of Gold.
It is the wrong path.
None would look to me."`,`Ziyun said, "This is excellently done. Every phrase fits in perfect cadence." Cixian said, "It really is a remarkable achievement." Baozhu said, "His is better than mine. It leaves mine looking rather mediocre." Yet slight color rose in both cheeks, and there was clear chagrin in his expression. Ziyun offered comfort: "Yours is fine too. Only your topic was broader and more general, making it harder to achieve the precision of a tight fit. His topic of 'Su Qin Turns His Back on His Sword' is inherently more favorable, so his piece more easily shows to advantage over yours." Somewhat consoled by these words, Baozhu relaxed a little. He then threw again and got "A Purple Swallow Threads the Curtain" (紫燕穿帘). He said, "This is a fine topic." He thought carefully for a good while, then asked Ziyun: "I seem to recall a line, "by the embroidered window, sad and unable to sleep". Is it a poem or a lyric?" Ziyun said, "It is a poem by Han Wo." Baozhu said, "This might work a little better." He then recited:

"A purple swallow threads the curtain.
By the embroidered window, sad and unable to sleep.
Lingering slowly,
Until at last standing before the lattice door.
All around is still.
Cherished but unseen."`,`Ziyun and the others praised it greatly. Shufang said, "Do you know what this "all around is still, cherished but unseen" of his is really about?" Cixian smiled: "There is a deep charm of spring longing and yearning for someone." Ziyun also laughed. Shufang laughed: "That's not it. Yesterday a Qin Jiliao (talking myna bird) flew away from him. Yesterday I went to his place just as he came dashing out of his room in a panic, looking in every direction.`,`He asked me, "Have you seen it?" His manner just now in reciting that passage was exactly like his frantic demeanor yesterday." Baozhu laughed too: "Today it came back again." Shufang threw again and got "Tang poetry, Butterflies Fill the Blossoming Branches" (花开蝶满枝). Shufang thought for a while, then said:

"Butterflies fill the blossoming branches.
This is the season of my breaking heart.
I am the Five-Pestilence Emissary who scatters lovesickness.
Butterfly Loves Flowers (蝶恋花).
The spring days pass slowly, slowly."

Cixian and the others praised it greatly: "This one is even better." Baozhu said, "His are always better than mine — both of my rounds today were not as good as his." He threw again and got "Smashing the Brocade Screen" (打破锦屏风). He said, "This is a fine topic — and yet extremely difficult. One must center the thinking on the phrase 'smashing through'; if one can make it cohere, it will be a fine piece indeed." Shufang said, "This is the hard one — I could never piece it together, and I fear the line from the Classic of Poetry alone would be no easy matter." Baozhu sat in rapt thought: he thought of a Tang poem line but could not attach it to the The Western Chamber (The Western Chamber); he thought of a line from the Western Chamber but could not attach it to the Classic of Poetry. The anxiety was considerable. After a long while, he asked: "Is there not a line in the Classic of Poetry that goes 'whereby to pierce through my wall'?" Cixian said, "Perfect — that line is already secured; the trick now is to make the middle part connect smoothly." A pleased expression came over Baozhu's face, and he recited with relish:`,`"Smashing the brocade screen.
The twilight shadows fill the latticed chamber.
Jing-dong-dang, the curtain rings clatter.
The moon is high.
Whereby to pierce through my wall."

Ziyun and the others praised it highly. Ziyun said, "This is truly excellent. Even among those sixteen forfeits, it would rank in the top tier. Let us drink three cups in congratulation." Only then did Baozhu truly smile with satisfaction. Shufang began to feel anxious again, fearing that another round would leave him unable to match this, and said: "Let's leave it here. It's really quite taxing on the mind. I won't throw anymore." Ziyun said, "This game is indeed taxing, but he has completed three rounds and you only two. Throw one more." Shufang said:`,`"What if the dice pattern comes up again as a repeat?" Cixian said, "A repeat doesn't count. It must be a new one to hold any interest." Shufang had no choice and threw several repeated patterns before finally getting "Chu and Han Contend for Supremacy" (楚汉争锋). He said, "Now that I've thrown this, I'll call it the last one." Ziyun agreed. Shufang composed himself in thought and walked off alone among the peach blossom thicket. Ziyun and the others also strolled through the flowers. After a while Shufang said: "I have thought of something — only this line from the Tang poetry still feels a little forced. But if I drop that line, I can't find another, so I'll have to make do." He recited:

"Chu and Han contend for supremacy.
The Hegemon King is divinely martial.
You who lend your divine might, beat the drums three times.
Urgently three strikes.
A hundred men's match."

Everyone praised it.`,`Ziyun said, "Today we have produced six more, bringing our total to twenty-two. It would be wonderful if we could eventually collect a full hundred." Cixian said, "A hundred is not attainable. Besides, there are not that many bone-tile names (gǔ pái míng). Tune-pattern names (qǔ pái míng) are more than sufficient. It would be better to drop the bone-tile names and replace them with something else; that way, a hundred might be achievable. If we keep the bone-tile names, the useful ones amount to only fifty or sixty. Among them there are a few with delightful names that one never manages to throw — such as "Grandfather Leading Grandchildren" (公领孙), "Zhong Kui Binds His Forehead" (锺馗抹额), "Greedy Flower Not Full" (贪花不满), and "Thirty Bald-Clawed Dragon" (三十秃爪龙). If one could set those to verse, they would surely produce wonderful lines. As for restricting the source to the Western Chamber, that is also a bit narrow. Better to allow any line from a dramatic libretto (qǔ wén), which broadens the scope considerably. Only 'Knocking Over the Oil-Pot Lid' (推倒油瓶盖) would be somewhat harder." Ziyun said, "From the Classic of Poetry there is "the jar is emptied", which could work; and for the tune-pattern name we could use 'Oily Gourd' (油葫芦)." Cixian said, "And for the Western Chamber, which line would you use?" Ziyun thought for a moment and laughed: "The lines available from the Western Chamber happen not to fit this rhyme." The four of them sat under the flowers; Ziyun asked why Qinyan had not come today. Baozhu said, "Today he went again to a private banquet in my place. He has one particular virtue: when he performs, he does not pay close attention to the prescribed stage business; but his natural elegance and charm are such that his own expression and bearing, flowing freely as it ought to, often surpasses the conventional stage business of the script. That is why some say he is untutored while others say he is divine." Ziyun laughed: "From now on, Mei Gengxiang will probably watch no theater but Yunong's and drink at no feast where Yunong is not present." Shufang laughed: "Yunong's conduct is not yet as remarkable as Meixiang's. I hear that lately Meixiang goes to Hongji Temple once a day. There is a certain Tian Xiangfan there, a renowned man of romantic temperament, who has created quite a stir. He once saw Meixiang perform and thereafter followed his carriage every single day: wherever Meixiang went east, he went east; wherever Meixiang went west, he went west, for a full month. Meixiang took pity on him and agreed to a conversation, and from a conversation they became bosom friends; now they are the closest of intimates, inseparable for a single day." Cixian laughed: "Can this be true! I always thought Meixiang a quick-witted, worldly-wise old hand beyond the ordinary. Surely no one could keep him captivated? This man must be no common person." While this conversation was at its most animated, a servant of Ziyun's came forward to announce: "A visitor has come to call!" Ziyun put on his cap and outer garments and went out.`,"As for what happened next, let the following chapter tell the tale."]},f=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations15:o},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),i={16:["After Academician Mei Shixie departed for his post, Lady Yan assumed charge of the inner household while Xu Shun managed affairs without, leaving the estate in perfect order. Ziyu continued his studies, delving into the various philosophical schools alongside his classical texts. On fine mornings and moonlit evenings, he would gather with close friends—most often Liu Wenze and Yan Zhongqing—by a bright window and clean desk, sharing in the quiet pursuits of brush and inkstone.","Sometimes they would drink wine and converse freely, or compose poetry and assign rhymes, enjoying the closest of bonds. Xu Duxiang from Yiyuan also paid several visits during the month, though they did not always meet. Because Duxiang commanded a grand presence and currently occupied a position of prosperity, he was viewed as a celestial being of wealth and status. As a result, those seeking his favor arrived in an endless stream, keeping him heavily occupied with social obligations.","Even on those rare days of leisure, he found himself occupied with seeking out rare flowers—the fragrance of winter jasmine and sweet osmanthus, the vibrant hues of embroidered brocades and patterned silks. Though captivating for a time, such splendor ultimately paled in comparison to a secluded bamboo grove deep within a small courtyard, or a few plum blossoms unfurling upon a paper screen, which offered a serene freedom far surpassing that of jade and bamboo.","Now let us speak of Wei Pincai. He was a man neither entirely coarse nor refined; though he mingled widely in cultured circles, he was no true confidant to anyone, serving rather as a willing companion-for-hire in wealthy households. Currently residing at the Mei mansion with Li Yuanmao, he had no real duties and spent his days wandering about idly. Furthermore, Yuanmao possessed a rather dull and obtuse temperament, making his constant company truly tiresome. Pincai thought to himself: 'I came to the capital seeking fame and profit, yet after several months here, I have achieved nothing. Uncle Mei has gone to Jiangxi and will not return for two or three years, while Uncle Wang assumes an air of great importance and shows no genuine concern. Though Chang'an is wonderful, it is not a place to linger indefinitely. I would do better to find a host of my own; perhaps I could catch the coattails of the powerful and gain some advantage. Third Master Fu has the widest circle of acquaintances. Asking him to find me an opportunity shouldn't be too difficult.' Having made up his mind, he took a carriage into the city to the Fu residence at the Golden Archway. A young servant went to the gate to inquire.","Pincai learned that the Third Master was not at home, but was playing cards across the street at Lord Gui's residence. When the servant came out, Pincai remarked, 'I left a card for Lord Gui last year but never had the chance to meet him; today is the perfect opportunity to pay my respects.' He went straight to the opposite gate and sent in his calling card. Hearing a voice invite him in, the two central doors opened, and Pincai entered a modest courtyard. Lord Gui emerged from the main hall, stepped forward to shake his hand, and invited him to sit on the kang inside. Pincai said, 'I have visited several times, but always when you were out. What a coincidence that we kept missing each other.' Lord Gui replied, 'My official duties keep me busy, so I rarely leave the city. I often think of asking Third Brother Fu to join me in finding you outside the city for a day of leisure, but if he isn't busy, I am. Perhaps in a couple of days things will settle down.' After exchanging pleasantries, Pincai took note of the room. It was neatly arranged, comprising three bays in total, with the eastern section partitioned off as a study. The eastern side of the courtyard featured a whitewashed wall, while the western side had a moon-gate shielded by a decorative screen, likely leading to the inner chambers. Above the kang hung a palindrome poem written in white on a blue background, flanked by a pair of gilded-paper scrolls inscribed by the Supervisor-General of the Board of Revenue. Eight square chairs lined the sides, with a writing desk and a small potted landscape arranged to the east, alongside several square stools. Pincai was just about to speak when Lord Gui added, 'Third Brother Fu is playing cards right here in that room. Shall we go sit over there?' He then led Pincai inside. At the entrance to the study, a young servant lifted a fragrant-colored inner curtain. Stepping inside, Pincai saw Third Master Fu toss his cards onto the table, give a great yawn, and stretch his back. Seeing Pincai, he stood up with a grin and said, 'It has been too long. How have you been?' Pincai shook his hand. Two other men were in the room, one facing south and the other north. The man facing south immediately rose to acknowledge them, while the man facing north merely shifted slightly in his seat, his gaze remaining fixed on his cards. Pincai observed the man in the upper seat: a face flushed with wine and meat, a two-pronged yellow beard, and serpentine eyes; his clothing and cap were new but lacked official refinement, and he appeared to be in his mid-forties. At the lower seat sat a myopic man past fifty wearing eyeglasses, also dressed in new clothes. Pincai asked, 'May I know the honored surnames of these two gentlemen?' The man in the upper seat answered at once, 'My name is Yang. I live in this neighborhood.' When asked for his name, the older man answered slowly, 'My name is Yan.' Lord Gui explained, 'This is Master Yan Jian'an, a private secretary in the Hua household. That gentleman is a specialist in geomancy and Third Brother Fu's sworn brother. He lives in that large residence in Donghu Lane, goes by the literary name Meichuang, and is the eighth in his family.' With that, a servant brought over a stool and placed it beside Third Master Fu, allowing everyone to sit. The Third Master remarked, 'Look at you! Every day you're out of the city enjoying yourself, yet you never come to see your older brother. You know I think of you, yet you don't think of me. I've looked for you two or three times, and you were always hiding away. Watching theater every day—what a fine time you're having!' Pincai laughed and replied, 'Not at all! There isn't a day I don't think of the Third Master. It's just that since my patron left for Jiangxi, all household matters have been entrusted to me, keeping me rather busy.' The man named Yang then asked, 'You are at Lord Mei's residence?' Pincai confirmed this, prompting the man to ask, 'Are you acquainted with the Mei family?'","'How could I not be?' Lord Gui interjected. 'I planted the trees on their ancestral burial ground myself. Old Yang the Eighth's feng shui is outstanding; most families in the inner city ask him to assess their properties.' Pincai engaged him further, though Yan Jian'an sat coldly aside, speaking only with Fu and Gui. Third Master Fu complained, 'Let's stop here. This card game is insufferably dull, and I've been losing. Let's settle our accounts.' Yan Jian'an countered, 'Why stop so soon? We've only played two rounds.' Meichuang agreed, 'Let it be; no need to continue.' Everyone rose, shifted their seats, and counted the chips, revealing that Yan and Fu had both lost. Pincai apologized, 'I seem to have broken up the game.' Fu the Third pounded his lower back with his fist and grumbled, 'I never liked this game anyway. Losing money just adds to the boredom.' Yan Jian'an murmured his agreement. Yang Meichuang laughed and teased, 'Who told you to play so recklessly? You kept throwing out wild cards—if you don't lose, who does?' Yan Jian'an chuckled in return, 'You can talk, but when have I ever seen you actually win money? All you're good at is bullying people. You can throw your weight around with me and Third Brother Fu, but not with Lord Gui.' The group shared a laugh before Lord Gui ordered a servant to bring out food and wine. Over four or five meat and vegetable dishes and a pot of yellow wine, the five men enjoyed a modest midday gathering.","During the meal, Pincai asked Yan Jian'an about the situation in the Hua household. Old Yan's spirits lifted as he boasted, 'My employer's young master is a rare individual in this world, and the grandeur of the mansion is beyond description.' When Pincai inquired about the number of colleagues on staff, Jian'an replied, 'There are over ten residing in the mansion, another ten or more serving at the old master's post, and countless others who come and go. I have shared thirty years of friendship with the old master and even served on campaigns with him. My standing is quite different from those other friends; the young master treats me with the courtesy due a senior. The rest are another matter entirely.' In truth, Yan Jian'an was a half-seasoned, half-green sycophant. He had old ties with Lord Hua, who tolerated his narrow-mindedness, foul language, and peculiar temper, allowing him to stay in the household. Young Master Hua found him even more difficult. Yang Meichuang, on the other hand, was a thoroughly unrefined social parasite who recognized only power and profit, possessing no genuine ability. Hailing from the inner provinces, he was timid and apprehensive. Relying on his sworn brotherhood with Fu the Third, he cultivated relations with wealthy acquaintances, using geomancy as a guise to make wild boasts and extract money. He colluded with landowners and conspired with landscape gardeners to cheat people, eventually purchasing a candidate status of the ninth supplementary rank despite knowing nothing of actual geomancy. On this occasion, he and Pincai found common ground and agreed to exchange visiting cards as sworn brothers. Pincai was eager to cultivate the connection and showed him every attention. Only Old Yan, mercenary to his core, looked down on Pincai, who in turn found the older man deeply disagreeable. After a convivial gathering, the five men prepared to part ways, with Yang Meichuang arranging to meet Pincai another day.","Pincai then accompanied Fu the Third back to his residence for a brief visit, taking the opportunity to share his thoughts. Third Master Fu suggested, 'In that case, why not simply move in here and stay for a while?' He added, 'In our uncle-by-marriage's household, friends are incredibly numerous. Today I heard Old Yan mention that someone recently left, so there is currently a vacancy.' Pincai asked, 'Who is this uncle-by-marriage?' Fu the Third replied, 'Don't you remember the procession of carriages outside the city last year, with that man in a sable coat and embroidered python robe? That is Young Master Hua.' Pincai was inwardly delighted, realizing that Young Master Hua's influence reached the heavens. If he could ingratiate himself and secure even a minor official position, his path would be much easier.","He pressed further, 'What sort of duties does a private secretary perform there?' Fu the Third laughed, 'Duties? You accompany him drinking, attend the theater, and draft a few unimportant letters during your leisure hours. Since you can also play and sing, you will suit his tastes perfectly. He is fundamentally a good person; if you flatter him with high praise, he will be pleased. Contradict him, and he turns cold. I can see that your social manner is excellent, your character is well-rounded, and your mind is naturally sharp. Our uncle-by-marriage also has some literary talent. Last year, the Empress asked him to compose poetry and responded favorably, even rewarding him with a yellow-braided pouch and a four-happiness ring. If you wish to go, I will recommend you tomorrow. I guarantee it will work out.' Pincai's face lit up with pleasure, and he bowed in gratitude. He considered Old Yan an obstacle but reasoned, 'Everyone minds their own affairs; if I simply avoid him, there will be no issue.' After sitting a while longer, he took his leave of Fu the Third and returned to his lodgings.","The following day, Fu the Third arrived at the Hua mansion, met Young Master Hua, and recommended Pincai as a secretary to handle miscellaneous affairs. Young Master Hua agreed, saying, 'I have no strict limit on staff here. As long as the person is capable and your close friend, there is nothing to discuss. Tell him he is invited and ask him to come.' He immediately instructed Lin Shanzhi to inform the chief steward that Master Wei's stipend and board were arranged. Fu the Third agreed repeatedly, paid his respects to Madam Hua, and then hurried out of the city to inform Wei Pincai, urging him to arrive the next day.","That same day, Pincai explained the situation to Ziyu, expressing his gratitude for the months of hospitality. Ziyu was taken aback, asking, 'Elder Brother, why must you leave? Have I done something to offend you?' Pincai laughed reassuringly and replied, 'Since arriving at your esteemed home, I have received boundless generosity from your parents and yourself. How could I ask for more? However, I came to the capital under my father's strict instructions to establish a career before returning.","'This opportunity provides a modest foothold, and Third Brother Fu has gone out of his way to arrange it. I must go and see what comes of it. It is only across the city, so I can visit frequently. If you do not forget me, I have heard the gardens and pavilions of the Hua mansion are magnificent for outings. Please explain this to your mother on my behalf; before I depart tomorrow, I will pay my respects and take my leave.' Li Yuanmao, standing nearby, felt a pang of sadness upon hearing Pincai was bound for the Hua household. 'If you leave, I will be here alone, and you will lose a companion. I have heard Young Master Hua has a difficult temper; you had better not go and invite trouble. Stay here instead, and we can reconsider after the New Year.' Pincai sighed, 'Everyone has their own path, and my situation is not like yours. You are the son of a county magistrate, enjoying a comfortable life provided for you. I, on the other hand, cannot simply enjoy ease; I must provide for my family.' Hearing this, Ziyu realized he could not insist and went to explain the matter to Lady Yan. She replied, 'If that is the case, we have no choice but to let him go. Still, when your father left, he instructed me to treat him well, noting his capability. There is little for him to do here at present, but if the need arises in the future, we can always ask him back.'","That evening, Lady Yan had Ziyu host a farewell dinner for Pincai, presenting him with forty taels of silver. Pincai was overwhelmed with gratitude. He and Yuanmao talked late into the night, both reluctant to part ways.","Early the next morning, Fu the Third sent two carriages to collect Pincai. After bidding farewell to Lady Yan, Ziyu, and Yuanmao, Pincai gathered his belongings, brought Si'er along, and boarded the carriage. They first stopped at the Fu residence for a brief visit before Fu the Third personally escorted him to the Hua mansion. At the gate, a messenger was sent ahead to announce Master Wei's arrival. Peering from the carriage, Pincai was awestruck by the imposing façade, which surpassed even the Viceroy's yamen in Nanjing. A grand spirit screen of finely ground brick stood before the gate, adorned with carved floral patterns and flower-corniced drip tiles beneath a glazed roof, rising over thirty chi high and stretching seventy chi wide. Two massive stone lions, each over eight chi tall, guarded the entrance. Looking through the outer gate, a central avenue flanked by towering trees stretched toward the inner gate an arrow's flight away, fading into the distance. Dozens of attendants sat near the inner gate. After half an hour, the messenger returned with an invitation to enter. Fu the Third and Wei Pincai alighted, straightened their robes, and proceeded inside. As they approached the inner gate, the twenty or thirty uniformly dressed attendants rose slowly. Several recognized Fu the Third and greeted him, asking, 'Is this the newly engaged master secretary?' Wei Pincai returned their greetings. Passing through the inner gate, they walked another hundred paces along the avenue before reaching the great reception hall. A guide led them around the hall, through covered corridors with winding balustrades, and into an inner courtyard meticulously arranged with flowers, bamboo, and ornamental stones. Stepping through a flowered gate and a connecting hall, Pincai felt as though he had wandered into a painted scroll. Long galleries and tiered pavilions, painted beams and carved rafters, green tiles and glazed roofing gleamed dazzlingly beneath the sun. Never in his life had Pincai witnessed such vast, magnificent, and solemn splendor, and his heart fluttered with awe. Fu the Third, familiar with the estate, led the way as a guide offered, 'Please allow me to escort the Third Master to the Western Flower Hall.' They followed a winding path until they reached a polished-brick moon-gate adorned with floral motifs. The guide stopped and gave a soft cough. Four handsome young pages emerged from within, and the guide instructed them, 'Please escort the guests to the Western Flower Hall.' Pincai followed Fu the Third through the gate into a garden paved with intricately pierced Taihu Lake stones. Below lay a clear pool where two golden carp swam lazily through a gap in the rocks. Slender bamboo swayed along the path, while vibrant blooms greeted them at every turn. After ascending several uneven stone steps and crossing a flat stone bridge, they passed a pavilion and a rockery reminiscent of the Lion Grove, navigating a cave in the artificial mountain before finally arriving at the garden reception hall.","Beyond lay several more garden pavilions, cloaked in dense green shade and alive with the clamor of birdsong. The courtyard was ablaze with opium poppies and corn poppies, their vibrant hues complementing the wisteria draping gracefully from ancient cypresses by the pool, alongside blossoming crabapple and redbud.","Approaching the flower hall, they encountered a carved balustrade flanked by windows of five-colored glass. In the center hung a crimson curtain of layered gauze, interwoven with silver threads. A page boy hooked the gauze curtain onto a silver butterfly clasp adorned with kingfisher feathers. Inside the hall, the floor was covered with a mallard-green velvet carpet. Above, the ceiling resembled the cabin of a boat, crafted from fragrant nanmu wood and intricately carved with delicate flora. A wooden plaque bearing the calligraphy of Wang Duo read: 'The Hall of Mossy Blooms and Mountain Rain, Where Sentiments Connect.' Countless lanterns hung amidst a canopy of pearl tassels and spirit-umbrellas. The central flat doors were engraved with Wen Zhengming's cursive script. A large kang, draped in ancient brocades of dazzling brilliance, dominated the room. On the kang table rested a precious tripod censer, exuding a rich, fragrant smoke. The walls were papered in white floral damask; one side displayed eight landscapes framed in green silk by Wang Youcheng, while the other featured two antique cabinets holding nanmu wood boxes, likely containing rare books. All the tables, stools, and chairs were carved from purple sandalwood and cushioned with five-colored floral brocade.","It was a realm of embroidered splendor, enough to dazzle the eyes and bewilder the mind. Fu the Third and Pincai took their seats, waiting for the time it takes to drink two cups of tea. Suddenly, a page boy emerged and announced, 'The Young Master is feeling unwell today. He invites the Third Master and the Master Secretary to the East Garden to meet the other secretaries. Master Wei will reside in the East Garden alongside Master Zhang and Master Gu.' Fu the Third replied, 'Please convey my regards and wish him well.' Pincai also stood up, adding, 'Please deliver my respects as well.' The page boy bowed and agreed. A page waiting outside the window then guided them back through the moon-gate toward the East Garden.","The East Garden lay to the front and east. Pincai followed Fu the Third through a maze of winding corridors, encountering attendants at every turn. The Hua mansion operated under strict rules: each gate had a steward, and every entry and exit was recorded in a ledger. After walking for quite some time, Pincai lost track of the many courtyards they had passed. Behind the connecting hall, an eastern alley stretched for what felt like half a mile. Only after passing through yet another gate did the East Garden finally reveal itself. It was a sprawling sanctuary of pavilions and terraces, mountains and waters, and dense groves of flowers and trees, offering an entirely different kind of beauty. The guiding page handed them over to the garden staff and departed. They were led through a mottled bamboo fence to a hall of ten rooms, flanked by side chambers. This was where Zhang Xiaomei and Gu Yueqing practiced their painting. The two men came out to greet them, knowing Pincai was the new arrival recommended by Third Master Fu, and sat down to converse. Pincai noted Zhang Xiaomei's handsome features; a young man of twenty from Hangzhou, he specialized in fine-brush figure painting and was also skilled in playing the flute and strings. Gu Yueqing, a native of Suzhou slightly older than Xiaomei, was equally refined and focused on landscape and floral painting. Also present was Wang Qingyun, a drafting secretary in his fifties and an old friend of the Duke. Yan Jian'an, who handled miscellaneous writing tasks, resided in another courtyard but was invited over for introductions. Yan Jian'an remarked, 'Who would have thought that after meeting just the other day, you would join our household today? What a curious turn of events.' Pincai replied humbly, 'I am deeply indebted to Young Master Hua for his misguided favor in bringing me here. I fear I possess little talent and must rely entirely on your esteemed guidance.'","The two elders, Yan and Wang, replied politely, 'Not at all. Since the master invited you based on your reputation, you surely possess genuine talent. We look forward to learning from you.' Pincai repeatedly murmured, 'I would not dare.' Third Master Fu interjected, 'Brother Wei is my sworn brother and a close nephew of Lord Mei from outside the south city. He is highly capable and a true friend. Being new to the household, he naturally needs your guidance. We are all one family now, so let us not be strangers. When I see our uncle-by-marriage tomorrow, I will formally entrust him to your care.' Turning to Pincai, he added, 'Let's go inside and see which room you will take.' They moved to the inner courtyard, which also consisted of five rooms. Zhang Xiaomei occupied the two eastern rooms, while Pincai was assigned the two on the west, leaving the central room open for receiving guests. Fu the Third immediately ordered the pages to bring in the luggage and arrange the furnishings.","Just as Fu the Third was preparing to leave, someone entered and announced, 'The Young Master has sent a banquet and requests that the Third Master and the secretaries accompany Master Wei for a few cups of wine. The Young Master apologizes for not joining you, but he is indisposed, and sends his regrets to the Third Master.' Fu the Third stood up, expressed his thanks, and noted, 'It is getting late; it is exactly time to eat.' They gathered around the round table in the central room, enjoying a relaxed and cheerful meal. Pincai marvelled at the endless array of dishes; large and small bowls, grand and modest plates—totaling over forty varieties. They drank until the second watch before Fu the Third finally took his leave. His servants waited with lanterns as Pincai escorted him to the garden gate, where Fu the Third offered a stream of parting advice. As Pincai prepared to follow him further, Fu the Third stopped him:","'There is no need to see me out. You might get lost trying to find your way back through the garden, which would only cause trouble. We will see each other in a few days.' With that, he departed without looking back. Pincai returned inside and conversed with Zhang and Gu for quite some time, inquiring about the intricate workings of the household before finally retiring for the night.","The following day, Zhang and Gu introduced Pincai to his various colleagues and the household's managing stewards, including those in charge of the treasury, the gates, the kitchens, the stables, the storehouses, and the ledger-keepers at every entrance. He met everyone of significance. The mansion of a nobleman truly resembles a deep sea; newly arrived, Pincai felt entirely unmoored and constrained, scarcely daring to speak an extra word, focusing solely on cautious humility and agreeable flattery. Let the reader note: with Wei Pincai's entry into the Hua mansion, many events were set into motion, though those tales belong to a later time.","Meanwhile, Ziyu found himself deeply missing Pincai for several days following his departure. It was mid-April, and due to an intercalary fifth month, the season had shifted slightly later than usual. Peonies still bloomed vibrantly in the courtyard, accompanied by lilacs and crabapples—a profusion of crimson fragrance, powdered beauty, and pristine grace. He wandered among them, admiring their solitary elegance.","The fragmented chatter of birds and the slanting shadows of blossoms left him feeling a touch weary. He recalled the ancient lines, 'The warm wind scatters the birds' songs; the high sun deepens the flowers' shadows,' marvelling at their exquisite capture of the scene. His thoughts then drifted to Lu Sulan and the warmth shown to him that day. Seeking out the fan Sulan had inscribed, he studied it closely, thinking, 'I truly ought to give him something in return.' He selected an antique inkstone, two boxes of fine scented ink, an album of twelve ink orchids by Xu Songling, and a round silk fan. Carefully copying his two recently composed 'Farewell to Spring' regulated verses in neat calligraphy, he initially intended to have a servant deliver the gifts. Yet, oppressed by the long, stifling day, a desire to visit friends and seek out fragrant company took hold of him. He informed his mother of his intent to call upon Liu, Yan, and the others, and set out in his carriage with a page boy. To his great disappointment, he found none of them at home. Feeling thoroughly deflated, he made his way alone to Sulan's residence, arriving just as Sulan returned from the theater. Welcomed inside, they exchanged the usual pleasantries. Ziyu presented his gifts, which Sulan received with gratitude, examining them with careful appreciation. Noting the elegant calligraphy and refined paintings, Sulan thanked him repeatedly. They moved into the adjacent study, a secluded and elegant space where Sulan practiced painting and calligraphy. Sulan inquired, 'Why are you out all alone today? Have you been across the street? Did you see the one who holds your heart?' Ziyu smiled faintly, 'I visited several places today but found no one. I came specifically to see you and haven't crossed the street. Is Yu Nong at home?' Sulan let out a soft sigh and fell silent. Puzzled, Ziyu asked, 'Why does Xiang Wan seem so unhappy?' Sulan replied, 'It is not I who am unhappy. I was thinking of the one in your heart. This tangled, unspoken bond between you two—how will it ever be resolved? It causes so many meaningless tears to fall, yet no one truly understands why. If you ask him, he refuses to speak, leaving everyone to guess in vain. Given that you rarely even see each other, how has it brought him to such a state? Even his master is baffled, claiming Yu Nong has developed some strange melancholy—he grows despondent without reason and has become increasingly distant from those around him. He was always somewhat aloof, but he never carried such a heavy heart. Since that shared meal at Yiyuan, he hardly pays attention to anyone. Whenever he speaks, he forces the conversation toward plum blossoms (mei hua), no matter the topic. He constantly remarks how fortunate he is that Yiyuan has a Plum Vale (Mei Yan) and even asked Xiao Jingyi to paint four hanging scrolls of plum blossoms in various colors. That much might be overlooked, but...'","'Then he suddenly asked if the bolts of silk ordered from the south for Duxiang included any patterned with plum branches, or perhaps mixed flowers. Remarkably, Duxiang managed to find several bolts, and Yu Nong has already had robes and padded jackets tailored from them, wearing them constantly. Tell me, is this obsession not extraordinary? If it is not meant for you, who else could it be for?' Hearing this, a profound ache surged through Ziyu's heart, and despite his efforts, tears began to fall. He tried to speak, but the words caught in his throat, leaving him to gaze blankly at Sulan. Sulan pressed on gently, 'What kind of bond is this between you two? I have tried my best to help you. Because I bear a slight resemblance to you, he often comes to pour out his vague, half-formed thoughts to me. I cannot help but wonder: do you think he even knows what lies in your heart?",`'And does he feel the same way toward you? He said it quite clearly himself: "This is a matter of my own heart—what does it matter if anyone else knows? What does it matter how others treat me? As long as I understand it clearly myself, that is enough." Master Gengxiang, what truly resides in your heart? You need not hide it from me. If you find it difficult to tell him directly, I can speak on your behalf. You clearly harbor deep longing for him; why not share it with me?' Ziyu remained silent. Sensing his reluctance, Sulan suggested, 'Shall we go together and visit him?' Ziyu hesitated briefly before agreeing, 'We might as well.' Sulan led Ziyu out the door, and a few steps later, they arrived at the gate of Qiushui Studio, where five or six carriages were already parked. Sulan observed,`,"'It looks as though he has guests; it might not be convenient to enter. Let us go back and send someone to check the situation first.' Ziyu felt a wave of unease, uncertain of who was inside or whether Yu Nong was obliged to entertain them. He reasoned that one must naturally attend to their guests and could hardly refuse them. Lacking a better idea, he simply followed Sulan's lead.",`Sulan returned to his own gate and sent someone to inquire at Qinyan's residence. Shortly after, the messenger returned, reporting that Qinyan was confined to his bed with an illness. The guests had been invited by his master, Changqing; they were several clerks from the Board who had come for an early meal and would soon be departing. Sulan invited Ziyu, 'Please, come inside and wait.' Relieved by the news, Ziyu returned indoors, taking a seat with a heavy heart. Sulan maintained a cheerful stream of conversation, asking about various matters before gently returning to the topic of Ziyu's true feelings for Yu Nong. Unable to deflect the questions any longer, Ziyu sighed, 'The matter of Yu Nong is a long story.' He recounted the scene Wei Pincai had witnessed during his journey, and how he himself had been captivated by Yu Nong's incomparable performance of "Waking from a Dream" at the guild hall earlier that year, before he had even come to know Yu Nong's character and inner depth. He spoke of the incident at Yiyuan, where the false Qinyan had tricked him into confessing his admiration—an admiration rooted in his belief in Qinyan's pristine self-possession, making the rumor of his self-abandonment all the more shocking. He described how Yu Nong, listening from behind the screen, had been so moved he nearly wept aloud. Yet, when they finally sat together, they were both struck mute, like ancient souls reunited in this life, each reading the other's heart without the need for words. 'He acted so naturally, without conscious thought,' Ziyu murmured. 'And I, too, acted without deliberate intent.`,"'I find I cannot banish him from my heart or my sight for even a moment. I had no idea he was so consumed with thoughts of me behind my back until you told me. I understand the depth of his feelings for me, but does he truly grasp what lies in mine? What is it about me that could move him so profoundly? Perhaps it would be better if we never saw each other again; it might spare us the heartache of every meeting.' As Ziyu spoke, his expression grew increasingly sorrowful, hovering on the edge of tears.","Deeply moved, Sulan shed a few tears of his own. After a long silence, he offered words of comfort: 'You two must have shared a profound bond in a past life; how else could it have come to this? With your talents and bearing, you are naturally cherished by all. But it is truly extraordinary that Yu Nong—a soul of ice and snow—should intertwine his feelings so tenderly for you alone.",`'Despite all of Duxiang's gentle warmth and devoted attentions, he is merely a kindred spirit in the realm of affection. I can see that Yu Nong's regard for him is not even a fraction of what he feels for you. Yet Duxiang cherishes him all the more, believing that "each has his own predestined affinity, determined by heaven and beyond the coercion of human feeling." Furthermore, your heart, Gengxiang, is fixed solely upon Yu Nong without the slightest wavering—this is the true nature of a deeply passionate soul. None but Yu Nong is worthy of your admiration, and none but you are worthy of his devotion. Does not the "Airs of the States," when it speaks of love without licentiousness, refer precisely to you and Yu Nong?' Hearing this, Ziyu felt boundless gratitude toward Duxiang and marvelled at Sulan's perceptive insight. It was no wonder the 'The Opera Stage Flower Manual' had crowned him with the title of Tanhua.`,"They conversed for a long while before Sulan offered Ziyu refreshments and sent another messenger to check on Qinyan. The messenger returned with troubling news: 'The first group of guests has left, but unfortunately, another group arrived demanding to see Qinyan. Changqing told them he was not at home, but they refused to leave and sat down to wait. Since Changqing did not know them and refused to entertain them, he retreated to his room to smoke. The men forced their way in, snatched his opium pipe, and threatened to report him to the Metropolitan Police. Left with no choice, Changqing had to beg their pardon and call in Li Si from the neighboring distillery and Duan Zi Wang San from across the street to mediate. The room is now packed with onlookers, and the situation remains unresolved.' Ziyu let out a long sigh upon hearing this. 'Even a single meeting with Yu Nong is fraught with such difficulty. It is growing late, and I must return home. When you see him tomorrow, please convey my regards and tell him he must not continue like this. He must prioritize his health. He should visit Duxiang's place more often to avoid idle gossip. I cannot go out frequently, but we will meet in a few days. If you see Duxiang, please convey my deepest thanks to him as well. Someday soon, we should all gather for an outing in his garden.' Sulan replied, 'Whenever you plan to visit, set a date and come here first. I will arrange for Yu Nong to join us, as it is much quieter here. His master has a difficult temper, and it is a great misfortune that Yu Nong is bound to such a person. His luck is truly poor.' Ziyu asked, 'What sort of temper does his master have?' Sulan explained, 'He loves money, fears the powerful, and disdains the poor. Because Yu Nong is favored by Duxiang, he has been treated relatively well; his master never treated others so favorably.' This revelation added yet another burden to Ziyu's heart. Unable to find peace, he lingered in hesitation. Seeing the lateness of the hour, he finally hardened his resolve, stepped outside, and mounted his carriage, looking back several times before settling into his seat as they left the lane. Just as the page boy stepped onto the carriage's footboard, another carriage drawn by two horses came galloping fiercely toward them. It was Intendant-General Wang, charging headlong in their direction. With no time to yield, Ziyu prepared to step down, but Wang Wenhui quickly waved him off, exchanged a few brief words, nodded, and drove away.","Ziyu had spent half the day out, only managing a conversation with Sulan; every other friend he had sought was absent. Instead, he had the misfortune of running into his father-in-law—a truly vexing encounter. He considered visiting Gao Pin, but the distance was great, and having been away so long, he feared a reprimand upon returning home. Defeated, he had no choice but to head back in low spirits.","It is a truth that nine out of ten things in life rarely go according to plan. But let the following chapter tell the tale."]},m=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations16:i},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),s={17:[`When Ziyu returned from Sulan's and had paid his respects to his mother, he made his way to his study. The evening meal being finished, a solitary moon arose, casting its silvery radiance among the blossoms. A gentle breeze whispered past, and the heavens and clouds were swept clean and bright. Rolling up the Xiang bamboo blinds, he leaned against the railing and gazed out. Suddenly, a young page entered to announce: "Masters Gao, Shi, Yan, and Wang have all arrived together." Ziyu had been lost in wistful thought; seeing them arrive in unison brought him boundless joy, and he warmly invited them in.`,`Ziyu recounted how he had called upon each of them earlier in the day to no avail. Wang Xun was the first to speak: "Today we were all gathered in Zhuoran's studio, where we met with Tian Xiangfan and Meixiang, and chanced upon Zhujun who arrived later. Xiangfan is truly a kindred spirit. The way he and Meixiang interact is genuinely enviable." Gao Pin chimed in: "Xiangfan is now a man whose six senses are entirely purified, his five skandhas rendered void. He steadfastly abides by their three-point covenant, strictly forbidden from wandering astray. I am only now convinced that the world is indeed filled with hen-pecked men! No wonder the likes of Yongan and Zhujun guard their bedchambers so dutifully, not daring to take a single step outside without a direct order. For if they disobey, the ankle-crushers await them at night!"`,`"And Xiangfan is only dealing with a partner who is half-feminine and half-masculine, yet already he is brought to such a state! Is it any wonder about the strict rules within the Hall of the Four Fears?" This caused everyone to laugh. Zhongqing said: "You yourself emerged from behind those very doors; now that you are far away, you boast." Nanxiang said: "I saw Zhuoran's letter to his wife—it read like a subordinate's petition to a superior, filled with phrases such as 'deeply indebted to your grace' and 'seeping into the marrow and soaking the flesh.'" Everyone burst into uproarious laughter. Gao Pin said: "Preposterous! Even as a fabrication, it lacks the ring of truth." They joked for a while longer before Gao Pin said: "Yuxiang, the day after tomorrow there is a most splendid affair, and I have come to discuss it with you." Ziyu asked: "What affair?" Gao Pin said: "The fifteenth is Meixiang's birthday. Today we all deliberated and agreed to join forces with Qianzhou and yourself to form a 'Six Gentlemen Decoction'—pooling our funds to find a spacious pavilion, inviting all those renowned beauties for a day of lively festivity, and celebrating the birthdays of Xiangfan and Meixiang. What say you to this?" Ziyu said: "Excellent, excellent! But where shall we convene?" Wang Xun said: "My home would suffice, but it lacks a garden. It would be better in Qianzhou's garden. With the six of us as hosts, plus Xiangfan, that makes seven; Meixiang, Yaoqing, Xiangwan, Peixian, Jingfang, Ruixiang, Shouxiang, and Xiaomei make eight. We shall require three tables to seat everyone. As for the pooled funds, we cannot fix an amount in advance; we must simply make the arrangements and settle the accounts thereafter." Everyone agreed. Ziyu sat in a daze. Zhongqing laughed: "Yongan, you have not executed this errand thoroughly enough; you will surely incur someone's displeasure." Before Wang Xun could reply, Nanxiang said: "On what grounds do you say that?" Zhongqing said: "Did you not notice that when Yongan was calling the roll, he omitted a most vital person? Will that not invite reproach?" Nanxiang calculated for a moment and laughed: "Indeed, indeed."`,'Wang Xun said: "Are you speaking of Xu Duxiang? I had not forgotten him; I only feared his social obligations were too numerous and he might be unable to attend." Ziyu said: "Though Duxiang has many engagements, judging by his temperament, if we invite him, he will certainly come regardless of his busyness. And Xiao Jingyi must absolutely be invited as well." Everyone said: "Splendid, we shall simply add those two. That makes nine, and together with the eight, it is seventeen—a truly bustling assembly." Nanxiang said: "Is there no one else?" Wang Xun said: "Who else could there be?" Nanxiang said: "What a fine memory you possess! Since you are convening a grand assembly of flowers, you have actually forgotten a king of flowers. To invite Gengxiang but exclude Yunong, leaving him alone in the corner, lacks all reason."',`Wang Xun said: "Quite right, I deserve a reprimand! I entirely forgot Qinyan for a moment, yet he absolutely must come. And that Qin Qiguan, styled Yuyan, should be summoned too, making it a perfect ten." Everyone said: "That is even better." Ziyu said: "Now that we have discussed it, how shall we invite the guests?" Wang Xun said: "You draft a brief note to the two gentlemen Xu and Xiao of the Yi Garden, as well as to Qianzhou and the others, and tomorrow we shall notify them ourselves." Thus they conversed straight on until the second watch before dispersing. After Ziyu had seen them off, he sat alone in deep contemplation for a while, reflecting: "The gathering the day after tomorrow will be an event to span a thousand ages. I wonder if Qinyan's illness will have subsided by then? At that time, among the ten trees of the jasper forest, the pollia will naturally reign supreme; I wonder how the thoroughwort of the high official compares to my Yunong? Recalling his demeanor toward Tian Chunhang, he is clearly a man of talent and wisdom, and must harbor his own profound depths of feeling. Each possesses their own unique strengths, so there is naturally no need to compare them against one another."`,"Thereupon, he gently ground the fine ink, slowly took up his Xiang bamboo brush, and wrote:",`"The ninety days of spring are gone and past recall; two or three kindred spirits come unbidden to gather. Let us make an elegant assembly of zither and wine, and savor our leisure amid birds and blossoms. Though the grounds may lack the majesty of famous mountains, we rejoice that the courtyard is free of common weeds. We pity those of orchid essence, fallen into the pear-garden world; we convene our bamboo-grove assembly, and count the flower-tidings. The many labor at refining the musical scores, by chance resembling those who weave brocade; their innate wisdom formed into heartfelt understanding, they would blush to be counted mere players of the money-counting trick. We have transported tender warmth into a secluded temple; we have discovered poetic elegance amid the dusty world. We celebrate the day the pearl formed in its azure sea, and congratulate the moment the jade issued from Lantian. City-toppling celebrated scholars should naturally cherish one another; the powdered red and the blue gown may well be weighed on the same scale. Set out the thirty-six tiles of the mandarin-duck game, let the red beans be freely tossed; prepare a thousand pairs of kingfisher-wing ornaments, let the purple clouds be welcomed at will. Respectfully, I address this note in advance. Bestir your lofty carriage early, and grace our humble lane with your radiant presence. Mei Ziyu bows in respect. To the honored desks of Master Duxiang and the recluse Jingyi."

Having finished writing and sealed the letter, he affixed his seal and handed it to a page to deliver to the gatehouse, instructing that it be sent to the Yi Garden first thing the next morning, to invite Masters Xu and Xiao to drink at Young Master Liu's residence the day after tomorrow, making the instructions clear and unmistakable. The page assented, and Ziyu retired for the night.`,`The next day, Wang Xun, Shi Nanxiang, and the others went to Liu Wenze's house and informed him. Wenze was highly delighted, declaring that the following day they would arrange the festivities right there in the 'Sword Reclining, Zither Sleeping' studio. As it happened, orchids and thoroughworts were emitting their sweet fragrance, while peonies and crabapple blossoms were in full, resplendent bloom. After a short while, they went to notify the assembly of flowers to gather fully at the chen hour the next day. Wenze said: "Tomorrow among the floral forest, I fear a few may be unable to attend. I know Qin Qiguan suffers an eye ailment, and Du Qinyan is also ill and not yet recovered. Last night I saw Sulan, who mentioned that Gengxiang sat there for half the day, then went to visit Qinyan; it happened that his master was hosting guests so he did not enter, and Qinyan remained unaware that Yuxiang had come to visit him. Even if those two do not come tomorrow, there are still eight, which will be lively enough. I imagine Gengxiang and Jingyi will certainly grace us." Nanxiang said: "As for drinking games at the banquet, there are very few fresh ones. If they are too abstruse, I fear the guests may be unable to manage them on the spot. We need something that appeals to both refined and common tastes, easy to comprehend and perform, yet avoiding the overly familiar. Games like 'covering and guessing' also feel rather dull." Wang Xun said: "The poetry-matching game we played here before would suffice." Wenze said: "To play that game again would also feel flat. Let us wait until tomorrow and see what the setting inspires." That day Wang Xun and the others dined at Wenze's house, conversed a while longer, and dispersed. Wenze then had servants go around to confirm the reservations, emphasizing that the following day they must absolutely gather early to enjoy a full day's pleasure. All should arrive in casual attire, with no need for formal robes and caps. The messengers returned, confirming that all had been informed.`,"Now let us speak of Tian Chunhang. Since forging his bond with Huifang, he had not set foot outside the temple grounds. Huifang came without fail once a day, whether morning or evening, sometimes for pure conversation, sometimes for a modest drink, occasionally offering words of admonishment and needle-sharp advice. As a result, Chunhang was fully satisfied in his heart, dedicating himself solely to grinding ink and reading the classics, wielding his brush in literary and poetic pursuits. He originally possessed the rich learning of three winters, able to compose ten thousand words while leaning on a horse; for a time his fame had moved the capital, and all those in power wished to gather him under their patronage. Alas, Chunhang was proudly upright and self-reliant, considering it a disgrace to seek favor. He had never carried his visiting-card to call upon a vital official, preferring instead to dwell in a desolate temple with a jade-like companion by his side and celebrated men of the same voice. Huifang also helped him befriend many worthy companions, such as Xu Duxiang, Xiao Jingyi, Liu Wenze, Shi Nanxiang, Yan Zhongqing, and Wang Xun. Zhongqing's previous discord with Chunhang had originally been intended to spur Chunhang onward; after Gao Pin illuminated the underlying circumstances, and mentioned that Zhongqing was still sending fifty taels of silver for his living expenses, Chunhang was naturally filled with the deepest gratitude and admiration. Zhongqing had Huifang smooth things over for him, rendering their relationship even more harmonious than before. Only with Ziyu had he not yet met face to face. On this day, knowing that Wenze and the others were hosting a birthday banquet for Huifang, he was immensely pleased in his heart; yet because their close bond was now universally known, he conversely felt somewhat abashed and intended not to go. But unable to refuse the graciousness of the crowd, he had no choice but to fulfill his promise.",`Wenze had the 'Sword Reclining, Zither Sleeping' study arranged from the very break of dawn. Because the theme was a birthday celebration, some subtle adornment was required: in the center hung a scroll painting of 'Picture of the Immortals' Grand Assembly.' All the antiques and furnishings were exquisitely refined. Along the long corridor, outside the Xiang bamboo blinds, over ten pots of thoroughwort were arranged; catching the faint ripples of the gentle breeze, their fragrance assailed the senses. Wenze was standing alone before the corridor when he saw a figure approach from the front; gazing from afar, he knew it was Huifang arriving in splendid attire. Upon stepping onto the edge of the stairs, Huifang immediately bowed with the utmost respect and reverence in a grand ceremony. Wenze hurriedly helped him up, saying: "Why do you act so, Meixiang? You should allow me first to offer you birthday congratulations." Huifang said: "On this lowly one's natal day, to be graced with the favor of all the noble lords is an honor Huifang can hardly bear. Yesterday I intended to visit each residence to express my gratitude, but feared you might blame me for failing to accept my elevation gracefully. Moreover, today in the Great Veil Heaven all the immortals gather together, causing us humble fowls and dogs to ascend alongside you. Though I cannot attain immortality, I can at least cast off the vulgar; therefore I have respectfully obeyed your command and come bowing and kneeling." Wenze said: "This is also a grand occasion for us companions, gazing upon a city-toppling beauty, merely borrowing the flower to offer to Buddha." While they were speaking, Lu Sulan, Li Yulin, and Jin Shufang arrived together, followed by the four—Gao, Shi, Yan, and Wang; Huifang thanked them all one by one.`,`While everyone was exchanging courtesies, they saw the servant who had received the letter leading Ziyu in. Though Huifang did not recognize him, he had already guessed his identity in his heart, and stepped forward to bow and give thanks. Ziyu supported him, saying: "Can this be Meixiang? I, Yuxiang, have long admired your name but lacked the fate to meet you face to face; today, fortunate enough to catch the wind of your presence, I already feel your pure fragrance filling my very being." Huifang repeatedly said he dared not accept such praise. Observing Ziyu's bearing and features, he secretly marveled in his heart: "Truly a sun and star of heaven, a luan and phoenix among men! He possesses an aura of unblemished purity and harmonious essence, shining brightly with a grace that rises above the crowd. No wonder Du Yunong is so deeply enchanted by him. Alongside my Tian, they might be called twin paragons, a Chu and a Liang born together!" Ziyu then greeted Lu Sulan and the others. Suddenly they heard from outside: "Master Xu and Master Xiao have arrived!" Everyone went out from the hall together to welcome them; there came Ziyun and Cixian, moving gracefully as scions of great families draped in their furs, with Yuan Baozhu and Wang Lanbao following behind. Further back were eight bright-faced and handsome young attendants carrying clothing bundles, bronze basins, and rinsing vessels.`,`Huifang stepped forward several paces to bow; Ziyun and Cixian each helped him up from one side and said: "Meixiang has always been easy and unconstrained, so why so formal today? Surely you are putting us to too much trouble." They then entered the room and exchanged greetings with all present. All the young dan actors also completed their introductions, seated themselves by seniority, and settled down. Ziyun said: "Thanks to the kind invitation by letter from Gengxiang, Qianzhou, and all the gentlemen, today's occasion is truly a grand gathering. Yesterday I drank my fill of reading Gengxiang's pearls and jade, and even today my teeth still carry their lingering fragrance. And now we find ourselves in this great assembly of flowers, allowing your humble guests to follow in the rear and bask in the perfume—truly a wondrous matter." Cixian said: "Colored phoenixes of the scarlet mountains, black-robed scions of the deep lanes; no elegance of sash and sandal could surpass this. And yet may the cold-hilltop wild cranes also soar freely among them?" Wenze, Wang Xun, and the others all said in unison: "Duxiang and Jingyi are foremost among celebrated men, leaders of the literary and poetic assembly; there is no moment we do not long for your refined example. That you do not disdain our humble abode and have come so graciously today is all we could wish for in this life!" Nanxiang said: "Between friends, what matters is congenial rapport; in my humble view, there is no need to maintain the stiff formalities of mere social calls."`,`Zhongqing said: "Zhujun is a candid and open-hearted person, and his opening words show it immediately. Today's gathering is all the closest of friends; it would be best to speak freely of what lies in the heart, without standing on ceremony." Then Gao Pin laughed and said: "Today the Queen Mother of the West has arrived early, and it is only the Old Immortal of the South Pole who is coming late. Could it be that halfway here he ran into the somersault cloud of the Little Pilgrim and was struck, injuring the little longevity-star and making it inconvenient to walk? Or perhaps he slipped and fell once again in a carriage rut." Everyone burst out laughing: "Zhuoran's wit is priceless. When the birthday-star arrives, let him be fined three great cups." Huifang seemed to blush slightly. Baozhu said: "How many guests are still to arrive today?" Wenze said: "Just the birthday-star, one person. Among the flower contingent, four are absent: Qinyan and Qiguan are both ill and sent their regrets early this morning; Guibao and Chunxi will certainly be here. Once Xiangfan arrives, we can take our seats."`,"Before Wenze had finished speaking, Chunhang had already arrived. Everyone exchanged greetings afresh, and all the beauties were also introduced. Banter and teasing naturally followed. Baozhu and Sulan drew out a red felt mat and placed it on the floor, setting up two folding chairs, intending to ask Chunhang and Huifang to sit together and receive obeisance. The two of them absolutely refused to sit, so the mat was quickly put away. By now Chunhang and Huifang felt they were outnumbered and simply submitted to the general raillery; to answer back would only invite more. Ziyu felt a deep admiration for Chunhang's free and easy bearing, and paired with Huifang, the two were truly an ice-jar and an autumn moon, each enhancing the other's radiance. He also observed all the beauties in turn, each possessing their own unique loveliness. The only thing that diminished his pleasure was the absence of Qinyan, which left him with an unsettled flutter in his chest.",`Wenze then gave the order for the servants to lay out three tables. He asked: "For today's seating, shall we go by seniority, or shall we place the birthday honorees at the head?" Chunhang and Huifang said together: "We absolutely cannot accept that; seniority is the natural arrangement." Everyone agreed to go by seniority. Wenze arranged the wines, and the center table was first set in order. By age, Cixian was eldest; knowing he could not refuse, he took the seat of honor, with Gao Pin beside him, then Zhongqing. The left-hand table had Ziyun at the head, then Nanxiang, then Ziyu. The right-hand table had Tian Chunhang at the head, then Wang Xun, with Wenze as host. There were three guests at each table.`,"Once the seating was settled, Wang Guibao and Lin Chunxi arrived and were introduced all around. At the center table, Shufang, Yulin, and Chunxi attended; at the left table, Baozhu, Lanbao, and Sulan; at the right table, Huifang and Guibao. The assignments made, everyone took their seats and began to pour slowly, drinking at a leisurely pace. It was truly:",`Scholars of the Jade Continent gather first before the Dragon Gate;
the assembled immortals of the Jasper Isle pay court together at the Golden Palace.

Embroidered hearts and brocaded mouths, pearls from the ninth heaven falling profusely;
moon-shaped countenances and flower-petal skin, at every table the court robes are immaculate.

No less fine than wind-soup and unicorn-dried-meat, drinking the wine that grants long life, wisdom certified through three lifetimes;
no need for jade chimes and cloud music-frames, singing the song that transcends old age, each hoping to grow old together.

The Jade-Capital Gentleman, the Cloud-Feasting Gentleman, the Wind-Riding Gentleman, the Phoenix-Driving Gentleman;
in the crimson dust and the azure beyond, spanning the present life and past lives.

The Eyebrow-Painter, the Gauze-Washer, the Song-Treader, the Lotus-Picker;
iridescent phoenix and brocaded phoenix, manifesting in different forms.

Surpassing the forest-and-mountain hermit who, taking the plum tree as wife and the crane as child, merely counts as romantic;
sufficient to melt a heart of iron and stone, encountering just the flicker of an eye's corner or the curve of a brow, the ice breaks at once.

Guessing games and drinking forfeits, printing your heart against mine;
golden waves and jade-clear liquor, try the lord's lips again against the lady's.

Humor and play at will, turning everything upside down;
brief arias and long songs at the banquet, exhausting all artistry and making white even of brown.

Most are here to borrow the famous flower to express their pride;
with no concern at all for the common people's welfare.

With this they enjoy themselves without limit;
youth has its own time of waiting.

Rightly one feels the Western Garden's elegant gathering possessed only household entertainers;
even the Winding-Stream Cup-Floating had no such intimate companions.`,"As cups and chopsticks flew and sandals and shoes lay scattered, every pleasure of a youthful and refined assembly was savored to the full. Several were already in a state of half-collapsed jade mountains, like crabapple blossoms about to doze. At the tables, everyone's heart was at ease, every face glowing with joy. Only Ziyu kept thinking of Qinyan lying ill in bed, knowing he would be listless and depressed. His medicine-stove half-burned, his green window shut tight; he could not imagine the degree of his tedium. And he knew that Qinyan, aware of today's festive scene, would certainly be yearning for quiet and solitude. How he wished he could slip over there at this moment and offer a few words of comfort, brew tea and add incense, and help restore his spirits. And yet Qinyan might suspect that Ziyu, in the midst of pleasure, had simply forgotten his sorrows. Thinking so during this grand flower gathering, he would feel all the more wretched. To make matters worse, Sulan was right here too; otherwise, he might at least have gone to him for comfort. He sighed: though the scene before his eyes was full of people like clouds, he whom his heart sought was not here. At this moment Ziyu's expression grew somber; he merely pleaded drunkenness and withdrew from his seat to lie down by the heated kang, and no one paid him much attention. Besides, the food and wine had been plentiful and most had drunk beyond their capacity, so each left their seat and scattered to sit where they liked.",`The servants cleared away the remaining dishes and set out fragrant tea and fresh fruit. Chunxi and Guibao went to the Taihu stone rockery and sat chatting by the peony hedge. Yulin and Shufang had drunk themselves into a stupor and lay asleep beneath the crabapple blossoms. Lanbao was fishing by the pond. Baozhu and Huifang were playing chess, with Sulan watching the game, while Nanxiang and Gao Pin stood by offering Baozhu advice. Huifang said: "Three of you against my one; even if you win it is hardly a surprise." Baozhu said: "I can win against you without any coaching at all." Wenze said: "Today we have had real pleasure. It is a pity that two are absent from the flower contingent. The other is no great matter, but first and foremost Qinyan's absence has prevented Gengxiang from being fully at ease." Ziyun said: "Indeed! Qinyan's illness is rather mysterious: listless in spirit, no desire to eat or drink; it has been over ten days with no improvement." Cixian said: "Yesterday I took his pulse, and it appeared to be accumulated fatigue compounded by pent-up emotion and melancholy. Yesterday there were specks of blood in his phlegm; without several months of complete rest he cannot recover."`,`Ziyu, lying on the kang, heard all this clearly and could not help feeling more agitated. Zhongqing said: "Today's occasion cannot be without literature and calligraphy. Let Master Jingyi paint a picture and compose a preface to record this elegant gathering, so that we may attach our names to it." Cixian said: "As for painting a picture, I will offer my services. But as for lofty essays and classic texts, we have the great brushes of all the gentlemen present. The cold and meager words of a mountain recluse would not match the splendor of the Golden Valley, and would only diminish the beauty of the famous flowers." Everyone said: "You are too modest." Ziyun said: "Today's idea arose because of Meixiang, drawing forth the blooming of a hundred flowers, surpassing even the cut silk flowers of the Tang palace. I would like to gaze upon the pearls and jade of all the gentlemen. How about we compose a linked verse first?" Everyone said: "Excellent." Chunhang said: "In the ancient style, or the modern style?" Cixian said: "The modern style is difficult to develop fully, and with so many people it might easily become flat and straightforward. It would be better to use the ancient style."`,`So they proceeded in order of age, with Cixian again taking the lead, followed by Ziyun, then Gao Pin, then Nanxiang, then Wenze, then Zhongqing, then Chunhang, then Wang Xun, and finally Ziyu, making nine people in all. Wang Xun had already roused Ziyu and had him wash his face; Sulan brought out a sobering pill and gave it to Ziyu to ingest. Ziyu felt embarrassed and forced himself to pull it together. Seeing that Ziyu was silent and listless, half-drunk and half-sober, Sulan guessed in his heart that it was because Qinyan had not come. Firstly, there were too many people around to comfort him properly; secondly, he feared that bringing up Qinyan would only stir his feelings further, failing to relieve his mind while adding to his melancholy. So instead he walked away to find others to converse with. Wenze ordered the servant boys to place a small table before each seat, setting out a brush and inkstone and several sheets of patterned paper. When the ink was ground, everyone asked Cixian to pen the opening line. Cixian said: "Let us leave aside the word 'birthday'." He added, "Pardon my presumption!" took up the brush, wrote a line, and read aloud: "Jade trees sing clearly, the orioles in disarray."`,`Everyone listened, wrote their lines, and noted the character 'jing'. Next should be Ziyun; Ziyun said: "After this everyone should write two lines each." He pondered for a moment, then wrote: "Day by day the spring breeze blows but does not scatter them; / The scattering flowers of the heavenly maidens are newly marvelous." Everyone wrote this down too, noting the character 'yun', and all said together: "A very fine continuation. With the opening of the third line, one feels inspiration rising." It came to Gao Pin, who wrote without overthinking: "Cutting silk to make flowers, scattering them across half the sky; / The feeling and features of the flowers grow ever more spirited." Everyone said: "Splendid!" and wrote it down one by one.`,`Nanxiang said: "This line needs to shift the rhyme. These flowers are after all different from real flowers; if we just treat them as flowers, then what kind of flowers were Xi Shi and Taizhen?" So he wrote: "One only feels the flower's heart still lacks a little truth; / The butterflies have males, but who can clearly discern?" Everyone clapped their hands and said: "Superb! With this line the boundary is clearly drawn, saving it from becoming an indistinguishable blur. Zhujun is from start to finish a marvelous talent." Nanxiang said: "I wouldn't dare claim so! I merely recognize the theme clearly." It came to Wenze's turn; Wenze said: "This line must be matched well to form the pivot, otherwise the energy will dissipate. These male butterflies are rather hard to match." He thought deeply about it. Zhongqing said: "Hurry and hand in your paper; outside the musicians are already blowing and striking to open the gates." Wenze said: "I have it. Though the mandarin ducks are small, they are always close." Cixian and Ziyun said: "That is a fine match, both skillful and apt." Nanxiang said: "Credit to him." Wenze laid down his brush; Zhongqing said: "How can one line be enough?" Wenze laughed: "You pushed so hard, I forgot." He thought a moment more and wrote: "The Creator's hand finely selects, carving without flaw." Everyone said: "This line is also well turned out, and shifts the rhyme again." Zhongqing continued: "One by one carved and engraved, laying out eyes and brows; / Exhausting the Dragon Palace's ten pecks of pearls." It was Chunhang's turn; he continued: "Cutting a pair of jade branches from the azure sea; / Little Jade feels a flash of anger, Green Jade feels sorrow." Everyone praised again: "Good! It lifts the theme clearly again." Next was Wang Xun; he thought for a bit and wrote: "The jade-like person also fears usurping a thousand autumns; / Chanjuan seems to have stolen Chang'e's elixir." Everyone was just about to praise it when Gao Pin said: "This line scolds too viciously; surely not every one of them is like the rabbit in the moon palace?" Everyone burst out laughing, and Wang Xun felt rather uneasy. The beauties all scolded Gao Pin: "It is only him, who never has a good word to say in his whole life; in the future we must punish him by making him a mute."`,`Gao Pin said: "How strange. Other people scold you all, and I defend your honor, yet you scold me. Truly no good deed goes unpunished." It was now Ziyu's turn; Ziyu's mind was on Qinyan, feeling hollow and listless, as everyone well understood. Though Ziyu tried his utmost to conceal it, his inspiration was at a low ebb, and he had no choice but to strain and write:

"A glance in passing, that jade-girl's eyes turned back once;
luan-comb sweeping gently across cloudy-green tresses."

Chunhang said: "This takes us to the dressing scene, so the continuation will need to describe things finely." Ziyu at this moment was thinking of the expression Qinyan had worn while performing 'Waking from a Dream,' which accounted for the line 'that jade-girl's eyes turned back once.' The others did not understand why. They only saw that his spirits were poor and his mind not really on the poetry, so the lines came out somewhat flat and diffuse. It was again Cixian's turn; he wrote:

"In the mirror, the lotus face, fresh from sleep;
the charming voice forms the white-ramie song."

Next came Ziyun:

"Lithe and pliant, knowing how to sing the red-gauze melody;
a clear and nimble beauty, touched by chance, the soul is lost."

Gao Pin said: "'The soul is lost'; alas, what can be done?" And wrote:

"In the Bronze Sparrow's deep spring, the greater and lesser Qiao;
where flowers are joined on a single branch, the sisters."

Nanxiang said: "Fine as it is, that Bronze Sparrow line is a little muddled." He matched it immediately:

"Jade-like, a pair of discs, surely Qiongyao and jasper;
slender waist swaying into the Magic Harmony willow."

Everyone praised: "This 'sister flowers,' 'Qiongyao and jade', the match is excellent. The poem's energy has been pulled back into shape." Wenze then continued:

"A city-toppling beauty without equal in the world;
melts even this world's heart of iron and stone."

Everyone said: "Wonderful! This line needs a match of equal force." Zhongqing thought a moment, then laughed slightly and wrote:

"Spring returns on earth, to the tottering old man."

Chunhang said: "That is truly a marvelous match." Then he saw the next line:

"How a single smile gives birth to a hundred charms."

He continued:

"At the banquet, silks compete, gold lightly tossed;
Zheng Yingtao, truly so remarkable."`,`Wang Xun matched:

"Feng Zidu, his fame no empty claim;
slowly the long days pass in the early summer."

Wenze said: "Feng Zidu. Nowadays there is a Feng Zipei; they seem like brothers." Ziyun said: "Feng Zipei is actually not bad; he has one particular trait: he simply refuses to take pleasure among the flower company." Wang Lanbao said with a cold laugh: "Of course he won't join our crowd; he actually gets annoyed when he sees us." Ziyu said: "Why so?" Guibao cut in: "He has his own feelings about it." Ziyu continued:

"Brocade seat and flower banquet, day turns easily to night;
in splendor and beauty, worthy of the 'Same Carriage' ode."

The second round came to Cixian, who wrote:

"Yuan and Bai's poems, originally formed amid lotus gatherings;
on the red felt stage-carpet, passion and sentiment in abundance."

Ziyun continued:

"Practiced in singing 'Lady Ding's Ten Laments';
the plain and beautiful, no humble part left unremarked."

Gao Pin said: "Wonderful! For this line let me produce a good match." The young beauties, hearing this, suspected another jest at their expense and all crowded around to watch what Gao Pin was writing.`,`Gao Pin, smiling, wrote out slowly:

"Rain and clouds, one travels all the way to Zhong'a."

Everyone burst out laughing, and the beauties all spat at him and pummeled him in a flurry. Ziyun laughed: "It is my fault, for I provoked that line from him." Nanxiang said: "Even as playful writing, it should not be so cutting. Change one character and it can be saved; change 'traveled' to 'how could'." The beauties finally agreed to this. Gao Pin said: "Very well, the wrath of the multitude must not be incurred." He then wrote:

"Heaven-born loveliness, to be cherished and preserved."

Nanxiang said: "A brigand reciting scripture, a butcher becoming Buddha. Zhuoran has actually developed a kind heart and knows how to cherish! That too is remarkable." He continued:

"Let not the flower branch be lightly cast aside;
would that we could, like Wang Xian, buy a Taogen."

Wenze linked:

"How laughable that Wang Rong bored through the plum pits."

Zhongqing laughed: "Here we go again, straying into impurity; your hearts still have something unclean in them." Then he saw what Wenze had written next:

"Once heaven gives birth to a fine jade youth."

Zhongqing linked:

"Must heaven and earth make the yin and yang wrong?
Only heard of the female rainbow becoming a divine maiden."

Everyone said: "This is cautionary and admonishing verse, not vicious mockery. Such things do indeed exist in this world, though not among us." Chunhang linked:

"Let not the male wind turn into a great king;
in the painted hall, the fine banquet is held day by day."

Everyone laughed again. Gao Pin said: "Poetry expresses the will; the one who ties the bell must untie it. If I were to write this one, it would be wrong again." Wang Xun's turn came, and he said: "We can close now, so let it fall to Gengxiang to write the conclusion." He wrote:

"Through the fan-base, peering at my lord, half a face glimpsed;
picking up the piece of moonlight, a single bright sliver."

Everyone exclaimed in praise: "Fine! It is time to conclude, and this conclusion is no small achievement. The closing lines must be able to hold together the whole poem." Ziyu thought for a moment and wrote:

"Snowflakes fly up on the jade branches bright."

Everyone praised it unanimously as a powerful close, able to carry the whole poem in a single breath.`,`Cixian made a fair copy and read through it several times aloud: "It truly flows in one unbroken breath, with no sign of stitching or joint." He said: "Tomorrow I will paint a 'Picture of the Hundred Flowers Competing in Beauty', how would that be?" Everyone responded enthusiastically: "Wonderful! Why don't we now make a comparison between the people and the flowers, fairly assessed, without personal bias." So they deliberated together and decided: Baozhu as the peony; Huifang as the herbaceous peony; Sulan as the lotus; Yulin as the green peach blossom; Shufang as the crabapple; Lanbao as the rose; Guibao as the cotton rose. Chunxi, small in stature yet richly talented and beloved by all, was assigned the orchid. The eight assignments all felt fitting. They then wondered what flowers Qinyan and Qiguan should be. Ziyun said: "Qinyan's art and beauty are exceptional, yet his temperament is exceedingly cool. Likening him to the plum blossom is most apt; and since his passionate love of the plum is not for Gengxiang's sake, then whose?" Everyone agreed: "Quite right." Gao Pin said: "Only I fear that Lin Hejing would not consent, for Gengxiang would have taken away his leather-shod little son."`,`Ziyu could not help blushing. Zhongqing said: "And Qiguan?" Ziyun said: "Qiguan's temperament is fierce and upright, his appearance extremely fine; he somewhat lacks a gentle, flowing charm. Let him be likened to the chrysanthemum." Gao Pin said: "Chrysanthemums vary in variety: some white, some yellow, some red, some purple. The white ones are fine, but the rest tend to give a feeling of austere autumnal severity. Qiguan, though his temperament is fierce, has a tenderness in him that is quite winning. Better to liken him to the apricot blossom." Everyone agreed: "A fine apricot blossom, perfectly fitting." Wenze said: "Speaking of chrysanthemums, yellow or white, do you all know that in the eastern garden a new courtesan has recently arrived, called White Chrysanthemum? Have you heard anything about her?" Everyone said they had not.`,`Gao Pin said: "There is nothing in the world that I cannot find out. I know that she came from Guangxi with a company commander who brought her to the capital; the commander has now abandoned her and left the city, so she ended up in the pleasure quarter. She is actually rather fine-looking in a pure, unadorned way, hence the refined nickname. But people from the two Guangs seldom bind their feet; they all have natural six-inch feet, smooth and round, with a double-footed gait, walking like the wind. As a person, she is exceedingly vivacious and charming." Zhongqing said: "That explains your official title of Inspector-General of Intelligence from All Quarters." Everyone laughed again. Ziyun said: "After today's gathering, such a magnificent feast will be hard to convene again. On the eighteenth, Yaoqing is moving to new lodgings; would all the gentlemen be willing to move the wine-jars there for another gathering?" Everyone agreed: "There is absolutely no reason not to come. Anyone who fails to attend will be fined to host a day's feast, and we'll have another gathering." Baozhu said: "I fear I don't have that standing; surely not everyone will come on my account." Chunhang said: "Why would they not? And besides, you are the king of flowers, and these assembled blossoms will naturally come to pay homage. As for us flower-admirers, when we encounter beauty as striking as national-fragrance and heavenly-perfume, how could we not be eager and enthusiastic?" Nanxiang said: "Leave it to me; if one person fails to come, I'll be fined to host a day; two people, two days' hosting." Baozhu said: "Truly? After tomorrow's wine wears off, don't tell me you'll have forgotten." Only Ziyu sat in silence. The others went on talking and laughing; the bright moon was now directly overhead, the red lanterns about to burn out, and it was past the third watch.`,'Cixian said: "The night is already deep, so we can disperse." So everyone rose; Baozhu confirmed the date of the eighteenth, everyone agreed, come rain or shine. Then they each mounted their carriages and scattered in all directions. The next day, Huifang went around to call and express thanks; only Ziyu was ill and did not receive him.',"On the eighteenth, all the celebrated gentlemen and the celebrated young dan actors did indeed come to Baozhu's new lodgings, from the wu hour until the zi hour. That day the entertainment was confined to drinking games and guessing forfeits, clear singing and clapper-beat, and they dispersed having fully enjoyed themselves. Among them Ziyu did not come owing to illness. Zhang Zhongyu was added to the company, always the most eager to please in a festive scene.",`Qiguan was added to the flower register; only Qinyan had still not recovered. Gao Pin and Wenze held Nanxiang to what he had said: "If one guest is absent, I'll be fined to host a day." Since Ziyu did not come that day, Nanxiang was fined a day of hosting; Nanxiang was thoroughly happy to oblige. Another day's gathering was held at his residence. Only Ziyu and Qinyan had both not yet recovered. Truly: a few plum blossoms, tender and about to fall; the moon has dipped once more below the bamboo bridge in the west. As for what happened next, let the following chapter tell the tale.`]},y=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations17:s},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),h={18:["Qinyan's illness lingered and he remained listless, keeping his door closed to visitors; only a few close friends from the same troupe came by from time to time to offer comfort. Baozhu and Sulan also passed on word from Ziyu, recounting his recent days and explaining his inability to visit often, yet urging Qinyan to take the utmost care of his health. Hearing these messages, Qinyan only grew more heavy-hearted. Since falling ill, he had gone nowhere at all; even Yiyuan he had long since ceased to visit. But his teacher Changqing depended on him as a money tree, and with Qinyan ill for so long and unable to receive clients, Changqing had lost a good deal of his enthusiasm.","Now, those who made their career as teachers of theatrical troupes were originally dan role performers themselves; over thirty years their lives went through four transformations.",`What were the four transformations? In their youth, they possessed a lovely bearing and were cherished by all. When the undifferentiated was breached and the two positives met, people called them "rabbits." After the age of twenty, the body would grow larger, the looks would coarsen, yet they still flirted and primped, wearing splendid headgear and fine clothes. When performing on stage, they cast aside all shame to display the utmost seductive wantonness. They twisted and minced, constantly striving to captivate souls and draw men's essence, earning them the name of "foxes." After thirty, the voice had gone hoarse, the beard had appeared, and acting was no longer possible; with no other recourse, they would put on the look of a market-town roughneck, buy some boys, train them for a year or so, and then send them out to earn money. Those who were well-favored earned more money and were treated almost like a father. Those of ordinary looks who could not charm people and could not earn were nagged morning and evening, scolded for a day's absence from entertaining clients and beaten for two days' absence. When the boy's apprenticeship was up, the teacher would open his mouth and demand three or five thousand cash; once the money was in hand, he'd send the boy out the door as bare and empty as he'd come in, without even an old garment to his name. If the teacher had no wife, he would require the disciple to share his bed at night. Such ferocious ruffians, more dreadful than a wild tiger by several degrees, were at this stage appropriately compared to "tigers."`,'Finally, when their luck ran out, they could only linger in the troupe carrying flags and playing bit-parts, reducing them to the status of "dogs." Such was the standard life-portrait of a troupe teacher. Four months had passed since Qinyan arrived in the capital the previous twelfth month. In that time, Xu Ziyun had already spent several thousand taels of silver. This was no small sum, and it explained why Changqing treated Qinyan exceptionally well.',"Should Qinyan remain ill for a year and a half, one might fear Changqing's heart would change. However, this remained mere speculation by others, and we shall let it rest for now.","Now let us speak of Wei Pincai, who had entered the Hua mansion full of expectation, imagining he would immediately make his mark. Who could have known that after a month inside, the host and guest had still not come face to face? The several times he had asked for an audience, he was put off with the excuse that the young master was busy. Those he did meet and mingle with were all dubious characters. There was also that class of arrogant household servants who put on great airs: they would meet him with merely a perfunctory bow, openly take the superior seat, and speak in nothing but muddled nonsense. The young ones were full of bluster and pretense of understanding; the old ones traded on their seniority and were thoroughly devious. Pincai tried his utmost to get along with everyone and attended to all social obligations. He dared not offend even a dog in the Hua household, all in the hope of securing some favorable outcome. But unknown to him, the losses were mounting steadily. The household had several hundred friends, private secretaries, and servants; hardly a day passed without some occasion arising. Once accustomed to this social round, one could not be selective about whom to flatter. Whenever there was a celebration of any kind, one had to contribute a share. Occasionally, three or four friends would gather together and generate some matter, whether it involved splitting a meal bill or gambling away a stake. Then there were the young lads, the coachmen, the grooms, the cooks, and the like, who came regularly to extract small gratuities; fail to comply and gossip would spread at once. Though only a month had passed, he had already become mixed up with all the idle hangers-on of the household. Some proved congenial, while others did not. The congenial ones included Gu Yueqing, Zhang Xiaomei, and their sort, whereas the uncongenial ones were men like Yan Jian'an and Wang Qingyun. Pincai could only attend to his own station, keeping close to the congenial ones while distancing himself from the uncongenial. But what truly vexed him was that he had still not had a proper meeting with the young master, despite having been there a month. Meanwhile, he had nothing useful to do, merely mixing with the crowd day after day. Truly: two meals of old rice, one pillow of dark and sweet sleep.","On this particular day, he went out for a stroll, came out of the city gate, and found the scene much livelier than inside the city, with carriages flowing like water, horses like dragons. Following the road, he came to Mei's residence on Mingke Alley, went in and found Ziyu still ill in bed, listless and low-spirited. Ziyu asked how Pincai was doing; Pincai could only speak positively, rattling off a few easy lies. He then went to pay his respects to Lady Yan and express his thanks. Coming out to find Li Yuanmao, he discovered his door locked. He took his leave of Ziyu again and went out, feeling lonely and desolate with nowhere to go.",'Wandering aimlessly to the edge of the Tiger-Subduing Bridge (Fuhu Qiao), he recalled that Zhang Zhongyu lived at the Wu residence nearby. He inquired at the gatehouse and, as luck would have it, Zhongyu was at home and invited him in to sit. After a few pleasantries and a serving of tea, Zhongyu soon spoke: "Now that my little brother is in the city, it must be hard to get out often. Why not find a place to sit and watch some opera to dispel the tedium?" Pincai replied: "Very well. I have actually been quite worn out these past two days, so if we are going, let us go." So the two of them went together to the theater, found seats, and watched two or three plays. Some young protégés came by to chat with them. Across the way, on the lower level of the opposite gallery, Pincai spotted Li Yuanmao together with Sun Sihui. Pincai went over, exchanged a few words, and came back.',`Zhongyu remarked: "Those two brothers-in-law are a pair of born good-for-nothings if ever there was one. What is the point of looking after them?" The plays that day felt stale and old-fashioned, making Zhongyu restless enough to say: "Let us go." He paid for the seats, and together with Pincai went up to a wine-house for a small drink and a talk. Observing that Pincai appeared in poor spirits and lacked his usual demeanor, Zhongyu asked: "I heard you have entered the Hua family mansion. Given the grandeur of that establishment, and that the young master Hua loves social connections, things must naturally have taken a great turn for the better lately." Pincai said: "If elder brother hadn't asked, I would not have brought it up myself. At first, Third Master Fu told me that the young master Hua held the reputation of Lord Mengchang, keeping hundreds of retainers at his table. I went in to find plenty of retainers indeed, yet they are all thoroughly mercenary people, constantly plotting to squeeze one out or cut another down. I have been inside for over a month, and the young master continues to be cool and distant. If this goes on much longer, I fear I have misread the situation entirely." Zhongyu said: "How many times have you met the young master?" Pincai said: "I've met him a few times, but only for a few casual words before he moves away. He has many old familiars, leaving a newcomer naturally unable to push through." Zhongyu was silent for a good while, then sighed: "In the world today, if you want to maintain your self-respect and dignity, you can only stay home behind closed doors. To move within the circles of wealth and influence requires freshly drilling your speech and comportment, which is itself no easy matter. At the highest level stood two men whom we could never emulate: the former worthy Chen Meigong, and Li Liweng, the author of the 'Ten Kinds of Songs'. Both possessed learning second to none. When unfavorable fate prevented them from obtaining official positions to render great service to the state, they assumed the roles of lofty recluses. In doing so, they deployed all their lifetime's learning to navigate the gates of the powerful. They relied on a few brushstrokes of painting and calligraphy, a few poems and essays, to make princes and lords change their expression and the courtly world run toward them; even those great officials had to flatter them in return. Had they offended someone, they could have been cut down anywhere, so naturally they relied on an unshakeable skill. Isn't that formidable? Those at the highest level, nowadays, are gone. As for the second tier, it would be hard to find one even when selecting from a wide pool. Their ten essential qualities are:`,`Pincai said: "What are the ten qualities?" Zhongyu said: "A full measure of amiability; a second-tier degree of talent; three jin's capacity for wine; four-seasons appropriate dress; mastery of the five musical scales; an official rank of the sixth grade; skill at the seven-syllable verse; ability to cast the net eight ways; comprehension of the nine streams of learning; and perfect ease in all social occasions." Pincai shook his head: "So many requirements?" Zhongyu replied: "After each, one must append an additional word: the word 'not'! One must maintain amiability without ever changing, possess talent without ever showing it off, and hold a capacity for wine without ever getting drunk. One needs seasonal dress without ever pawning it, a mastery of scales without ever making an error, and the rank of the sixth grade without ever actually serving. One must command the seven-syllable verse without ever going rusty, cast the net eight ways without ever letting a thread drop, comprehend all nine streams without ever running short, and achieve perfect social ease without ever appearing common." Pincai said: "On that account, being a person becomes very difficult indeed. I don't have a single one of these. How could I learn all of them?" Zhongyu said: "Actually, one needn't have all of them; a few will do for social management. Moreover, each person has their own timing and fortune, but one does need to have some genuine skill of one's own, so that people look up to you." Pincai said: "And the third tier?" Zhongyu said: "The third tier also has its seven-character formula. The first is 'tong'." Pincai asked the meaning of this, and Zhongyu laughed: "If you have passed the first examination level, you have naturally read some books. You know a little about the refinements, which already elevates you above market-town roughnecks. The second quality is 'half-passable'. It means knowing how to be obsequious, buttering up those inside and below. It involves the servility of a slave while addressing others as brother, pulling on the family façade and leaning on the ancestors, boring through cracks to fill holes, and drumming up the draughty take. That constitutes the third tier." Pincai remarked: "Do not look down on the third tier. I suspect nowadays everyone belongs to the third tier." Zhongyu agreed: "Is that not so! In my view, it follows no fixed pattern. Even someone with all ten qualities may fail to get anywhere, while someone lacking a single one of the seven characters might stumble onto an opportunity. In the end it's all each person's own fate. As the saying goes: 'When luck arrives, the wind carries you to the Prince of Teng's Pavilion; when luck retreats, thunder smashes the Jianfu stele.' I know that this young master Hua is an easy person to deal with. Just look at how many people pass through his household and manage to extract all sorts of benefits. Let me give you a method: to get on his good side is not so difficult. I also know this person, as he once entrusted me with some matters.`,`"I know there is someone in his household called Lin Shanzhi, his personal attendant." He put up a thumb as he spoke: "He is a person of this caliber. His word is followed, his plan is adopted, and he never leaves the young master's side. You must first work through this connection; once that gate is opened, everything else becomes easy. There is also that group of eight young pages always at the young master's elbow. Children lack great discernment, so offering them a small advantage is enough. Slowly, word by word, blow it into his ears. Today he hears that Master Wei is fine, and tomorrow he hears it again, until the sentiment works its way into the young master's heart. This strategy is known as 'releasing the leashed sparrow.' You let it out on fifty or sixty feet of string, knowing that in the end, it will always be reeled back in. Just don't begrudge the small initial outlay." Pincai nodded and said: "I am much obliged and truly grateful!" Zhongyu continued: "For instance, once you've established a connection with the young master, observe what his temperament is, what pleases him and what he dislikes, and naturally follow his preferences.`,`"Follow them to nine parts in ten, but always hold back one part for later rather than spending it all too quickly. Consider the handling of a wayward horse. If you wish to manage it, going head-on against its nature proves absolutely impossible. Instead, let it run until it is completely exhausted, and then a single pull of the reins will turn it around. Or take this case: if someone can think through a matter to nine parts, you must think it through to ten. That one extra part constitutes the art of turning the wayward horse, which is known as 'reining in the wayward horse.'`,`"Then there are those who have all sorts of faults and do not mix with people at all. They just stay in their rooms with their wives and concubines and enjoy themselves in isolation. With those, too, you must discover one or two things they enjoy; then meet those preferences, offer small tokens of loyalty and faithfulness, and never ask them for anything. Naturally they will set their minds at ease, saying: 'That man has a bit of genuine sincerity, and he's not just trying to exploit me.' Once they've swallowed that bait, you can manage them however you like. This is what's called 'fishing for the golden cicada.' As for one's general manner, though one must be amiable, one also cannot be spineless through and through. Those who have no relevance and serve no purpose, such as Yan Jian'an and Wang Qingyun, don't bother with them; mingling indiscriminately with them serves no purpose at all. Among the household retinue, there are those who cannot say a single useful word inside, yet are masters at bamboozling outsiders. You must see clearly for yourself: those worth attending to, attend to; those not worth attending to, don't attend to. If you attend to those who are useless, the ones who matter will look down on you." Pincai burst out laughing when he heard all this: "Elder brother is truly the foremost great talent of our time. Chen Ping's stratagems and Zhuge Liang's plans are no more than this. You can describe the temperament and character of every sort of person in the world as if you had written them on the palm of your hand. Your little brother ought to come to you with a student's card and pay formal respects as a disciple, so that the method of the heart may be properly transmitted." Zhongyu laughed: "I've already told you everything. What is there left to bow to a teacher for? Follow what I have said, and it is guaranteed not to go wrong. In the future, if good things come to you, do not forget your teacher. That is all the good conscience a student needs." With that, both laughed, and before they knew it, half the day had passed. Zhongyu settled the bill and they came out together. Zhongyu said: "Little brother, go back into the city now. I have some business to attend to and cannot keep you company." With that, he bowed and departed. It was still early. As Pincai walked along the way, he spotted Sihui and Yuanmao in the distance, just rounding a corner ahead. Pincai thought: "Where are they going?" and quietly followed. He entered a small alleyway packed with idle onlookers, all crowded at someone's doorway peering in. Pincai had heard people say that the East Garden was where prostitutes gathered, so he joined the crowd and stood watching. In one household, a thatched-wall mud enclosure containing two thatched rooms, he saw Sihui and Yuanmao standing just ahead of him. They were looking at two women seated on a long bench. Both were around twenty years old, fat-faced and full-bellied, their hair greased and their faces powdered, wearing rather showy clothing. He saw one woman say to Sihui: "Come in and sit." She laughed and simpered, making Sihui and Yuanmao itch with desire. They hovered on the edge of entering but not quite entering, staring in a daze. He also saw an awkward-looking man over forty squatting on the ground, wearing a short jacket belted at the waist. A large pouch hung from it, big enough to hold two strings of cash. Then from behind the curtain walked out a woman of around twenty or so, rather good-looking. She had an oval face with a charming scattering of freckles, her hair done in a T-style knot with her temples soft and loose, a flower tucked in her hair. She wore plain clothes, and on her feet she had a pair of pointed-toe "four-happiness bats" high-soled shoes with pile ornaments. She sat down on the bench and chatted with the other two. Her speech sounded different from northern speakers, more like a southerner. From head to toe she possessed an appealing prettiness noticeably different from the others. He heard one of the plainer-looking women begin to sing:

"Fine young lord,
Every day at the gate you stare and stare,
Your staring sets my heart astir,
Your longing has blurred your very eyes.
Wait a while.
Cloud and rain on Mount Wu, done in an instant,
All for a mere two hundred copper cash."

Pincai found this amusing, and then thought: though it is a crude and lewd ditty, the feeling expressed is truthful. He listened again: the homely one had turned and was singing directly at Sihui and Yuanmao:`,`"One of them has a pockmarked face, and the other has bad eyes,
A blind-eyed hen paired with a mangy toad.
You like me, and I like him. Do not make such a fuss.
In the tender quarter, you are clearly not an old hand."`,`While the bystanders could not make out the meaning, Pincai understood perfectly that she was insulting those two. He nearly burst out laughing and had to hold it in. He looked again at the good-looking one, who appeared to be a newcomer. In the capital, a prostitute wanting to enter a proper establishment would first spend a few days in the East Garden or the West Depot to get a sense of the market. Naturally, she would soon lose all sense of shame and become practiced and worldly. Likewise, those no longer of use in the better houses were demoted to these two places. This was none other than the White Chrysanthemum (Bai Juhua) that Gao Pin had spoken of, newly arrived from Guangxi. Watching her behavior, Pincai could see she still had a measure of bashfulness about her. A small boy came up beside her and offered a pipa lute. She took it and played a suite of "Wang Zhaojun's Lament" (昭君怨), causing the crowd at the doorway to grow even more numerous.`,`Yuanmao, being shortsighted, pushed straight through into the doorway and stood there staring. Pincai watched the woman play and sing:

"The willow branch, the willow branch,
In years past, within the palace, we vied in slender waists.
Now cast aside to grow at the roadside,
Kingfisher brows knotted, lamenting the crossroads.
Where is the painted boat moored?
The fine horse neighs into the wind.
I wait in vain for the harbinger of spring to come down the path in the second month,
And have become only the withered wood of autumn in the west wind."

Tightening the strings a notch, tuning again, one key higher, she sang on:

"To think that once we were mandarin ducks and paired;
Today we are the stars Shen and Shang, never meeting.
And so it is that a dew-and-morning marriage does not last long.
Through ten thousand mountains and rivers I came to this place;
Two separated phoenixes gaze at each other in vain from afar.
I lament that for a beauty with ill-starred fate, there is seldom a good ending;
Even to hold the pipa again would weep the bowels dry.
I think of my love,
Seven feet tall, the bearing of a heavenly god.
A captain of a thousand, in command of a hundred,
From south to north of Dongting Lake, his fame was great.
A kindly father and loving mother,
One tender while on the Li River.
And now he has abandoned me, this slight, solitary, friendless girl.
Truly I have become a single spray of late chrysanthemum braving the autumn frost.
At Shi Gong Dam, the sorrow pierces the heart;
At Huamei Pond, the wasted body nearly perished.
Through all of Xiang and Yuan, the three rivers and nine rivers,
I only hoped to catch up, to welcome the twin oars on Tao-gen and Tao-ye.
Who knew that from Chu's end to Wu's beginning the sky stretched endlessly long?
Then passing Jinling where the royal air had not fully yielded,
Looking toward Yangzhou from the lamplight at Guazhou,
Crossing the Yellow River,
Dreading to see the three-dam current raging day and night,
Through Huai, Xu, Ji, and Yan, I had no heart to admire the scenery.
Mercifully, all the way safe and sound I came to the imperial capital.
I only wonder where that fickle young man has hidden.
I am Zhao Wuniang who cut her hair to search for her husband;
Do not mistake me for a common street-walker on the road to Handan."

As she played and sang, her voice filled with grief, moving Pincai to tears. He thought: "This person truly is a woman of deep feeling. As she recounts her whole life's suffering, I wonder where that heartless man has gone." Then he heard Sun Sihui say: "Alas, alas. The things on my body are now completely empty. How vexing! How vexing!" He stamped his foot and sighed: "Ha! A gentleman has no cause to remove his jade from his person, yet it has been rolled up and carried away in his bosom. From now on I shall be as in mourning without my belt-ornament and see what he can do about that!" Yuanmao said: "These pickpockets in the capital are truly hateful. Last year I took ten taels of silver from my father for Wei senior Pincai to take me to the theater. At the theater gate I stumbled, and someone helped me up and dusted me off. I still thought him a good man, but when I looked later, the silver was gone. When my father found out afterward, he scolded me roundly for a full day. Now you see what these dog-things do to people." The listeners could not help smirking inwardly. Sun Sihui said: "'That lovely person, a true lord's desire to match.' Why do you not hasten your steps and enter?" Yuanmao laughed: "Not me. With ten pairs of eyes watching, how could I go in?" Pincai heard this and gave an involuntary laugh. Yuanmao recognized the voice as very familiar, and looked around in all directions; spotting Pincai, he flushed crimson and pushed his way out with Sihui to greet him. Sihui said: "Elder Brother Wei, I know that you are now a crafty three-burrow rabbit, fully installed in the public gate with a bow and formal step. You do not even bother to look in on old friends any more. Today, having unexpectedly encountered you, my heart is glad." Pincai said: "Occupied with all manner of affairs, I had been meaning to come and pay my respects. The day has gotten late and I must return to the city." Yuanmao said: "How are you getting on these days in the Hua household? Are you going back into the city now?" Pincai said: "I'm heading back now." Yuanmao said: "We are also heading back, so let us walk together." As they chatted along the way, Pincai asked: "Just now, when you heard them singing, did you understand what they were singing?" Yuanmao said: "Not a word. I was rather taken by that plump woman. She kept smiling and beckoning at me, but I did not dare go in and sit down."`,`Sihui said: "Splendid, splendid! Cheap in price and little work required. Tomorrow I shall take you along and give it a try, cousin. If we delay our journey, the nimble-footed may get there first, and though we repent it will be too late. As long as one finds true pleasure, why mind ten pairs of watching eyes?" Pincai, hearing him still mouthing his pedantic bookish phrases and talking absolute nonsense, suppressed his laughter and let it pass. At the crossroads they went their separate ways. Behind Pincai came the rumbling of a carriage passing at speed; Pincai quickly stepped aside, and saw that seated in the carriage was the woman who had just been playing and singing, with an old woman sitting on the carriage's running board. It went rushing past like the wind.`,"But let us set aside Pincai's side of the story.","As for this White Chrysanthemum, she hailed from Wuzhou Prefecture in Guangxi and was extraordinarily good-looking. She had been married to a man named Song, a shiftless fellow. Chrysanthemum was by nature good at making connections, and she had come to know a military officer surnamed Zhang, a native of Huguang. The two became very intimate in Guangxi and vowed to grow old together, a relationship that endured for several years. Last year this Officer Zhang was sent on a duty assignment to the capital. White Chrysanthemum, being a person of feeling and loyalty, set off after him as soon as he left, taking some travel funds and a small maidservant.","Somehow she took the wrong road and ended up going all the way out of Guangxi Province and into Hunan, and still had not caught up with him; she did not know how far he had gone ahead; moreover her travel money was exhausted, she knew no one in this strange place, and she was trapped with no way forward or back, so she settled in Hunan. Then she received false information that this Officer Zhang had been stationed in the capital as a company commander and could not leave the capital.","So she sold some of her clothing for travel funds, boarded a convenient boat, and headed for the capital. When she arrived, Officer Zhang had long since completed his assignment and returned; as a result, Chrysanthemum found herself stranded in the capital, and had no choice but to lean on her doorway and smile for a living.","The woman who had come to fetch her today was from the Tao household, an establishment keeper. This Madam Tao had three girls in her house; the best of them, known as Jade Celestial Fairy (Yu Tianxian), was a native of Yangzhou, born vivacious and coquettishly attractive. These past two days she had received a big-spending patron, none other than Xi Eleven from Guangdong. Madam Tao had investigated his background and learned that he was from a wealthy family in Hainan with assets in the millions. His ten brothers all held posts as circuit intendants and prefects, their father was a currently serving brigade commander, and the family ran a foreign trading firm. She had also learned that he was a thoroughly naive big spender and plotted to make a large profit from him. Unfortunately, none of her girls could understand much of his speech. Xi Eleven was moreover addicted to opium, smoking one or two liang a day. Although the three girls could take a few puffs of white-earth tobacco, they were laid low by a few fractions of the black-earth variety. Furthermore, neither side's speech was very intelligible to the other.","As a result, Xi Eleven was not greatly pleased. Madam Tao knew that Chrysanthemum hailed from Guangxi and was also very good-looking. Confident that Chrysanthemum could hold his interest, Madam Tao brought her over and formally adopted her as a foster daughter. At once new clothing was found for her, and she was introduced to all her foster sisters. She and Jade Celestial Fairy became especially fond of each other. After suffering such terrible hardships, Chrysanthemum felt as if she had escaped hell, leaving her with nothing to complain about. With one heart she awaited the coming of Xi Eleven.",`Now, this Xi Eleven had been in the capital for less than half a year, yet he had already spent tens of thousands of taels of silver, dumping it all into a cesspool. Someone advised him to take a concubine. Being a habitual wanderer, he found respectable women of good families most disagreeable; he hunted for someone among the prostitutes, but found no one to his taste. One day Madam Tao came to invite him, saying she had a new arrival from Guangxi at her establishment. Xi Eleven, hearing she was from Guangxi, was filled with joy; he called a small page boy and had him bring the opium equipment, then, making no use of a carriage, strode off on foot in a grand manner. When he arrived at the Tao household, Madam Tao came out first to greet him and spent some time fawning and flattering with the most eager of shoulder-hunched smiles, then said: "Come quickly and bring out Fourth Miss." Not long afterward, White Chrysanthemum came out, graceful and swaying, fragrant in her attire, a spring-blossom expression on her face. She came forward to meet him, and they exchanged a few words, finding their speech perfectly matched to each other's. Xi Eleven looked at her appearance: she was beautiful as a flower, pliant as water, sweet as honey, sticky as candy. He was enormously pleased; after a few brief questions they went into the room together. He had his page arrange the opium equipment properly, lit the lamp, and smoked and chatted. Xi Eleven was a man who took big pulls of opium. Chrysanthemum prepared his pipe, beginning from half a fen per pull and gradually building up to three fen per pull before he was satisfied. Chrysanthemum's skill in preparing opium was excellent, as she could produce it neither too raw nor too well-cooked. Xi Eleven also liked to smoke the "noodle-style" opium. She would take the opium and twist it into a narrow rod on the needle, holding it over the flame and turning it on all four sides until it hung in a string about five inches long. She would place it at the pipe-bowl opening, and Xi Eleven would take a great shuā-shuā-shuā pull to draw it all in at once. He would then hold his mouth closed so that not a single wisp of smoke escaped, a practice that remained his lifelong supreme technique. Chrysanthemum also took a few puffs, then nestled into Xi Eleven's arms and continued preparing the pipe for him. Xi Eleven smoked seven or eight qian in all, which proved enough. His ardor surged up, and the two of them put away the lamp, closed the door, and enacted a secret drama. Their encounter resembled the butterfly's love of the flower or mandarin ducks turned upside down, displaying manifold subtleties beyond description. One was as fierce as a tiger bounding down from the mountain, while the other was as ripe as a melon dropping from the vine. They carried on for two full hours before each was fully satisfied and content; then tidied themselves up, relit the lamp and smoked more opium, followed by drinking wine and eating food.`,"Xi Eleven stayed there seven or eight days running. Each day he spent several dozen strings of cash, and the madam was already sharpening her ax. He paid for clothing on the person, jewelry to make, and furnishing for the room, all of which went without saying. Then all the girls started wanting this and wanting that. Even the madam herself, the hangers-on, and the general crowd put on their act, every single one of them. But Xi Eleven was by nature free and generous, never stingy.","Chrysanthemum saw that Xi Eleven's powerful and vigorous manner surpassed her previous love by twice over. Knowing he was a great gentleman waiting in the capital for an official appointment, she conceived the desire to become a proper wife. Xi Eleven had originally come searching for a concubine, and seeing Chrysanthemum's great beauty and her expert skills, he too wanted to buy her as a concubine. But the madam was not entirely willing. Chrysanthemum had only been there a few days, was not actually her person, and there was no purchase price to extract. The madam wanted to keep her in the house to earn more money. If the girl went off to make a proper marriage, would all that effort not be wasted? So she worked to stir up trouble between them. To Chrysanthemum she said Xi Eleven was a heartless man who had dozens of concubines at home. She claimed to have heard from his second brother that once a woman was brought home, she was immediately forgotten as he went off chasing new ones, marking him as a man of the new-is-exciting, old-is-discarded type. She warned that a woman absolutely must not marry such a man and told Chrysanthemum not to make the wrong decision. To Xi Eleven she said that Chrysanthemum still had a husband living and was unwilling to be sold. She also said that Chrysanthemum's nature was difficult, and having grown accustomed to this way of life, she could not settle to propriety. She promised that if the lord wanted to take a concubine, she could guarantee to find him a perfect one, so he need not take this one. But the two of them had already formed so warm a bond that despite the madam's interference, neither believed a word. Chrysanthemum told Xi Eleven her whole history in full detail, explaining that the madam had brought her over expressly to serve him. Her desire to make a proper marriage had nothing whatsoever to do with these people and only required gifting them a few taels of silver. Xi Eleven made up his mind at once, summoned an official matchmaker, and bestowed fifty taels of gold on Old Madam Tao. He took Chrysanthemum away, bought a maidservant, and hired an elderly female attendant. And so Chrysanthemum married Xi Eleven as a concubine, entering the proper path of life from that point on. As for what happened next, let the following chapter tell the tale."]},w=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations18:h},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),r={19:["Following Zhongyu's instructions, Pincai put them into practice: he first lavished every effort on winning Lin Shanzhi's favor, and then painstakingly cultivated the goodwill of the eight young pages. Shanzhi had originally been a celebrated young dan actor in the Lianjin troupe; he later joined the Dengchun troupe, and Young Master Hua, having taken a liking to him, purchased him at a high price. The eight pages who came afterward had all been trained by Shanzhi. Needless to say, this Lin Shanzhi was thoroughly schooled in music and song. Wei Pincai was by nature a quick-witted person, and his kun-opera singing was superb; even playing and strumming, he could hold his own. Moreover, on the journey up the previous year he had traveled along with a troupe, and on the boat had managed to pick up a great many operatic texts; after arriving in the capital, with theater-going every two or three days, he had naturally added a good deal more to his repertoire.","One day, Lin Shanzhi was teaching Yuling to sing an aria when Pincai happened to wander in. Shanzhi invited him to sit while he continued the lesson. As it happened, this aria was one of Pincai's favorites, so he began to sing along from the sidelines; his pitch was flawless and his articulation perfectly clear. He sang with such sublime expressiveness that Lin Shanzhi was completely astonished. From that moment on, they became close friends, and Shanzhi would praise Pincai morning and night in front of Young Master Hua.","Young Master Hua placed the most trust in Shanzhi; since Shanzhi did not praise people lightly, if he was willing to praise someone, they must truly be excellent. Thus, Pincai gained a firm place in the young master's heart. The members of the Eight-Year Troupe were all from Suzhou and Yangzhou, so their temperaments naturally matched his. Pincai knew how to curry favor: today he would give someone a fan, tomorrow a lotus-shaped pouch to someone else. He always gave them exactly what pleased them; such things were beloved by the young boys, who felt this 'master' was wonderfully attentive and understanding. As for the general managers and stewards, he flattered them with even greater thoroughness; in less than a month, everyone was speaking well of him. Old Yan and Old Wang need not be mentioned; even Zhang and Gu, though skilled at currying favor themselves, could not match Pincai's geniality, meticulousness, and air of utter devotion.","One day, having learned that the young master had gone out, Pincai invited Zhang Xiaomei to come out of the city. Xiaomei wanted to find Feng Zipei, so the two shared a carriage straight to Feng Zipei's house. This Zipei was the most familiar with Young Master Hua; he had already met Pincai, and the two hit it off well. Feng Zipei was also the son of an official family, but because he had lost his father early and had accumulated some family wealth, his mother doted on him, allowing him to squander his time in singing halls and befriend wealthy nobles; consequently, his family's fortunes gradually declined. Thanks to the support of several good friends, he still found himself flush with cash and dressed in brilliant attire; his particular kind of delicate, charming demeanor made him both pitiable and lovable.",`That day, Zhang and Wei came out, brought a small page boy, and arrived at Zipei's gate; they had the boy go in to inquire. Fortunately, he was at home and invited them in, leading them to sit in the study. This was Pincai's first visit. Seeing that the room consisted of two north-facing bays and was quite splendidly furnished, he also noticed that the table was completely covered with scattered books, brushes, inkstones, and curios, all jumbled together in complete disorder. Hanging on the walls were flutes, pipes, and pipas, as well as swords, arrows, and similar items. Pincai said to Xiaomei: "Little Feng has such a fine appearance, why doesn't he tidy up the things in his room a bit?" Xiaomei laughed: "He probably doesn't have the time to tidy up; there is no one in the world busier than he is." As they were speaking, Zipei came out. It was late in the fourth month, and dressed entirely in gossamer silk, he appeared all the more graceful and lithe. Before he had even stepped past the screen door, his laughter preceded him, as he called out: "What have you come here for? Have you come to pay your respects to Lord Number Two?" Pincai smiled and was about to speak, but Zhang Xiaomei stepped forward and clasped him tightly in an embrace; Zipei embraced Xiaomei in return, and the two held each other around the waist. Xiaomei said with a beaming smile:`,`"Indeed, we have come to pay our respects to Lord Number Two." He then pressed his face close to smell Zipei's cheek and added: "So fragrant! You're no Lord Number Two, you're practically a little young master." Zipei said: "You're being frivolous again, acting up so that Lord Number Two can hardly bear it." Pincai laughed heartily from the side. The three of them horsed around for a while before sitting down, though none of them could think of anything particular to say.`,`From outside came the sound of someone shouting: "Is Feng Number Two at home?" Zipei answered back: "Not at home." Pincai thought the voice sounded very familiar, and then saw someone barge straight in, saying: "Well, well! You're hiding in your cave and yet you answer that you're not at home." They all looked and saw it was Yang Meichuang; everyone was well-acquainted, making it even livelier, and their conversation was entirely filled with talk of games and merriment. The four of them discussed matters: "Surely we can't just sit here chatting today and call it done; wouldn't that be a waste of this beautiful spring day?" Xiaomei said: "We had planned to listen to some opera." Feng Zipei said: "Bah! Country bumpkins coming to the city don't recognize a bright horn lantern, they think it's a pig's bladder. Today is a memorial anniversary, and you still want to listen to opera?" Yang Meichuang said: "Indeed, today is an anniversary. What shall we do? Let's go to a restaurant." The other three were all enthusiastic; Zipei went inside to change his clothes, and then they all walked out together to a wine house.`,`The waiters, seeing four young men and recognizing Yang and Feng, perked up immediately and knew today would be lively. Master Yang the Eighth said: "What shall we eat?" Feng Zipei turned to the waiter and said: "Recite the menu for us." The waiter recited dozens of dishes one by one, and the four of them ordered five or six items, deciding to start eating first and see about the rest later. The waiter first warmed four pots of yellow wine and brought a table of fruit and nuts, then served the dishes one by one. After they had drunk for a while and exchanged some jokes, Meichuang said: "We can't just leave it at this; even the waiter will look down on us. Let's call for someone." Pincai was the most eager and said: "Excellent; who shall we call?" Meichuang laughed: "I have many favorites, and I like novelty; I'm not like some people who always call the same person every day. The day before yesterday I saw a boy in the Lianzhu troupe named Yulin, very handsome; the moment he stepped off stage someone took him out, so he must be very popular." Pincai said: "I expect he's not as good as Qinguan." Meichuang said: "Which Qinguan?" Pincai briefly recounted the story of watching the opera at New Year's, and added: "This Qinguan is always icy cold to everyone except Mei Yuxiang; I'm afraid he won't come if called." Meichuang said: "Where is there a young protégé who won't come when called? You call him today." Pincai thought to himself: "Now that I am in the Hua household, they ought to know about it; naturally they won't treat me the same as before. I'll call him, and if he doesn't come, I'll call someone else." Meichuang then asked Xiaomei: "Who will you call?" Xiaomei said:`,`"I'll call Rong-guan." He then asked Zipei; Zipei said: "With three already summoned, that's quite lively enough. I won't call anyone; I'll just drink the 'border-trimming wine.'" Meichuang laughed: "You might as well be the young protégé yourself." Hearing this, Zipei held a mouthful of wine and sprayed it straight at Meichuang's face. Meichuang dodged, but a good deal of it splashed onto his clothes. Meichuang said: "Why get so frantic over a single sentence? It must have struck your true weak spot." Everyone laughed. Meichuang took off his robe, asked for some strong clear spirit to spray on it, placed it on the railing under the eaves to air out, and then laughed again: "It's a pity this mouthful of wine was wasted; why didn't you spit it into my mouth?" Zipei grabbed a handful of melon-seed shells and threw them at him, and Meichuang accepted this without retaliation.`,`Then the waiter came in and said: "Qinguan and Yulin both say they're ill and can't come; Rong-guan is coming." Pincai had originally expected Qinguan wouldn't come, so he simply let it be. But Yang Meichuang was displeased in his heart and said: "How is it that of three people called, two are not coming? I wonder if they are truly ill, or just making excuses?" Xiaomei said: "Naturally they are truly ill; what is there to make excuses about?" Pincai said: "There is also Qiguan, who is very good; I called him a few times in the first month, and he always came." Pincai then wrote a slip to call Qiguan, and Meichuang separately called Er Xi. The waiter said: "Someone has been sent to call Qiguan. Er Xi has already finished eating with some guests over there, and they are just breaking up."`,`The waiter informed Er Xi; before long, Er Xi came over, greeted everyone in turn, and sat down beside Meichuang. After pouring a round of wine and presenting a round of food, he asked: "Who else is called to the table today?" Meichuang said: "The ones called are all ill and cannot come." Pincai was not very pleased to see Er Xi, because in the first month he had endured many cutting remarks from him. Er Xi, however, did not hold a grudge; moreover, the bill from that day had been sent over by Pincai the very next day, without any evasion, so Er Xi still respected him. He asked Pincai: "What was the surname of that gentleman from before? The one with the squinting eyes. The one whose silver was stolen by Xiaoli. We never see him anymore." Pincai said: "I live in the city now, so I don't associate much with those friends." Er Xi said: "Where in the city are you?" Pincai said: "The Hua Ducal Mansion." Er Xi exclaimed: "Good heavens! The Hua Mansion." He then asked where Zhang Xiaomei lived, and Xiaomei replied: "I live with him in the same residence." Er Xi said: "I hear there is opera singing every day in the Hua Mansion; does the mansion have its own troupe?" Pincai said: "Several troupes, in fact." Er Xi then went around to each person urging them to drink, playing finger-guessing games, taking penalty cups, stopping at nothing.`,`They had been making merry for a while when still neither Rong-guan nor Qiguan had arrived. Xiaomei said: "How strange. Today is a memorial anniversary, yet we can't even call out any young protégés." Er Xi said: "Who else is there?" Xiaomei said: "Qiguan from your troupe, and Rong-guan from Lianzhu." Er Xi said: "As for Rong-guan, when I left the house I saw him going to the Sanhe Restaurant; I expect they haven't broken up yet." Meichuang said: "That Yulin is in your troupe too — is he truly ill?" Er Xi said: "Ah, Yulin! Don't even mention it. He and Qiguan both got involved in an incident the day before yesterday, nearly resulting in a loss of life. Their teacher still refuses to let it go and is going to file a lawsuit against the man. Qiguan can't come today either." Thereupon everyone asked what the matter was. Er Xi said: "It is a long story; let's drink two cups first before I tell it." Everyone drained several more cups. Pincai, hearing that Qiguan was involved in an incident, asked Er Xi: "Tell us about it, so we can all hear." Er Xi said: "There is a Lord Xi the Eleventh from Guangdong; are you gentlemen acquainted with him?" The three said they did not know him. Feng Zipei said: "I have met this man, but we are not close; speak your mind freely." Er Xi said: "This Lord Xi is a candidate for office in the capital. I hear he brought tens of thousands of taels of silver with him, intending to purchase a high official post. Who knows what he spent it on, but he came up short and only managed to purchase the rank of Department Magistrate. This man truly considers himself a big spender; as soon as he arrived in the capital, he first got to know Chunlan of the Dengchun troupe, and kept Chunlan in his rooms every day — the clothes, gold bracelets, heated carriages, and such things need not even be mentioned. Chunlan has the most stage appearances; for every one of Chunlan's roles, he made a dozen sets of costumes, and the ornaments were all of gold; it must have cost over ten thousand taels. Chunlan's teacher deliberately called Chunlan back to provoke and squeeze him, and in bits and pieces he spent quite a lot more. Later, to buy Chunlan out of his apprenticeship, he spent another five thousand strings of cash; Chunlan then followed him, sharing an opium pipe on the kang every day and eating at the same table. For example, with a single dish, if Chunlan tasted it and said it was too salty or too bland, he would smash the bowl and all. He spent hundreds of strings of cash to make a fur jacket, and when Chunlan said, 'The fur trim isn't nice, I don't want it,' he took a look, agreed it wasn't nice, tore it to pieces with his bare hands, and had another one made. Now, this kind of foolish profligacy with his own money is one thing. But since he was so close to Chunlan, he shouldn't have gone bothering others; yet he cared nothing for that. If someone caught his fancy, regardless of whether they were willing or not, he would simply force himself on them." Everyone listened quietly as he spoke. Pincai said: "We asked you about Yulin and Qiguan, and here you are going on endlessly about this foolish spendthrift." Er Xi laughed: "Told all the way through, it's actually better listening than an opera. You see, he has so many ways of tricking people! He is from Jiaying Prefecture, so he possesses that clever 'Western' method. When he lures someone, he first tries to buy their heart with money, but there are also those who do not love money but value character. This Lord Xi has a coarse appearance, tall and hulking, looking like a military officer, and he speaks stupidly. He also smokes opium, an ounce a day, and his face is dark and sallow." Meichuang said: "Hurry up and tell us; what is this clever 'Western' method?" Er Xi said: "He has a wooden barrel, small at the top and wide at the bottom, painted with foreign lacquer and traced in gold. Inside, it makes a ding-dong sound, rather like a clock.`,`"On top was a lid; inside was a layer of board, and under the board a crossbar; on the outside there was a brass padlock, so you couldn't see anything. If he took a fancy to someone and they weren't compliant, he would coax them into the inner room to look at the barrel. Hearing it ring inside, a person would naturally lean over the edge to look; Xi Eleven would then take some things — gold or silver ingots, or jade trinkets, all valuable items — and toss them into the barrel, saying that whatever they could fish out would be theirs. How could the victim know the secret mechanism? They would reach a hand down. It turned out the middle board had two holes; one hand would go in but couldn't reach anything, so they would reach down the other hand. He would then take a key, turn the lock, and those two hands could no longer be withdrawn. The barrel couldn't be lifted either, leaving the person bowed over. He would then ignore their protests and force himself upon them. Only when his lust was sated would he release them; shouting was useless, since the deed was already done. Once released, there was nothing to be done. Those who were worldly-wise would demand more gifts from him; but if an innocent tried to make a fuss, he would turn nasty, accuse them of extortion, beat them, and curse them. He would even threaten to send them to the ward office to be dealt with. Most of the ward officials are in league with his sort; once sent there and locked up, they would suffer all kinds of humiliation, and their teacher would have to bring money to ransom them, at the very least a hundred strings or so. This is Xi Eleven's behavior. Now, how did Yulin and Qiguan get involved? This same Xi Eleven first drank at Yulin's house. Yulin is an honest, gentle sort, not skilled at flattery. But Xi Eleven took a fancy to him and gave him a set of clothing, a pair of bracelets, and forty strings of cash for his teacher, which worked him into a fever. The second time he invited Yulin alone, without many other guests, and gave Yulin another hundred strings of cash. Yulin couldn't stand the way Xi Eleven looked and always acted cool toward him, which made Xi Eleven annoyed. For the third occasion, his teacher organized many young protégés and invited Yulin again, but this time Yulin simply didn't come. His teacher, still seeing Xi Eleven as a big catch, forced Yulin to go and pay his respects. Xi Eleven then went even further: he apparently had it all planned. He kept Yulin for dinner, poured several cups of wine into him, and then tried to trick him into looking at the barrel. But somehow Yulin had heard about the barrel trick beforehand and refused to look. Having no recourse, Xi Eleven tried to assault him by brute force. Having the advantage of strength, he pinned Yulin down; Yulin refused and wept and screamed. His attendant heard it and tried to come in to look, but Xi Eleven's people wouldn't let him in. He forced his way in regardless and saw Yulin pinned down with his trousers already pulled off; only the sight of someone entering made Xi Eleven release him, and he had to claim it was only playful teasing, saying the boy didn't know how to take a joke. Yulin rushed out, straightening his clothes as he went, weeping and cursing; the attendant also made a scene in the gatehouse. Xi Eleven wanted to beat Yulin but couldn't catch up, so in a fury he sent him to the ward office, and that might have been the end of it. But as true ill-luck would have it, more trouble was yet to come. That evening he also summoned Qiguan. You all know Qiguan's temperament; how could he possibly comply? Xi Eleven tried to lure him into looking at the barrel; Qiguan, not knowing about it, fell for the trick and put both hands in, and he could not withdraw them. Xi Eleven tried to use the same method again and started pulling at Qiguan's undergarments. Qiguan understood what was happening and let fly with his leg, landing squarely on that particular organ. Xi Eleven nearly died of pain and sank down in a crouch." At this, Zhang and Wei burst into laughter and said: "Served him right! Served him right! Such a thing must have its heaven-sent retribution. More wine; let's all raise a cup to celebrate." Feng Zipei said nothing; Yang Meichuang said:`,`"Get on with it." Er Xi drank some wine and continued: "Qiguan was also in a terrible state. His hands couldn't be withdrawn, and he became furious; ignoring the pain, he wrenched and twisted with all his strength until the mechanism was broken. Qiguan's two hands were scraped raw, dripping blood; he too came out weeping and cursing. Xi Eleven, his own vital part in agony, retreated to hide. Qiguan went back and told his teacher; and since he is close to Yuan Baozhu, he also told Baozhu. Baozhu was furious and went into Yiyuan to tell Lord Xu. Lord Xu was outraged and said: 'How can the world contain such a creature, that anyone would tolerate him behaving like this? This is beyond endurance!' He then learned of Yulin's situation as well, and immediately sent someone to the ward office to have Yulin released overnight. At the same time he considered reporting the matter to the Metropolitan Circuit Censor, intending to have Xi Eleven's barrel confiscated and Xi Eleven prosecuted. Lord Xu is a straightforward and upright person; his words were spoken without any attempt at concealment, and somehow word leaked out. Xi Eleven, afraid of a public scandal, and moreover having run out of money, gave up his debts on the western account, rushed to the Board of Personnel and paid to have himself listed as unable to continue due to insufficient travel funds, and left the city. I hear he went to Tianjin; he'll probably hide there for a while and then come back. So Yulin is furious and unwell, and Qiguan's hands are not yet healed. How could he possibly come out? That's the whole story; now you all drink a big cup. My tongue is quite dry."`,`This tale set everyone laughing and marveling at the strangeness of it. Feng Zipei said: "What a vile, despicable creature. He doesn't care who anyone is; if he sees a young good-looking person he thinks they're fair game. And he doesn't even look at himself." Meichuang laughed: "You're so indignant. Can it be that you've already had a look at his precious barrel?" Zipei spat at Meichuang twice. Meichuang said: "Can they make that kind of barrel here in the capital, or not?" Xiaomei laughed: "Are you thinking of copying the design?" Meichuang gave a little laugh. Pincai laughed and said to Er Xi: "You've described it so clearly, you must have had a look at the barrel yourself." Er Xi's face reddened, and with a sideways glance he reached over to pinch Pincai's mouth. Meichuang said: "He probably doesn't need to use the barrel." Er Xi then gave Meichuang two pinches and said: "Those of us who work in this trade, if you have money, everything is fine; why go to all that bother with such theatrics? It's a pity they can't read feng shui the way you can, that's why they had to suffer like that."`,`Having said this, Er Xi laughed himself. Pincai thought privately: "Who would have expected that Qinguan and Qiguan, even as performers, should be so proud and dignified, so steadfast in their principles. That is something to marvel at." He then asked Er Xi who exactly this Xi Eleven was, and how he could behave so lawlessly and still have so much money. Er Xi said: "I heard from Chunlan that he too is the son of a well-born family. His grandfather served as a Provincial Administration Commissioner, and his father is currently serving as a Brigade Commander; his family runs a foreign trading firm." Pincai said: "What about Chunlan now?" Er Xi said: "Went out with him." The group then talked and joked some more, drank another round. Seeing that the day was growing late, Xiaomei and Pincai both needed to return to the city and settled the bill. Meichuang arranged with Er Xi to settle the payment the next day. Meichuang declined to accompany Pincai and the others into the city; since he lived outside the city, he went back to Zipei's place, and the two of them had a smoke of opium together; then Meichuang dragged Zipei off to Jade Celestial Fairy's establishment in Vermilion Cosmetics Lane.`,`Now let us speak of Pan Qiguan: since being tricked and humiliated by Huifang, he had been genuinely vexed, wishing to settle scores with Huifang but fearing his wide connections and numerous supporters. At the same time his lascivious urge had not been extinguished and he still hoped to actually get what he wanted. He was also smarting over the two hundred strings of cash, and had begun to suspect that Zhang Zhongyu had colluded with Huifang to make a fool of him, so he made veiled, insinuating remarks to Zhongyu. Zhongyu, having been unjustly blamed, truly had nowhere to air his grievance, and came to resent Pan Qiguan. "Since he suspects me anyway, I might as well dig a proper pit for him. I'll scheme with Huifang to deal with him." Pan Qiguan then had to go to Baoding to inspect some fabric shops he owned there and was away for two months before returning. Idle with nothing to do, he had a series of quarrels with his wife and was suffering from pent-up frustration with no way to relieve it. He thought again of going to Huifang's place for amusement, but this time without Zhang Zhongyu; he came alone.`,`It was already evening that day; as chance would have it, he arrived at Huifang's gate at the very moment Huifang was returning from Chunhang's place. Huifang, seeing it was Pan Qiguan, was inwardly startled but had no choice; he jumped down from his carriage and invited the Third Master Pan inside. Pan Qiguan seized Huifang's hand and puffed along into the inner room, where they sat down in the reception room. Huifang said: "Third Master Pan, I haven't seen you for days. Where have you been making your fortune? You rarely honor us with a visit. I recall that the other day, because the young master Hua was staying outside the city, I was summoned there and was truly remiss in attending to you. Please don't hold it against me. We have plenty of time ahead of us to be friends." Pan Qiguan, seeing Huifang so warm and gracious, felt half his former indignation dissolve, and said slowly: "What have I come here for? I know perfectly well you look down on me. Two hundred strings of cash, and all I got was old Zhang Number Two spitting a mouthful of wine in my face. The rabbit hides in its burrow while the wildcat can only drool and dream." Huifang heard these words and was quite annoyed, but had to put on a smile and say: "What interesting things you say. So what are we going to do tonight? Let us find somewhere to sit together." Pan Qiguan said: "What other place is there to look for? Right here is very comfortable. But I have sworn an oath and given up drinking; today I won't have a single drop." Huifang heard this and grew more anxious still, thinking: today is truly bad. Here he is alone, not even willing to drink, and he certainly won't be willing to leave. If I make some excuse to go, he probably won't let me. She turned it over and over in her mind, and a flush gradually spread across her face. After a long silent pause she made up her mind defiantly: "I might as well let him stay. If I were afraid of him, I wouldn't be called Su Huifang." She said: "Third Master, if you won't drink wine, at least you'll eat dinner." Pan Qiguan nodded; Huifang personally went to the kitchen for a while, then brought out the meal: three meat dishes, three vegetable dishes, a bowl of Shaoxing soup, and a pot of yellow wine.`,`Huifang said: "Even if you've given up wine, since you've come to my place you must keep up appearances." With a smiling face, she took up a large jade cup, filled it to the brim, and presented it with both hands. Pan Qiguan had not actually given up drinking; he simply feared wine would impair his plans. Seeing Huifang's manner now, he could not help grinning and saying: "All right, finish this whole pot, no refills." Huifang paid no attention to his conditions; the two drank together, also eating some boiled dumplings and similar fare. Pan Qiguan was becoming tipsy; as the cups went back and forth and another pot was added, he gazed at Huifang's peach-blossom cheeks and autumn-water eyes, glances full of allure, a hundred seductive expressions, which revived all Pan Qiguan's old nature. He sighed: "You little creature, you'll truly be the death of me. What are two hundred strings of cash? You have no need to do me such harm! Son, as long as you show me just a little heart, there's nothing I won't agree to." Huifang forced a smile and said: "Third Master, I don't understand. What do you mean, agree or not agree?" Pan Qiguan said: "As long as you have heart for me, whatever you want I'll agree to." Huifang said with a laugh: "You may not really be willing. What I want, what I want is a silver exchange house (yin hao). You yourself said so before." Pan Qiguan said: "I have three silver houses; and I'm already forty-eight years old, still with no sons. To give you one is no great matter. And what will you give me?" Huifang said nothing. Pan Qiguan said: "Why have you gone quiet again? It's just the two of us; no outsiders here. Is there anything that can't be said?" Huifang continued to give that not-quite-smiling, not-quite-unsmiling expression and said nothing. Pan Qiguan moved closer and pulled Huifang into his embrace; with his sticky, syrup-soaked face, he tried to press it against those fragrant, powder-warm cheeks. Huifang interposed a hand lightly and softly said: "You're being too impossible. Let go, and then I'll talk." Pan Qiguan rubbed his face back and forth on the back of Huifang's hand, panting: "Good boy, good darling; just say it." Huifang put on an expression of feigned annoyance: "Third Master, with you being so impatient, I simply won't say it now." Pan Qiguan had no choice but to release his hold. Huifang's hand was wet with saliva; she wiped it with a handkerchief and stood up, saying with composure: "Third Master Pan, I am no fool. Do you think I can't see through what's on your mind? But since I am a performer, I cannot pretend to speak of purity and cleanliness. Still, I am only eighteen years old this year and I have just completed my apprenticeship; out there in the world, I ask you to leave me some dignity and let people regard me as a person. Please don't harass me this way. If I have heart for you, that should be enough; don't let people see through it. Don't treat me like the disciples of a barber's shed. Third Master, you think I spent two hundred strings of your cash and you begrudge it. Well, if you want it back, I can repay it." Pan Qiguan said: "Good boy, who wants you to repay money? Don't blame me: I've been thinking about this for a full six months, and you won't let me feel just a little ease. If you truly have heart for me, that's fine; but I still fear you're just taking me for a fool again. If you try to trick me again, I won't stand for it. But whatever you say, I'll go along with it."`,`Huifang laughed again: "I just said, Third Master is used to frequenting the barber's sheds, you're treating my place as just the same kind of thing. I heard from Zhang Zhongyu that Third Master Pan is most generous: if he sets his heart on someone, not only three or five hundred, even one or two thousand strings he'll spend freely. How is it that Third Master also takes a liking to me, yet after spending a mere two hundred strings here you're already trying to recoup your investment? As for two hundred strings, not only would Third Master not look twice at it, even I, surnamed Su, don't consider it anything at all. Could it be that Third Master, for a cup of wine and a song, wouldn't even tip a hundred or so strings? Like those small-time operators who call a young protégé for a whole day and give four or five strings of Beijing cash? Let me tell you this: as long as you can truly show heart, I guarantee I won't let you down. But don't forget me; don't treat me as a lowly person, so that once you've had your way you drop me and go chasing someone else. Then don't blame me." Pan Qiguan, having been worked over by Huifang's speech, was left with nothing to say. Hearing line after line of apparent agreement, yet with the implied sense of wanting more money and that two hundred strings was too little. And then he thought again: "Such a celebrated young protégé naturally doesn't come easily to hand." He said to Huifang: "If you truly won't let me down, I can put my mind at ease. But words are no guarantee; later I fear you'll change your tune again." Huifang laughed coldly: "You mistrust me a thousand times and ten thousand times. What, do you want me to write you a contract?"`,`At this moment Pan Qiguan's lascivious heart was blazing; and yet he also wanted to appear generous, so he couldn't resort to brute force. Thinking it over, the only recourse was to win Huifang over with more money. He said: "I know you're a free-spending young protégé with a big style, always needing cash. Starting today, whenever you're short of money, just come to my shop and take what you need." Huifang said: "How could I go there? We can't let Third Mistress find out; she'd give you such a scolding that you'd suffer all over again." Pan Qiguan laughed: "Nonsense, tell me honestly: are you actually short of money or not?" Huifang thought: "This creature has been cut short by me and still doesn't understand. He still imagines he can buy me with money. I might as well milk this muddled fool for some; it can serve as lamp-oil money for Tian darling." With a smile she said: "Money? Of course I want money. It's just awkward to say so. I fear Third Master would suspect that I'm always taking money and giving nothing in return; spending a great deal and getting nothing." As she spoke, she looked down at the kingfisher-jade bracelet on her own wrist, then took it off and showed it to Pan Qiguan: "Tell me, is this jade any good?" Pan Qiguan looked: it was clear as water and entirely green in color. He praised it: "Fine kingfisher-jade, rarely seen in the city. It must be Yunnan-origin jade." Huifang said: "It was a gift from Lord Xu at Yiyuan. One of four identical pieces he gave to four people; I received one. I heard it was bought in Guangdong; one piece was priced at one foreign silver dollar." Pan Qiguan clicked his tongue: "More expensive than gold. Ten liang of gold weighs as much but is worth only two hundred silver." Huifang said: "Fine as it is, it's a pity there's no gold piece to match it." As she spoke, her eyes kept glancing at a heavy gold bangle on Pan Qiguan's wrist. Pan Qiguan understood; he wanted to give it to her, but it weighed ten liang and was worth two hundred silver, and his heart ached to part with it; yet if he didn't give it, he feared she would look down on him, so he said nothing.`,`He raised his arm and studied the bracelet for a while, then said to Huifang: "Pair it with this one and it would look perfect. If you want it, take it." He kept raising his arm but made no move to remove it. Huifang moved close beside him, thanked him, and began to take the bracelet off his wrist; the moment it was slipped onto Huifang's own hand, Pan Qiguan grabbed her around the waist, murmuring endless endearments, "my heart, my darling," nuzzling and sniffing at her face incessantly, and then began groping and pawing. This time Huifang was truly in a difficult position; saying anything more would be useless, as Pan Qiguan was past listening. He had clearly resolved not to leave empty-handed today; and having parted with the gold bracelet, he felt his face was quite bright and shining with it. Huifang could only put on a smiling expression; noticing a small ivory comb hanging from his lapel, she took it and began combing his beard. At this critical moment, they suddenly heard from outside someone calling out: "Is Huifang at home?" Then another voice: "The old father has come!" There seemed to be the sound of many footsteps. Huifang hurriedly broke free and said: "Something's wrong; the ward official is here to do his nightly inspection!" Pan Qiguan was a wealthy merchant; at the words "ward official's nightly inspection" he was instantly agitated and looked for somewhere to hide. Huifang said: "There's nowhere to hide. You'd better just go now, rather than running into them and being questioned up and down, which would look quite bad." Pan Qiguan had no choice; just at the moment when he had gotten his hands on something, he was scattered away again. He could only slink out through the dark and make a dash for the main gate. As for who the actual caller was, let the following chapter tell the tale.`]},p=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations19:r},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),d={20:[`The previous chapter told how Pan Qiguan had Huifang cornered at the crucial moment when someone suddenly came shouting, and Huifang feigned alarm and cried: "Dreadful — it's the ward officials come to do their inspection!" Pan Qiguan was a wealthy man of timid nature, naturally afraid of causing trouble, and had no choice but to slink away.`,"In fact, when Huifang had gone to the kitchen earlier, he had calculated that Pan Qiguan would certainly not let him off tonight, and so had instructed the household servants to pretend to be ward officials conducting the nightly inspection, and had also brought in two actual ward constables; at the moment when Pan Qiguan's harassment became intolerable, they were to come in shouting. Knowing that Pan Qiguan's lust was great but his nerve small, the scheme worked perfectly and he left tricked once more. Though she had obtained his gold bracelet, Huifang was still genuinely troubled in her heart, fearing he would come back the next day. The only recourse was to move to Chunhang's lodgings and hide for a few days, then see. Pan Qiguan went home in low spirits; fortunately he was afraid of his wife and dared not cause scenes openly outside the home — otherwise, even clever as Huifang was, it might have been hard to hold him off. Today had once more come to nothing; the only plan was to gradually win her over with more money and buy her heart around.","This chapter now turns to speak of several men of elegant tastes engaged in an affair of elegant taste. The company of celebrated men had by now gradually thinned. Since Ziyu's one meeting with Qinyan at Yiyuan, they had never managed to see each other again. Qinyan's illness came and went; he was not appearing in the troupe's productions, and only occasionally, forcing himself despite illness, would he go to Yiyuan for a visit.","Meanwhile Ziyu's own illness had also arisen from melancholy and worry; sometimes when he went to Yiyuan, Qinyan happened not to have come; sometimes when he went to Qinyan's lodgings, there was always some inconvenience — either his teacher Changqing was entertaining guests, or someone was sitting there. He also could not easily go to Sulan's again; Ziyu felt utterly helpless and could only mourn the grudging lot of fate. Qinyan, at home, did not know how many times Ziyu had come by; and hearing that Ziyu was ill, he felt all the more heartsick — yet since he had never once called at the Mei residence, it was awkward for him to go on his own initiative. Thus a deep and tender feeling of admiration and longing had nowhere to be eased; all he could do was sigh short sighs and long sighs, and let it haunt his dreams. You may wonder, reader, why when Ziyu went to find Qinyan, his teacher never tried to bring them together. First, Ziyu was only an occasional visitor — not one of those who regularly kept company outside — and so Changqing did not know him well, and not knowing what position Ziyu held, took him for no more than a young student with no great usefulness. Second, Ziyu had not spent a single cash on Qinyan. The spiritual sympathy between Ziyu and Qinyan being of a purely ideal and heartfelt kind, neither of them had thought about this dimension. Changqing, on the other hand, thought only about money and not at all about quality of person. That is simply how those of the inferior dan-actor class are — such was Changqing's nature, unchanging from beginning to end.","Let us speak of Ziyu: he was at home recuperating from his illness and not going out at all. Gao Pin had been invited by the prefect of Hejian, Lord Hu, to assist in compiling a local gazetteer. Liu Wenze's mother-in-law had been missing him and had come to fetch him along with his wife née Wu to the office of the Viceroy of Zhili. So three of the group were gone; only Ziyun, Cixian, Nanxiang, Zhongqing, Chunhang, and Wang Xun — six people — continued to meet from time to time.","One day, Ziyun and Cixian invited the celebrated gentlemen to the garden to view the dragon boat and enjoy the pomegranate blossoms. This day was the first of the fifth month, which happened also to be the Intendant-General Wang's birthday; though no formal celebration was held, a number of close friends, relatives, and colleagues came to offer their congratulations, and indoors there were also ladies — such as Lady Yan from the Mei household and Madam Lu from the Sun household — so it was quite festive. Wang Xun and Zhongqing were thus unable to attend the Yiyuan appointment. On learning of this, Ziyun and Cixian also went to offer birthday congratulations; there they happened to find Nanxiang and Chunhang both present, so they invited them to come back with them. Zhongqing and Wang Xun said that if the guests dispersed early, they would also come to keep the appointment.",`"But don't wait for us — we may be early or late." Cixian and the others agreed and returned to Yiyuan. Coming together to the foot of the steep rock face at the garden's entrance, Ziyun invited everyone to change out of their formal attire. He also said: "The day is very hot and the red sun beats down on people; moreover the dragon boat is at the Chanting-Autumn Water Pavilion, and the pomegranate blossoms are at Little Red Citadel — both are quite far from here. Better to ride over." The servants had already prepared horses in advance and brought them forward; the four mounted. From below the steep rock face they turned left, going up and down over a winding and twisting path along a small sheep-track of blue stone, with old vines brushing their heads and fragrant grasses catching their clothing. When the mountain path was done, they followed the enclosure wall. On the other side rippled the surface of the pond, red and green reflected in the water. A short red railing ran along the embankment. Slender bamboo and drooping willows swayed. Assorted flowers covered the trees in abundance. Orioles flew and called here and there — a scene enough to wash away all worldly care. Less than half a li further, another mass of towering stones had been piled into a high hill over ten zhang in height — like a single peak of Luofu overlooking the curve of the sea — blocking the way ahead.`,`Ziyun invited his guests to dismount; from the foot of the hill they ascended over thirty stone steps, arriving at a small pavilion furnished with a stone table and benches. A plaque named it the "Pavilion of the Misty Void" (缥缈亭). Looking across, the view was cut off by dozens of ancient pines, their dark canopy reaching to the heavens; only at their very tips could one glimpse the faint shimmer of green tiles and a corner of a vermilion tower. Here, a cool breeze drifted freely, the water and stones breathed a clean chill, and one felt a floating sensation, as if ascending above the mortal realm. Chunhang exclaimed: "How wondrous and profound! Even the utmost of literary imagination could go no further; even the Incense Burner Peak of Mount Lu could offer nothing superior." Nanxiang observed: "The earlier scenery resembled Wang Lutai's style; this evokes Xiao Chizhu—secluded and deep, without being narrow or perilous." Ziyun noted: "This was all designed by Jingyi; when laying out the arrangement, he revised his drafts numerous times." Cixian added: "It is thanks to those ancient pines; otherwise, the eye would exhaust the view in a single glance." Chunhang said: "One can only wonder how many landscapes reside in Master Jingyi's heart; his is truly a divine hand, commanding rivers and mountains. Ni Yunlin and Xu Qingteng would surely wish to take his arm and walk with him into these woods." Cixian could only offer a few modest disclaimers. The four rested a moment, then descended the stone steps. To the side stood a loft of five bays, staggered into two uneven levels—neither quite a tower nor quite a pavilion—with painted beams soaring amid clouds, and pearl curtains rolled up against the rain; an entirely different atmosphere altogether. Beyond the window railing lay a square pond of some ten mu, its surface layered with the green coins of lotus leaves, half of them already formed into canopies. Spanning the center was a red six-bend bridge, angling and sloping, connecting to a dozen or so pavilions on the opposite shore. Moored to the right were several small painted boats, all with brocade cables, ivory gunwales, and fragrant orchid oars. Cixian indicated: "That over there is the Chanting-Autumn Water Pavilion (吟秋水榭)." Looking again, they saw it was three stories high; along the left bank, an unbroken line of weeping willows brushed low over the water, meeting the long bamboo groves on the opposite shore, as if the two banks yearned to join.`,`Ziyun asked his guests to pause before crossing the bridge, leading them first into the loft. It was a neat set of three central bays, hung with curtains woven of fine copper wire, its water-polished nanmu wood carved into delicate railings. The furnishings within were of extraordinary refinement—precious tripods and jade zithers, jeweled tables and marble desks beyond enumeration. Beside the railing sat an antique bronze water clock, holding several bamboo indicator arrows; suspended in the center was a plaque reading: "Studio of Pausing Clouds and Narrating Rain" (停云叙雨之斋). Beside it hung a couplet, reading:

"Bowing to rocks, there are times one dons court robes and hat;
Gazing at clouds, there is nowhere one is not a celestial immortal."

The signature read: Huaguang Suxiu. Nanxiang exclaimed in surprise: "This is Young Master Hua's brushwork—I had no idea his literary arts reached such heights." Ziyun replied: "Young Master Hua possesses exceptionally high natural talent; his application has merely been somewhat shallow, having been misled by his power and position. In truth, his calligraphy, painting, poetry, and lyrics run rather contrary to what one might expect of his station." Chunhang agreed: "If one listened only to the gossip of the common crowd, one would almost miss the man entirely. From this couplet alone, one sees the elegance of his mind; from this calligraphy alone, one sees the heroic gallantry of his spirit." As they spoke, they saw two flower-decked boats glide out from the distance near the water pavilion—white hulls and green curtains still separated by the red bridge and green willows, accompanied by the gentle creak of soft oars and the lilting melody of a lotus-picking song. The water was bright as a mirror, reflecting the pavilions; swallows skimmed low, and fish breached the surface to blow bubbles. In an instant they had passed the red bridge and drifted slowly, slowly in to moor. One could now see Wang Lanbao with the hem of his silk robe tucked up, his braid coiled atop his head, a pomegranate blossom tucked behind his ear; holding a slender purple-bamboo punting pole, he pushed the boat along while shooing away the domestic and wild ducks, startling the mandarin ducks and water birds into scattered flight. Then a white egret came swooping straight toward the railing; upon reaching the eaves, it tilted its wings and flew up to the rocky crags.`,`Cixian laughed: "The proverb about 'catching the duck and startling the mandarin ducks' — today one sees it in action." While everyone was watching with interest, they saw figures stepping out of the boats like blossoming flowers: one boat held Baozhu and Shufang, the other Huifang and Sulan — five people in all. The boatmen moored the craft against the railing; Nanxiang said: "The hibiscus has not yet opened, and the water pavilion lacks a certain brilliance. With all this company of flowers making the crossing, it will no longer be desolate. Xiang Consorts and Luo Goddesses sporting on the river's edge — I imagine Duxiang-xiong would receive them with his jade pendant offering." Everyone laughed. All the young dan actors came up and made their greetings all around. Cixian said: "Look at the way Jingfang's narrow-sleeve movement stops and starts with an ease and flow — it really brings out an air of free and graceful charm. Zhujun's praising words — 'Gliding like a startled wild goose, swaying like a wandering dragon' — one feels truly captures the essence." Nanxiang said: "Jingfang's free-spirited ease, Meixiang's lively wit, Yaoqing's soft grace, Shouxiang's prettily quiet refinement, Xiangwan's rich elegance — all are celestial immortals become human. As for their artistry — Zhao Feiyan dancing on the palm, Zhang Jingwan singing within the curtains — these could perhaps give some idea of it." Ziyun invited the guests onto the boats; Nanxiang and the others stepped aboard. The prow was carved with a pair of mandarin ducks neck to neck; the boat's body was of crabapple-pear wood, with short red railings on both sides, glass windows running the length of the interior, and on the canopy a great rolled pavilion of green clay-dyed cloth with scattered flowers, with painted-flower silk in white hanging down from both sides. Inside were two cabins; the floor was laid with fine white velvet carpet; the aft section also had long windows; in the center was a bed-platform with Hu-style rattan chairs on each side interspersed with tea tables; a round table in the center could seat five or six people. The second, slightly smaller boat was for the attendants. By now Wang Lanbao had already changed his clothes and was sitting there quietly and decorously. Baozhu said to Nanxiang: "Did you call at Lord Wang's this morning?" Before Nanxiang could answer, Ziyun said: "I invited them from Wang's residence directly."`,`So everyone chatted and talked, looking at the garden scenery along the way. In several places soaring lofts rose to the clouds, carved ridgepoles overlooking the ground below; in several places towering crags jutted out starkly, with ancient trees and gnarled branches. They could also see, to the northwest, a long gallery that was the Peach Grove (Taohu); next to it was the Apricot Village (Xinglun); to the due north, looking through the bamboo grove, was the Pear Courtyard (Liyuan), behind which was the Peony Fragrant Kingdom (Mudan Xiangguo); to the northeast was a stretch of delicate and ingenious rock-work, below which were thousands of trees in green shade, with golden globes — the plum fruits hanging thick, half green and half yellow — covering the Plum Vale (Mei Yan) so it could not be clearly seen. The opposite shore was thickly overgrown with trees and rocks; how many more pavilions and courts it concealed one could not know. Chunhang asked Nanxiang: "How many parts of this garden have you toured so far?" Nanxiang said: "I've been here many times, but I've never actually toured it properly. A drinking session takes the whole day — when would one find time to stroll about? I've been to perhaps seven or eight places." Baozhu said: "I have toured it completely with Shouxiang." Huifang said: "I haven't been to Osmanthus Ridge, Chrysanthemum Patch, or Orchid Path; all the rest I've seen."`,`Sulan said: "Osmanthus Ridge is in front of the front hill; Orchid Path and Chrysanthemum Patch are behind the back hill. You cross the ravine into a large open space where there is a village-style compound — that's the Chrysanthemum Patch. Orchid Path is on the hillside, going from the foot of the hill up to the halfway point, with high and low galleries and winding paths — a most delightful place to explore. Beyond Chrysanthemum Patch there is also a Rice Farm, with shadoofs drawing water, looking like a genuine village — fishing nets, crab weirs, everything. There are also two craneenclosures and a deer pen nearby."`,`As they finished speaking, the boat had traveled over half a li to a bend, and the pond opened out into a spacious expanse. The Chanting-Autumn Water Pavilion was built out in the water, surrounded on all four sides by the pond, standing three stories tall. The first story had twelve bays arranged in a hexagonal pattern, with windows open on every face — purely glass-inlaid carved windows — partitioned into six sections. Each section was further divided into yin-yang, bright-and-dim arrangements, making twelve subdivisions in all, each varying in shape — square, round, flat, and angled — each with its own setting. The furnishings inside were beyond description. Looking from one room through to the next, only a pane of glass separated them, yet to actually get from one to the other required winding through several turns. The company roamed about this first story for quite a long while. Ziyun said: "The guests must be hungry by now; it is nearly noon — shall we be seated?" Four small pages were seen carrying in four gold-lacquered trays with several bowls of apricot-kernel porridge, which were distributed to each person. Chunhang said: "Why not go up to the upper two stories and then come back to sit?" So they turned up the stairway to the second story, which was somewhat smaller — four-sided in design, with an open corridor running around it with railings on all sides, and carved windows partitioning it into eight sections, with antique vessels and utensils of equal refinement. Looking northeast at the willow shade, one could see the dragon boat moored there — over three zhang in height; the hull was carved and painted in the form of a green dragon, with five or six tiers of framework built in the center, made entirely of five-colored silk, brocade, velvet, and clay, fashioned into parasols, canopies, and pennants — embroidered with scattered-thread, flat-gold, and knotted-stitch flowers of every variety, set off with peacock-gilded parasols, pearl parasols, and parasols threaded from silver needles. In the center were constructed paper-cut towers and pavilions, royal palaces and Buddhist temples, adorned with scenes from famous historical sites. Every figure inside moved by pulleys — the mechanical ingenuity was extraordinary. Layer upon layer of construction, a blaze of brocade and cloud, glorious as bright flowers and blazing flames. Atop it all stood a constructed golden peacock; the boat's bottom was weighted with stones; twenty-four rowers on both sides propelled it, with a band of eight musical tones inside — drums, gongs, strings, and winds, both the coarse ten-beat and the refined varieties. This design had been seen by Cixian in Jiangsu and drawn in a sketch from which artisans had been engaged to build it. Chunhang had come from the south and had also seen something like it before; he said: "Truly magnificently made — even the dragon boats of Changzhou Prefecture, the finest in the entire province, are no better than this."`,"They then ascended to the third story, which followed a three-sided design, with an outer corridor also running along three sides, its interior partitioned into six areas. Here, the windows, cabinets, and doors were of uniform fragrant nanmu wood, exceedingly antique and unadorned, rendering it all the more elegant and tranquil. Its elevated position caught an even more refreshing breeze; leaning against the railing to gaze out, one could take in eight or nine tenths of Yiyuan's entire panorama, with only the shaded sides of the hills remaining hidden. One perceived only layer upon layer of towers and pavilions, the graceful scattering of flowers and trees, fragrant grass spread smooth as a green carpet, clear springs plunging straight down like quicksilver, and water winding like trailing ribbons. The mountains were arrayed as host and guest; where the view was dense, it did not seem cluttered, and where it was sparse, it did not feel lacking. A natural painting—even the famed Wangchuan scroll was no more than this. A masterpiece of human endeavor—the Pingquan Villa was hardly worth mentioning in comparison. The company each leaned at the railing, gazing out in all directions, hearing only the melodious drifting of xiao flutes and drums from the dragon boat as it rowed out, rippling the clear waves.",`At the stern of the dragon boat hung a swing frame, with two children swinging up and down. Cixian said: "Please, let's go back down to look — looking from above down is not as fine as looking from below up." Everyone descended the goose-step stairway back to the first story, where they found a round table already set out in the front corridor of the central bays. This gathering was four host-and-guest gentlemen and five beauties. Ziyun was about to change into formal dress, but Cixian, Tian, and the three others stopped him; so they sent round the wine in informal attire, seating by seniority. To the east sat Nanxiang; Ziyun asked Lanbao to sit at his shoulder. To the west sat Chunhang, with Huifang at his shoulder. At the head sat Cixian, with Shufang at his shoulder.`,"Ziyun took the host's seat, with Sulan and Baozhu on either side. The table talk was the usual sort; let us not linger over it. The dragon boat had now drawn up before the pavilion and was circling round and round, coming and going without end.",'Servants at a distance set off a string of five-thousand-cracker "all-red hundred-seeds" — the sound unceasing. Everyone was watching and cheering when suddenly four men appeared at the railing outside, wearing short green-lacquered silk jackets and red-lacquered silk trousers, bare-chested with sashes at the waist, red scarves bound at their foreheads, barefoot in straw sandals, their legs wrapped tightly in blue cloth. They lined up at the railing and performed a full bow toward those above. Nanxiang did not understand the meaning; he was about to ask when a drumbeat sounded from the dragon boat, and all four men simultaneously turned somersaults and plunged into the water. Ziyun said: "These foolish fellows — they want to show off some skill too." He ordered servants to catch several pairs of ducks, and also told them to bring several red-lacquered gourds to throw into the water; then everyone understood it was a competition to seize the prize. The servants obeyed, first throwing a white duck into the water; the duck, hitting the water, gave a flip of its head, turned a somersault itself, then stretched its neck, flapped its wings, and quacked qua-qua-qua several times.',`One of the men pressed himself flat on the water's surface, kicked both legs, and shot across like a shuttle. The duck, seeing someone coming to catch it, fanned its wings and, half-submerged and half-floating, sped away. Just as it was going, a head suddenly emerged from the water, and one hand seized the duck. Ziyun said: "Well done! Make a note to reward him." Three more ducks and two gourds were thrown in together. The four men competed with each other to excel, displaying every skill: now face-down, now face-up, now sinking, now surfacing, now turning sideways, now lifting a leg — swimming and playing without end. Some caught their prizes, some could not; some caught and then lost hold; some caught halfway and had their prize seized by another — drawing laughter and delighted shouts from everyone at the table, and each person drank several cups. The young dan actors were even more excited and all came out from the table to lean at the railing and watch the performance.`,`Ziyun called them back inside and poured fresh cups. Cixian said: "Shall we use this as the basis of a drinking game today?" Everyone asked: "How would the game work?" Cixian asked the servants: "Those large gourds that were cultivated in the garden last year — I imagine you still have some." The servants replied: "There are a dozen or so lacquered ones; the rest are unlacquered." Cixian told them to bring the lacquered ones. Before long, the servants brought a large string of them; the ropes were undone and they were placed on an empty side table. Cixian then asked for the set of wine-forfeit tallies; the servants brought up a set of ivory tallies. Cixian drew out several at random, placed them face-down on the table with the blank side up, and said to everyone: "Each person take one tally at random — don't look at the written side — and inscribe your own name on it." Everyone did as he said.`,`Cixian then lifted the lid of a gourd, placed one tally inside each, screwed the lid back on tight, and instructed the pages to throw them into the water. "Whoever's gourd is retrieved — that is the person who must drink. This is a perfectly fair amusement." Everyone agreed: "Excellent — but what does it say on the tallies?" Cixian said: "This set of tallies takes its figures from the Water Margin — each character has a drinking episode associated with them. I drew them at random; I don't know exactly which names have come out." Ziyun laughed: "These tallies would also be good fun — the drinking would be hearty. Only, among them there are a few characters with large capacity; drawing one of those would really put one to the test." Everyone said: "There is nothing to do but submit to fate." One gourd was seen retrieved from the water; a page brought it to the table, dried it with a towel, opened the lid, and poured out the tally — it was Xiao Cixian's. Everyone looked at the other side, which had seven large characters and two lines of small text as annotation. The large characters read: "Li Kui Creates a Great Disturbance at Xunyang River." The annotation read: "The first two seats are Song Jiang and Dai Zong; the last seat is Zhang Shun. Li Kui drinks one large cup by himself; Song and Dai accompany with one small cup; then Li Kui finger-guesses ten rounds with Zhang Shun. If Li Kui wins, Zhang Shun drinks; if Zhang Shun wins, Li Kui drinks plain water." Everyone laughed. Cixian first drained the opening cup; Nanxiang and Chunhang accompanied with one cup each. Then he finger-guessed with Ziyun; Ziyun drank six cups of wine, Cixian drank four cups of plain water. Everyone said: "Quite entertaining." Then another was retrieved; the tally was Nanxiang's. The inscription read: "Wu Song Drunkenly Seizes the Happy Forest." The annotation read: "Without three cups you cannot cross the ridge — first drain three full cups. The person opposite is Jiang Menshen; win three rounds in a row to pass, then play a general round." Nanxiang said: "This one is too much — I'll drink the three cups, but let the general round be waived." Ziyun said: "Waiving is not allowed; and moreover you have a great capacity." Lanbao said: "For the general round, perhaps use half a cup, or split one cup into three portions." Everyone agreed. Nanxiang drank three cups, then began finger-guessing with Chunhang, and actually won three rounds in a row; then played out the general round; twelve cups of wine in all.`,'Then two more were retrieved from the water. Opening the first: it was the character for Xu Ziyun. The inscription read: "Song Jiang Kills Yan Poxi in a Rage." The annotation: "Drink two cups; the person seated beside is Yan Poxi. Song Jiang first drinks one cup himself, then urges one cup on Yan Poxi; if Yan Poxi refuses, Song Jiang drinks it himself."',`Ziyun laughed: "Who is Yan Poxi among us?" Everyone laughed. Cixian said: "No need to guess — it's the two sitting right beside you; and moreover, you still drink it yourself. There's no need for them, but you still have to invite them." Ziyun laughed and drank one cup, then offered a cup to Sulan: "Xiangwan — you are a good person; you mustn't imitate that Yan Poxi, who only had Zhang Sanlang in his heart and paid attention to nothing else. Just drink this cup." This set everyone laughing. Sulan was originally going to drink, but the laughter made a flush rise on his face, and he pursed his lips toward Baozhu and said: "Yan Poxi is over there — ask him to drink." Baozhu also gave a titter. Ziyun then took a cup and said to Baozhu: "What do you say — will you drink?" Baozhu took the cup, turned to Sulan, and said: "You fell into the trap — look at the tally: Yan Poxi is the one who doesn't drink; if you drank, you wouldn't be Yan Poxi." And he drained the cup. Sulan thought about it and realized that Baozhu had outwitted him. Now the second tally was looked at — it was Huifang's. When everyone saw it they burst out laughing; only Tian Chunhang managed to suppress his laughter, though his face showed some color. As it happened, this tally fell to Huifang — which was rather a case of karmic retribution for the Pan Qiguan business. The inscription read: "Pan Jinlian Flirts with Her Brother-in-Law on a Snowy Day." The annotation: "Three cups; the person seated to the left is Wu Song. For the first cup, one must reveal the chest, place one hand on Wu Song's shoulder, and say: 'Brother-in-law, drink this cup.' For the second cup, one must first drink half oneself, then say: 'Brother-in-law, if you have feeling for me, drink the other half.' For the third cup, one must stand up, feign an expression of anger, and drink it oneself; the whole table accompanies with three cups." At this Huifang flatly refused, saying: "Let's finger-guess away these three cups." Ziyun said: "The tally makes it perfectly clear — it was retrieved from the water; how can it be changed?" Cixian said: "And moreover you yourself wrote it on the tally — how can you go back on it now?" Nanxiang said: "If you're going to change the game, why did we follow ours as prescribed?" Huifang had no choice and hesitated for a long time. Lanbao laughed: "Retribution comes swiftly — now you're truly going to play Pan Jinlian's role for real." The company did not entirely understand, assuming Lanbao meant only the Pan Jinlian on the tally — not knowing that Lanbao had heard about the Pan Qiguan business. Chunhang understood perfectly and only lowered his head without speaking. Huifang, hearing this, blushed all the more and paid no attention; he had no choice but to take a cup of wine, stand up, lean toward Baozhu, and say: "Brother-in-law, do drink this cup." Baozhu was in the middle of eating some vegetable when Huifang's unexpected address caught him off guard, and he spat his mouthful all over the table with laughter, leaned against Ziyun, wiped his mouth with a handkerchief, and still could not stop laughing. Everyone broke into uproarious laughter. Huifang was at a complete loss; he put down the cup and started laughing himself. Cixian said: "Meixiang — another mistake. The annotation says the person seated to the left is Wu Song — not the person on the right. How is it you've offered this cup to Yaoqing?" Huifang said: "After all, who do you want me to offer it to? Isn't he seated beside me?" Baozhu said: "I don't quite count as beside you. Though it's a round table, I'm facing north and you're facing east — I certainly didn't expect you to address me as 'brother-in-law.'" And he laughed again. Huifang still refused to comply. Ziyun laughed: "Meixiang — can it be that you've never offered Xiangfan a cup of wine? Quickly, quickly! Look — two more have been retrieved. If you break the game rules, what will happen after this? In any case it's a one-time thing — it won't be your turn two or three times in a row." Cixian said: "Be quick and offer the wine!" Nanxiang said: "When Pan Jinlian was flirting with her brother-in-law in the old story, she had to show some seductive manner to make it convincing — none of this stiff, by-the-book stuff." Each one saying a word, everyone pressing together — Huifang truly had no recourse. The sharp-tongued too can sometimes come to grief. Huifang had no choice but to lean slightly toward Chunhang, raise the cup, and say: "Brother-in-law — drink this cup." Chunhang also had no recourse; he could only brazen it out and drink. For the second cup, Huifang also had to first take a sip, then bring it to Chunhang's lips; Chunhang, without waiting to be asked, drank it. Everyone said: "That cup doesn't count — start over. The tally says the correct words must be spoken for it to count." Chunhang pleaded again and again; finally it was let go. For the third cup, things were much easier. Huifang poured a cup for himself, stood up, and Cixian said: "This cup must be done with an expression of anger." Sulan said: "He has genuine grievance in his heart." Huifang gave a laugh, then quickly composed the beautiful face into an expression of anger and drained the cup in one go. Ziyun, seeing all this, felt great admiration in his heart; he drank three cups himself and urged everyone else to drink three cups as well.`,`They then looked at the next tally — it was Lanbao's. The inscription read: "Lu Zhishen Drunken at the Mountain Gate." The annotation: "First drain one large cup; the first two seats are the Guardian Kings; finger-guess three rounds with each." Huifang said: "He gets off so easily, while I had to go through all that trouble." Lanbao followed the game rules and finger-guessed three rounds each with Nanxiang and Chunhang.`,`The next tally was Shufang's; the inscription read: "Jin Cuilian Selling Her Singing in the Wine-House. She must play the pipa and offer a respectful cup to Lu Da, Li Zhong, and Shi Jin each." Everyone said: "This one is still manageable — on the edge between closeness and distance. And since she really does have the surname Jin — what an extraordinary coincidence!" Shufang had no choice but to take up the pipa, and offered cups to Nanxiang, Chunhang, and Cixian.`,`The next gourd retrieved contained Tian Chunhang's tally. Chunhang quickly looked at the inscription, thought for a moment, and said: "Terrible!" Everyone burst out clapping and laughing again: "Today Meixiang and Xiangfan are clearly bound together by fate." Huifang's face reddened and she said: "You've arranged this on purpose — otherwise why would only these two tallies come out like this?" Cixian said: "An absolutely unjust accusation! Say I purposely chose them — did you then purposely choose to draw them?" The inscription read: "Yi Zhangqing Captures Wang Duanhu." The annotation: "After, they became husband and wife; hold a red handkerchief between yourself and the person beside you, exchange three cups in bridal-goblet fashion; the whole table raises a congratulatory cup." Chunhang wanted to change the game rule, but everyone refused to allow it. He could only hold a handkerchief between himself and Huifang, and they each drank half a cup; the third cup set the whole table talking and laughing together — talking and laughing, laughing and talking — saying: "To witness this bridal goblet is rare indeed — let us each raise a great cup." So the whole table drank a congratulatory cup, which so embarrassed Huifang that she said: "From now on my unlucky stars have all passed. Now it will be my turn to laugh at people." The next tally was Baozhu's; the inscription read: "Wang Po Discusses Romantic Intrigue Upstairs." Seeing the annotation, Huifang laughed: "Now this is karmic retribution — who expected that someone else would also put on a fine show for people to watch." Baozhu's face had already flushed bright red. The game required three cups: for the first, the person to the right is Ximen Qing; adopt the manner of lifting the window curtain with a bamboo pole, strike Ximen Qing once with a fan, and offer the first cup. For the second, Ximen Qing must kneel, one hand clasping the tip of Pan Jinlian's tiny foot, and offer this cup to Pan Jinlian. For the third, the person to the left is Wang Po; Pan Jinlian curtsies and says: "Godmother, please drink this cup." Ziyun laughed: "As luck would have it, it has now come around to me." Chunhang said: "Having fragrant dust cling to one's stockings is the finest of affairs — and moreover with a lotus hook in the hand; why not drink ten cups?" Nanxiang laughed loudly: "'Fragrant dust cling to the knee' is still manageable — only don't kneel in the mud; then the lotus hook would be out of reach, and what you'd grasp would be a donkey's leg." This set everyone into wild uncontrollable laughter; Jin Shufang laughed so hard she twisted at the waist and tumbled straight into Cixian's lap. Wang Lanbao and Lu Sulan laughed and moved away. Baozhu said: "Another case of heavenly retribution — perfectly clear and precise." Everyone kept laughing, which made Chunhang quite uncomfortable; yet unable to take it seriously, he had to hold it in and say: "Zhujun's mockery deserves to be punished — he should draw an unpleasant tally." Nanxiang laughed: "I was speaking from the facts — what mockery is there?" Huifang said: "That is enough — don't be so boastful. You too will have your moment of losing the wind someday." Cixian laughed: "Yaoqing — what to do about this tally? It seems it cannot be changed — one can only accept some discomfort. And it is asking a lot of Duxiang's golden dignity on his knees." Everyone burst into laughter again. Huifang urged Baozhu to offer the wine quickly. Baozhu, being of a gentle and compliant nature, and unable to withstand the general pressure, had no choice but to brazen it out, and with the handle of the fan gave Ziyun a light tap, offering the first cup. Ziyun received it with a smile; everyone said: "Splendid! Let us each drink a cup." Ziyun said: "The drinking-game law is stricter than military law — no choice in the matter; honored guests, please don't laugh at the short man trying to see the show." He poured a cup, bent one knee, and offered it to Baozhu; Baozhu promptly took it and drank. Everyone said: "Splendid!" and each drank another cup. Baozhu then offered the third cup to Huifang, curtsied, and said: "Godmother, please drink this cup." Huifang accepted it and drank, laughing: "Good daughter — you've gone to such trouble." Everyone praised: "What a fine godmother and god-daughter! Let us drink another congratulatory cup." And each drank again. The last remaining tally turned out to be Sulan's; it read: "The Heroes of Liangshan Marsh Assemble in Righteous Unity." The whole table each drinks three cups. Everyone said: "That closes things out most entertainingly — this drinking game today really seemed as if it had been arranged on purpose." Baozhu said: "Only, it's too easy on him, and too easy on Jingfang — and Shouxiang only had to play the pipa. First of all, it's Yaoqing and I who had the worst of it."`,`Huifang said: "Someone knelt to offer you wine — and you're still not satisfied? What more do you want?" Baozhu said: "If you wanted someone to kneel to you, why didn't you take my tally and perform it for me?" This round of drinking lasted until the red sun was setting in the west, and then they had their meal.`,`After rinsing their mouths and washing their hands, they drank another round of fragrant tea. Nanxiang said: "We still haven't appreciated the pomegranate blossoms at Little Red Citadel — shall we take advantage of the evening glow to go look at those blazing pomegranate fires?" Ziyun then led everyone back to the boats, which crossed the red bridge back to the west side, where they disembarked at the foot of the artificial hill, passed through the Immortal's Cave, wended through the Apricot Tower (Xinglou) and Peach Grove, and arrived at Little Red Citadel. They saw pomegranate blossoms ringing in all directions like a rampart — some hundred or two hundred trees — crimson gleams flickering, a flowing fire about to blaze; interspersed were several varieties in yellow, white, and variegated agate colors, all blooming in succession. Truly as the "Rhapsody on Mount Tiantai" says: "The red citadel glows and rises to mark the summit" — which is why the place was called Little Red Citadel.`,'The day was now late. Nanxiang and Chunhang wished to leave; small attendants brought their outer garments and hats, they dressed and put them on, thanked the host and Cixian, and took the roundabout path out of the garden. Ziyun said: "Today there was actually one matter I had meant to trouble you two gentlemen with. The paired inscriptions for various spots throughout the garden still need several more sets added; today we were sidetracked by the dragon boat. Perhaps another day I can invite another outing and also include Gengxiang, Jiantan, and the other gentlemen — what do you say?" Shi and Tian both agreed, then mounted their carriages and departed. The five young dan actors also gradually dispersed. This second grand entertainment at Yiyuan — it was a pity it was not fully attended. As for what the second volume will relate — one must await a careful reading.']},b=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations20:d},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"}));export{b as _,p as a,w as b,y as c,m as d,f as e,c as f,g,u as h,l as i};
